window.searchIndex = [{"page": "Introduction", "title": "Cella Documentation", "url": "index.html", "text": "Cella is a systems programming language. It works at the level of C: raw pointers, no garbage collector and no runtime. On top of that it adds ownership and destructors, borrowed parameters, enums with payloads, generics, traits and modules. The compiler generates code with LLVM and builds executables and libraries for 64-bit Windows and Linux. This documentation covers the language itself: its syntax, its types and the rules the compiler checks. The standard library is documented separately."}, {"page": "Introduction", "title": "How the documentation is organized", "url": "index.html#how-the-documentation-is-organized", "text": "The chapters build on each other, so reading them in order works best. Getting started installs Cella and builds a first program. Basics covers the pieces every program uses: literals, numbers, strings, variables, functions and control flow. Data covers arrays, records, enums, unions and pattern matching. Memory explains ownership, destructors, parameter modes, borrows and raw pointers. Abstraction covers generics, traits, operators, conversions and closures. Programs covers modules, conditional compilation, calling C, checks and atomics. Reference lists keywords, operators, built-in types and project settings."}, {"page": "Introduction", "title": "Examples", "url": "index.html#examples", "text": "Every example in this documentation is compiled and run with the current compiler, and the output shown under it is what the program printed. Examples that the compiler rejects show its errors under the lines they point to. The examples print through C's printf, declared in an ext block as above. Most examples leave that declaration out to stay short; the copy button on each example copies the whole program, declaration included. Cella is still changing and hasn't had a release yet. This documentation follows the compiler as it is today."}, {"page": "Hello, World", "title": "Hello, World", "url": "hello-world.html", "text": ""}, {"page": "Hello, World", "title": "Installing Cella", "url": "hello-world.html#installing-cella", "text": "Cella installs a small launcher named cella, which manages compiler versions and runs the right one for each project. On Windows, run this in PowerShell: On Linux: The installer puts everything in C:\\cella or ~/.cella, or in CELLA_HOME if it's set, and adds cella to your path. Windows programs link with Visual Studio's C++ build tools and the Windows SDK when they're installed, and with MinGW-w64 otherwise. Linux programs need glibc 2.34 or later."}, {"page": "Hello, World", "title": "A first program", "url": "hello-world.html#a-first-program", "text": "cella new creates a project: This makes a hello folder with two files. hello.celp describes the project, and main.ce holds the program: cella run builds the program and runs it:"}, {"page": "Hello, World", "title": "What the program says", "url": "hello-world.html#what-the-program-says", "text": "mod hello names the module this file belongs to. Every source file starts with a mod line, and any number of files can belong to one module. The ext block declares puts, a function from the C library. Cella has no built-in way to print; programs call C functions, or a library that wraps them. cstr is a pointer to a zero-terminated string, which is what C expects, and a string literal converts to one. main: fun -> i32 declares the function the program starts in. Every declaration has this shape: a name, a colon, then what the name is. fun makes it a function, and -> i32 says it returns a 32-bit integer, which becomes the program's exit code. The body is a block of statements, one per line. Calls look as they do in C, and ret returns a value. No semicolons are needed: a new line ends a statement. main doesn't have to return anything. Without a return type, the program exits with code 0:"}, {"page": "Hello, World", "title": "Printing values", "url": "hello-world.html#printing-values", "text": "printf comes from C too. Its declaration ends in ..., so it takes any number of extra arguments after the format string, and they follow C's rules for variadic calls (Variadic functions covers them). val declares a named value, covered in Variables and constants: The rest of the documentation prints with printf. Examples leave out its ext block to stay short, but the copy button copies it along with the rest of the program."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Projects and building", "url": "projects.html", "text": "A Cella program is built from a project: a folder with a project file and the source files that belong to it. This chapter covers creating projects, what goes in the project file, building and running with cella build and cella run, projects that use other projects, and the launcher that manages Cella versions."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Creating a project", "url": "projects.html#creating-a-project", "text": "cella new creates a project in a new folder: The folder holds two files. game.celp is the project file: main.ce is the greeting program from Hello, World, starting with mod game. The argument can be a path, such as cella new tools/packer; the last part of the path is the project's name. Without an argument, cella new turns the current folder into a project named after the folder. The name becomes the project file's name and the name of the module in main.ce, so it must be a valid identifier (see Lexical structure): cella new never overwrites anything. A folder that already exists and isn't empty is an error, and so is a folder that already has a project file:"}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Libraries", "url": "projects.html#libraries", "text": "--lib creates a static library instead of a program: Its project file has OutputType = \"StaticLibrary\" along with the CellaVersion line, and in place of main.ce it has a source file named after the project, with one exported function: A library has no main. Other projects use it through a project reference."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "A .gitignore file", "url": "projects.html#a-gitignore-file", "text": "--gitignore also writes a .gitignore file that leaves the build folders, bin/ and obj/, out of Git:"}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "What a project is", "url": "projects.html#what-a-project-is", "text": "A project is a .celp file and the folder it's in. Every .ce file in that folder belongs to the project, and so does every .ce file in its subfolders, except in a subfolder that has a .celp file of its own: that subfolder is a separate project. Every source file starts with a mod line that names its module. Files don't have to be listed anywhere; adding a .ce file to the folder adds it to the build."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "The project file", "url": "projects.html#the-project-file", "text": "The project file is written in TOML. Each line sets one setting: OutputType is the only setting every project needs. It says what the build produces: | \"Executable\" | a program that runs in a terminal | | \"WindowedExecutable\" | a program that opens no terminal window on Windows | | \"StaticLibrary\" | a library that is copied into the programs that use it | | \"SharedLibrary\" | a library loaded when a program starts (.dll on Windows, .so on Linux) | CellaVersion pins the project to a Cella version. cella new writes the version that created the project. The launcher reads the pin to pick which installed compiler builds the project. A project pinned to a newer version than the compiler building it is an error, and one pinned to an older version gets a warning and builds: A misspelled setting or a missing OutputType is an error. Problems in a project file are reported with the file's full path, shortened here: Project settings lists every setting."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Building", "url": "projects.html#building", "text": "cella build builds the project in the current folder: The last line names each project built and the file it produced. cella build also takes a project file or a folder: cella build game.celp, or cella build projects, which builds every project in projects and its subfolders. The build writes into two folders beside the project file: bin holds what the build produces: the program or library, and on Windows its debug information. obj holds the intermediate files: object files for each module, and the parts of the standard library the project uses. The program is named after the project file: game.celp builds game.exe on Windows and game on Linux. Both folders can be deleted at any time; the next build makes them again. Errors and warnings print with the line they point to. A build with errors produces nothing and exits with code 1."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Build options", "url": "projects.html#build-options", "text": "cella build takes these options, in any order before or after the path: | --release | builds with optimizations and without overflow checks | | --target <target> | builds for another system | | --flag <name> | sets a flag to true; --flag <name>=false sets it to false | | --entry <module.function> | builds a program that starts in another function | | --hints | also prints hints | | --verbose | prints details of each step of the build, including the linker's command lines | | --emit-ir | also writes each module's LLVM IR into obj, as .ll files | | --emit-asm | also writes each module's assembly into obj, as .s files |"}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Release builds", "url": "projects.html#release-builds", "text": "Without --release, a build is a debug build: no optimizations, and arithmetic that overflows stops the program with a message. --release turns on optimizations and leaves out the overflow checks. Bounds checks on array indexing stay on in both. Both kinds of build write to the same bin folder. Checks and panics covers the checks and the settings that change them."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Targets", "url": "projects.html#targets", "text": "--target builds for a system other than the one running the compiler: | windows-x64 | 64-bit Windows, with Visual Studio's C++ build tools when they're installed and MinGW-w64 otherwise | | windows-x64-msvc | 64-bit Windows, with Visual Studio's C++ build tools | | windows-x64-gnu | 64-bit Windows, with MinGW-w64 | | linux-x64 | 64-bit Linux | Without --target, the build is for the system it runs on."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Flags", "url": "projects.html#flags", "text": "Flags are names that are true or false at build time. Code can test them in when blocks to leave parts of a program out of some builds. --flag sets one for a build, overriding the project's own setting for it: Conditional compilation covers flags and when."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Entry points", "url": "projects.html#entry-points", "text": "--entry builds a program that starts in a function other than main. The function is named with its module, and it must be pub (see Modules and visibility). This makes it possible to keep small extra programs, such as a demo or a benchmark, in the same project: A program built with --entry goes into obj/entry/<function> instead of bin, so it never replaces the project's own program. Functions covers what an entry function may look like."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Hints", "url": "projects.html#hints", "text": "Hints point at code that works but could be written better, such as a var whose value never changes. They aren't shown unless the build is given --hints: The [[Lints] setting](project-settings.md#lints) can turn such checks off or report them as warnings or errors instead."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Running", "url": "projects.html#running", "text": "cella run builds a program and runs it. It takes the same path and options as cella build: The program runs in the current folder and uses the same terminal. Its output appears as it runs, without the game -> bin\\game.exe line, and cella run exits with the program's exit code. A build with errors doesn't start the program. Everything after -- goes to the program as its command-line arguments: The program reads them through the parameters of main, as Functions shows. cella run runs only programs built for the system it's on, so --target can only name that system: A library can't be run, and a folder must hold exactly one program:"}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Several files", "url": "projects.html#several-files", "text": "A project can have any number of source files. Files that declare the same module share their declarations, so a function in one file can be called from another without anything else: The order of the files doesn't matter, and neither does the order of declarations in them. Files can also declare other modules; Modules and visibility covers modules, imports and qualified names. A C function declared in an ext block belongs to its module like any other function, so a module declares it once, in one of its files. Declaring the same function in two files of one module is an error."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "Using a library project", "url": "projects.html#using-a-library-project", "text": "A program uses a library project by referencing it in a [[ProjectReferences]] table. Path is the library's project file, relative to the program's: The library lives in its own folder with its own project file: The program calls the library's functions through the library's module: Building the program builds the library first, so cella build and cella run in the program's folder are enough. Each project writes its own bin and obj folders. A static library is copied into the program; a shared library is a separate file the program loads when it starts. Only pub declarations can be used from other projects. Declarations without a visibility word belong to their own project. Modules and visibility covers pub, imports with use, and the other visibility levels. To reference several projects, repeat the table. Alias puts every module of a reference under an extra name, which keeps two libraries apart when they have modules with the same name: With these references, shared.one() calls the shared module from a, and second.shared.one() the one from b."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "The standard library", "url": "projects.html#the-standard-library", "text": "Every project uses the standard library unless its project file says otherwise. The StandardLibrary setting picks how much of it: | \"Full\" | all of the standard library (the default) | | \"Core\" | only the parts that don't depend on the operating system | | \"None\" | none of it | The standard library is built with the project, into the project's obj folder. It is documented separately; this documentation covers only the language."}, {"page": "Projects and building", "title": "The launcher", "url": "projects.html#the-launcher", "text": "The cella command is a small launcher. It keeps track of the installed Cella versions, and for every command that builds or creates a project it runs the right compiler: For a project with a CellaVersion, it runs that version. When a folder holds several projects, it runs the highest version any of them pins. For a project without a pin, and for cella new, it runs the newest installed version. A pinned version that isn't installed is an error that names the command to install it: The launcher has its own commands: | cella install | installs the latest release | | cella install <version> | installs that version | | cella uninstall <version> | removes that version | | cella list | lists the installed versions and the ones available to install | | cella list --installed | lists only the installed versions | | cella self update | updates the launcher | | cella self uninstall | removes Cella completely | | cella lsp | starts the language server for the current folder, for editors | | cella --version | prints the compiler's version | cella install, cella list (without --installed) and cella self update are the only commands that use the network. Builds never download anything. To run one command with another installed version, put + and the version before it. This ignores the project's pin for that command only:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Lexical structure", "url": "lexical-structure.html", "text": "This chapter covers how Cella source text is put together: source files, comments, names, keywords, where lines may break, and how lists of members are separated."}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Source files", "url": "lexical-structure.html#source-files", "text": "Cella source files have the .ce extension and hold UTF-8 text. Lines may end in \\n, \\r\\n or \\r. Spaces and tabs separate tokens and mean nothing else: indentation is never significant. Every source file starts with a mod line naming the module the file belongs to: Only comments and blank lines may come before it. A file without a mod line, a file with two of them, and a file with anything else before it are errors: The other two cases report: A module name can have several parts separated by dots, such as mod game.physics. Modules and visibility covers modules, and how files share them. Characters that change how text is displayed without being visible themselves, such as the Unicode controls that reverse the direction of text, are errors anywhere in a file, even in comments and strings:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Comments", "url": "lexical-structure.html#comments", "text": "// starts a comment that runs to the end of the line. /* starts a block comment that runs to the next */, across lines if needed. Block comments nest, so a block comment can comment out code that has block comments of its own: A block comment that is never closed is an error, reported at its /*:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Names", "url": "lexical-structure.html#names", "text": "A name, also called an identifier, starts with a letter or _ and continues with letters, digits and _. Letters include those of every script, not only ASCII, and digits are only allowed after the first character. Names are case-sensitive: count and Count are different names. The compiler accepts any of these names, but by default it gives a hint for names that don't follow the usual style, such as a variable named Count or _offset. Project settings covers the rules and how to change them. Names are compared after Unicode normalization (NFC), so a letter written as one character and the same letter written as a base letter and a combining mark are the same name. _ on its own isn't a name. It marks a value or parameter that is never used, such as val _ = make() or a parameter written _: i32, and it can't be read: Names are usually written in camelCase for values and functions and PascalCase for types. The compiler doesn't enforce either."}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Keywords", "url": "lexical-structure.html#keywords", "text": "Keywords are words with a meaning of their own. Reserved keywords can never be names: Contextual keywords mean something only in certain places, such as the start of a declaration or after a declaration's colon. Everywhere else they are ordinary names, such as the fields of this record (Records covers records): Keywords lists every keyword with what it does."}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Lines and statements", "url": "lexical-structure.html#lines-and-statements", "text": "A new line ends a statement. There are no semicolons. Most statements fit on one line:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Wrapping long lines", "url": "lexical-structure.html#wrapping-long-lines", "text": "A statement continues on the next line when its line ends in a way that needs more: after a binary operator, an assignment operator, is or =>. Wrap a long expression after an operator. volume below has an expression body, => followed by its result, which Functions covers: A line can't start with a binary operator, an assignment operator, is or =>: +, - and * are different, because they can also start an expression: + 2, - 2 and * p are complete expressions on their own. A line that starts with one of them starts a new statement, and a value on its own isn't a statement: The same goes for declarations. The = before a value and the => before an expression body must stay on the line before:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Inside parentheses and brackets", "url": "lexical-structure.html#inside-parentheses-and-brackets", "text": "Inside ( ) and [ ], new lines don't end anything, so arguments, array elements and parenthesized expressions can be split anywhere: The ( of a call and the [ of an index must still be on the same line as what they apply to. On the next line, they start a new statement:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Member access", "url": "lexical-structure.html#member-access", "text": ". may start a line, so a chain of member accesses can be written one per line:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Prefix operators", "url": "lexical-structure.html#prefix-operators", "text": "A prefix operator, such as -, !, ~, @ or *, must have its operand on the same line:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Separating members", "url": "lexical-structure.html#separating-members", "text": "Lists of members in braces, such as the fields of a record, the cases of an enum and the arms of a match, follow one rule: neighbouring items are separated either by a , on the same line or by a new line. One item per line needs no commas, and several items on one line need them. Records, Enums and Pattern matching cover the forms in this example: A , at the end of a line, a , after the last item, and two items on one line without a , are errors: Lists in parentheses and brackets, such as arguments, parameters and array elements, always use commas, and they can't end with one either. Top-level declarations are separated by new lines only:"}, {"page": "Lexical structure", "title": "Literals", "url": "lexical-structure.html#literals", "text": "Literals write values directly in the source. Each kind has its own chapter: | Integers | 42, 1_000_000, 0xFF, 0b1010, 0o17, 7u8 | Numbers and arithmetic | | Floats | 3.14, 1.5e3, 2.0f32 | Numbers and arithmetic | | Booleans | true, false | Bool, char and strings | | Characters | 'a', '\\n' | Bool, char and strings | | Strings | \"hello\", \"tab\\there\" | Bool, char and strings | | Interpolated strings | \"{count} items\" | Bool, char and strings, String templates | | Arrays | [1, 2, 3] | Arrays | | Null pointers | null | Pointers and raw memory | | Uninitialized storage | undef | Variables and constants | A character that can't start any token is an error:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Numbers and arithmetic", "url": "numbers.html", "text": "Cella has signed and unsigned integers from 8 to 128 bits, two pointer-sized integers and two floating-point types. This chapter covers their literals, how a literal gets its type, the operators, and what happens when a result doesn't fit."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Integer types", "url": "numbers.html#integer-types", "text": "Each integer type is named for its signedness and its width in bits: | i8 | u8 | 1 | | i16 | u16 | 2 | | i32 | u32 | 4 | | i64 | u64 | 8 | | i128 | u128 | 16 | | isize | usize | pointer size, 8 on 64-bit targets | Signed types use two's complement, so an i8 holds -128 to 127 and a u8 holds 0 to 255. usize is the type of sizes, lengths and array indexes, and isize is its signed counterpart, the type of the difference between two pointers. sizeOf gives the size of a type or a value, as a usize:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Floating-point types", "url": "numbers.html#floating-point-types", "text": "f32 and f64 are IEEE 754 single and double precision numbers, 4 and 8 bytes, like C's float and double. Arithmetic on them follows IEEE 754: dividing by zero gives an infinity, and a NaN compares unequal to everything, itself included. C's printf takes f32 values as double, so %f prints both float types."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Integer literals", "url": "numbers.html#integer-literals", "text": "An integer literal is written in decimal, or in hexadecimal, binary or octal with a prefix. Underscores among the digits are ignored, so they can group digits: | Decimal | 1_000_000 | 1000000 | | Hexadecimal, 0x or 0X | 0xFF, 0xDEAD_BEEF | 255, 3735928559 | | Binary, 0b or 0B | 0b1010_0101 | 165 | | Octal, 0o or 0O | 0o755 | 493 | Leading zeros don't make a literal octal: 010 is ten. A minus sign is an operator, so -0x10 is the negation of 0x10. A digit that doesn't belong to the literal's base, or a prefix with no digits after it, is an error:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Float literals", "url": "numbers.html#float-literals", "text": "A float literal has a decimal point with digits on both sides, an exponent, or both: 1.5, 1.5e3, 2.5E-3, 1e10. Underscores separate digits as in integers. 1. and .5 aren't literals: A float literal too large for f64 is an error, while one too small to represent becomes zero."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Type suffixes", "url": "numbers.html#type-suffixes", "text": "A suffix names a literal's type directly: 300u16, 0xFFu8, 1_000_000u64, 4usize, -128i8. Integer literals take any integer suffix, and both integer and float literals take f32 and f64, as in 0.5f32 or 2f32. The value must fit the type. A bare u suffix makes an unsigned literal without fixing its width. It takes whatever unsigned type it's used as, and on its own it's a u32, or a u64 or u128 when the value needs one. It can't become a signed or float type. These are errors:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "How literals get their type", "url": "numbers.html#how-literals-get-their-type", "text": "A literal without a suffix has no type of its own until something decides it. Where a type is expected, as in a typed declaration, an argument or a return value, the literal takes that type, and its value must fit: An integer literal that becomes a float type must be exactly representable in it; 16777217 needs 25 bits of precision and f32 has 24. Float literals with a fraction round to the nearest value, as in C, so 0.1 is fine as an f32. Where nothing decides, an integer literal is an i32, or an i64 or i128 if its value needs one, and a float literal is an f64: A local declared from a literal without a type, like val small = 5, also takes its type from how the function uses it; Variables explains how. A literal larger than every integer type is an error, \"Integer literal too large to fit any type\"."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Arithmetic", "url": "numbers.html#arithmetic", "text": "+, -, *, / and % work on integers and floats, and - and + also work as prefix operators. Integer division truncates toward zero, and the remainder has the sign of the left operand, as in C. % on floats gives the remainder of a truncating division too. Operators bind as in C: prefix operators first, then *, / and %, then + and -, then shifts. One difference is that &, ^ and | bind tighter than comparisons, so x & 1 == 0 means (x & 1) == 0. The operator reference lists every level. - can't be applied to an unsigned value:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "How arithmetic is typed", "url": "numbers.html#how-arithmetic-is-typed", "text": "The operands of an operator decide which operation runs. The type the result is going to doesn't change it; it only receives the result. With two i32s, a * b multiplies in i32 even when it's stored in an i64, and a / b divides as integers even when it's stored in an f64. To compute in a wider type, convert an operand first: When the operands have the same type, the operation happens in that type. When one operand's type converts to the other's without losing anything, the operation happens in the wider type, and otherwise in the narrowest type both convert to. An i32 and an i64 add as i64s, an i32 and an f64 as f64s, and an f32 and an i32 as f64s, since an f32 can't hold every i32 exactly. Where no type holds both, the operation is an error:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Literals take the other operand's type", "url": "numbers.html#literals-take-the-other-operands-type", "text": "A literal next to a typed operand takes that operand's type when it fits, so small + 1 with a u8 stays a u8, and single / 2.0 with an f32 stays an f32. A literal that doesn't fit takes its usual type instead, and the rules above apply: small + 300 adds as i32s. A float literal next to an integer is an f64, so count / 2.0 divides in f64."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Expressions made only of literals", "url": "numbers.html#expressions-made-only-of-literals", "text": "An expression made only of integer literals is computed as if each literal had the destination's type, or i32 where there is none. So 1 << 31 stored in an i32 sets the sign bit, ~0 stored in a u32 is all ones, and 3 / 5 stored in an f64 is 0, since 3 and 5 are integers. When a literal doesn't fit that type, or a step overflows it, the expression is computed exactly instead, and the result takes the smallest of i32, i64 and i128 that holds it. So 300 - 100 fits in a u8, and 2147483647 + 1 without a destination is an i64. Float literals combine in f64. An exact result still has to fit the destination:"}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Mixed signs", "url": "numbers.html#mixed-signs", "text": "An operation on a signed and an unsigned integer happens in the smallest signed type that holds both, so u8 + i8 is an i16, u16 + i8 an i32 and u32 + i32 an i64. u128 with a signed operand has nothing wider and is an error, and so are usize and isize with an operand of the other signedness unless every value fits on every target."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Overflow", "url": "numbers.html#overflow", "text": "Integer +, -, * and prefix -, and their compound assignments, check for overflow in debug builds. A result that doesn't fit the type stops the program with a panic that names the operation and where it happened: The messages are \"addition overflow\", \"subtraction overflow\", \"multiplication overflow\" and \"negation overflow\". Release builds don't check, and the result wraps around: 255 + 1 in a u8 is 0. The OverflowChecks project setting turns the checks on or off in every build: When every operand is a constant, the compiler computes the result and reports overflow as an error, in every build: Checks and panics covers panics and the project settings that control them."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Wrapping operators", "url": "numbers.html#wrapping-operators", "text": "+%, -% and *% add, subtract and multiply with wraparound in every build, for code like hashes that means to wrap. Their compound forms are +%=, -%= and *%=. They take the same operands as +, - and *, but only integers."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Division by zero", "url": "numbers.html#division-by-zero", "text": "Integer division and remainder by zero stop the program with \"division by zero\", and dividing the smallest signed value by -1, whose result doesn't fit, stops it with \"division overflow\". Unlike the overflow checks, these happen in every build. The remainder of the smallest value and -1 is 0. A constant zero divisor is a compile error. Float division by zero isn't checked and gives an infinity or a NaN."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Bitwise operators", "url": "numbers.html#bitwise-operators", "text": "&, | and ^ are bitwise and, or and exclusive or, and prefix ~ flips every bit. They work on integers of any width and follow the same typing rules as arithmetic. Floats have no bitwise operators."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Shifts", "url": "numbers.html#shifts", "text": "<< shifts left and >> shifts right. A right shift of a signed value copies the sign bit in, and a right shift of an unsigned value shifts zeros in. The result has the left operand's type, and bits shifted past the top are dropped without an overflow check. The shift amount can be any integer type. A constant shift amount must be from 0 to one less than the width of the type. An amount known only at run time that's the width or more, or negative, shifts every bit out: the result is 0, or -1 for a right shift of a negative value."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Rotations", "url": "numbers.html#rotations", "text": "<<< and >>> rotate: bits shifted out of one end come back in at the other. The amount wraps around the width, so rotating a u8 by 9 is the same as rotating it by 1, and a negative amount rotates the other way."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Comparisons", "url": "numbers.html#comparisons", "text": "<, <=, >, >=, == and != compare two numbers and give a bool. Any two integer types can be compared, even pairs with no common type for arithmetic such as i128 and u128, and the comparison is by value, so a negative signed value is less than any unsigned one. Integers and floats compare where they could be added. Comparisons chain: a < b < c means a < b && b < c, with b evaluated once. Any of <, <=, > and >= can be mixed in a chain, and == and != chain the same way, so a == b == c means a == b && b == c. As with &&, a chain stops at the first comparison that fails, and the operands after it aren't evaluated."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Compound assignment", "url": "numbers.html#compound-assignment", "text": "Every binary arithmetic, bitwise, shift and rotate operator has a compound assignment form: +=, -=, *=, /=, %=, +%=, -%=, *%=, &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=, <<<= and >>>=. x += y works like x = x + y with x evaluated once, and it's checked for overflow and division by zero like the operator. The right side must convert to the left side's type without loss, except for shifts and rotations, whose amount can be any integer type."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Implicit numeric conversions", "url": "numbers.html#implicit-numeric-conversions", "text": "A number converts to another numeric type without being written out only when every value of its type fits the other type exactly: A signed integer converts to any wider signed integer, and an unsigned integer to any wider integer of either signedness. i8, i16, i32, u8 and u16 convert to isize, and u8, u16 and u32 to usize. isize converts to i64 and i128, and usize to u64, u128 and i128. f32 converts to f64. i8, i16, u8 and u16 convert to f32, and those and i32 and u32 to f64. Everything else is written out. Named constants follow the same rules as variables: a const of type i32 doesn't become a usize, even when its value fits. Conversions covers conversions in general."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Converting with T(x)", "url": "numbers.html#converting-with-tx", "text": "A conversion is written as a call to the type: i64(x), u8(x), f32(x). Every numeric type converts to every other one this way. Converting a float to an integer truncates toward zero. Converting an f64 to an f32 rounds to the nearest value, and a value too large for f32 becomes an infinity. A bool converts to any integer as 0 or 1; nothing converts to bool."}, {"page": "Numbers and arithmetic", "title": "Checked conversions", "url": "numbers.html#checked-conversions", "text": "A conversion that can lose information is checked like arithmetic. A constant that doesn't fit is an error: A value that doesn't fit at run time stops the program with \"conversion overflow\" in builds with overflow checks: Without the checks, integer conversions keep the low bits, so 300 converted to a u8 is 44 and -1 converted to a u32 is 4294967295, and float-to-integer conversions saturate at the type's limits, with NaN becoming 0. To keep only the low bits on purpose, mask the value first, so it always fits:"}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Bool, char and strings", "url": "text.html", "text": "This chapter covers bool, char for single characters, and the two string types: str, a pointer and a length, and cstr, a C string. It also covers string literals, their escape sequences and suffixes, interpolated strings with constant holes, and nameOf."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "bool", "url": "text.html#bool", "text": "A bool is true or false and takes one byte. ! negates it, and && and || are logical and and or. They stop as soon as the result is known, so the right side runs only when it's needed. &, | and ^ also work on bools and always evaluate both sides; ^ is true when exactly one side is. == and != compare two bools. &=, |= and ^= assign the result back. bools have no ordering, and no arithmetic. Conditions in if, while and the other control-flow forms must be bools. Integers aren't true or false on their own; compare them instead, as in count != 0. A bool converts to an integer with i32(flag), as 0 or 1, but nothing converts to bool."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "char", "url": "text.html#char", "text": "A char is one Unicode character, stored as its code point in 4 bytes. Any code point from U+0000 to U+10FFFF is a valid char except the surrogates U+D800 to U+DFFF. A char literal is a character in single quotes, and the source file's UTF-8 can hold any character directly: A literal holds exactly one character, or one escape sequence:"}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Operations on char", "url": "text.html#operations-on-char", "text": "Adding an integer to a char, or subtracting one, gives another char. Subtracting two chars gives the distance between them as an i32. chars compare by code point, and a for loop can walk a range of them, as Control flow shows. A char isn't an integer: other arithmetic, the bitwise operators and mixing a char with an integer type are errors. A step past U+10FFFF or into the surrogates is checked like integer overflow. With constants it's a compile error, \"Addition overflows 'char'\", and at run time a panic with \"char overflow\" in builds with overflow checks."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Converting chars", "url": "text.html#converting-chars", "text": "A char converts to any integer type with T(c), giving its code point, and an integer converts to a char with char(n). Neither conversion happens implicitly. Both are checked: a code point that doesn't fit the integer type, or an integer that isn't a valid char, is an error for a constant and a \"conversion overflow\" panic at run time."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Character literals with a suffix", "url": "text.html#character-literals-with-a-suffix", "text": "An integer type suffix after a char literal makes it an integer holding the code point, as in 'a'u8. This is how byte-oriented code compares bytes with characters, since a u8 and a char don't mix. The code point must fit the type."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Escape sequences", "url": "text.html#escape-sequences", "text": "char and string literals share these escape sequences: | \\n, \\r, \\t | new line, carriage return, tab | | \\0 | the zero character | | \\\\, \\', \\\" | a backslash, a single quote, a double quote | | \\a, \\b, \\f, \\v | bell, backspace, form feed, vertical tab | | \\xHH | the character with hex code HH, from 00 to 7F | | \\oNNN | the character with octal code NNN, from 000 to 177 | | \\uXXXX | the code point XXXX, exactly four hex digits | | \\UXXXXXXXX | the code point XXXXXXXX, exactly eight hex digits | Strings also take \\{ and \\} for braces, which interpolated strings otherwise use. \\0 always means the zero character, so \"\\012\" is a zero followed by the characters 1 and 2; octal codes are written with \\o. \\x and \\o cover only the ASCII range. \\u and \\U take any code point that is a valid char, and a string stores it as its two to four UTF-8 bytes. Any other backslash sequence, or one with the wrong digits, is an error:"}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "str", "url": "text.html#str", "text": "A str is a read-only view of UTF-8 text: a pointer to its first byte and the number of bytes, 16 bytes in all on a 64-bit target. The two parts are its read-only properties data, a ptr[u8], and byteLength, a usize. A str doesn't own its bytes, and nothing guarantees a zero byte after them. The length counts bytes, not characters: a character outside ASCII takes two to four bytes. C's printf prints a str with %.*s, which takes the length as an int and then the pointer:"}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Indexing bytes", "url": "text.html#indexing-bytes", "text": "text[i] reads the byte at index i as a u8. The index is a usize, or a type that converts to one implicitly, and it's checked against byteLength: reading past the end stops the program with \"index out of bounds\". A str can't be changed through: its bytes and its properties are read-only."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Building a str", "url": "text.html#building-a-str", "text": "str(data, byteLength) makes a str from a ptr[u8] and a length, the reverse of its two properties. This is how code takes part of a string, or views bytes from elsewhere as text, such as a buffer or a C string. str() is the empty string. Below, text.data + start points start bytes further on; Pointers and raw memory covers pointer arithmetic. The compiler doesn't check that the pointer and length describe valid UTF-8. A str built from a pointer borrows whatever the pointer came from, so one built from a local buffer can't be returned; Borrows covers the rules. == and + don't work on str values. + works only on constant strings, described below."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "cstr", "url": "text.html#cstr", "text": "A cstr is a pointer to a zero-terminated string, which is what C functions take and return. It's 8 bytes on a 64-bit target and has no length; C functions such as strlen find the end by looking for the zero byte. c[i] reads byte i as a u8; with no length to compare against, the index isn't checked. A cstr can be null, and it converts implicitly to a plain ptr, the untyped pointer described in Pointers and raw memory. A str doesn't convert to a cstr, since its bytes need not be followed by a zero, and a cstr has no arithmetic."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "String literals", "url": "text.html#string-literals", "text": "A string literal in double quotes has no type of its own, like a number. It becomes a str or a cstr where it's used. As a cstr, its bytes are followed by a zero; the zero isn't counted in a str's byteLength. A literal's byteLength and data can be read directly, and byteLength is a constant. A local declared from a literal takes its type from how the function uses it, and is a str when nothing decides. A literal used both ways needs two literals, or an explicit type: A string literal must end on the line it starts on, and two literals next to each other don't join; + joins constant strings."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "String literal suffixes", "url": "text.html#string-literal-suffixes", "text": "A suffix of u8, u16 or u32 turns a string literal into an array of its encoded text, with no zero at the end: u8 gives the UTF-8 bytes, u16 the UTF-16 code units and u32 the code points. This is useful for byte signatures and for APIs that take UTF-16. magic is an array[u8, 6]; Arrays covers arrays. Other suffixes are errors:"}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Constant strings", "url": "text.html#constant-strings", "text": "String literals, const declarations of type str or cstr, and the results of nameOf are constant strings. A val isn't one, even at the top level of a module. + joins constant strings into a new one, at compile time. A str constant and a cstr constant don't join with each other."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Interpolated strings", "url": "text.html#interpolated-strings", "text": "A string literal can contain holes in braces, as in \"{title} v{version}\". Outside a template, an interpolated string is a constant string: every hole must hold a constant string, and the compiler joins the pieces. The result is a string literal, usable as a str or a cstr. Holes can hold str and cstr constants alike, nameOf, + of constant strings, and other string literals, including interpolated ones. A hole with a value that isn't a constant string is an error. Holes that hold numbers or other run-time values, and holes with format specs like {price:.2}, belong to string templates, where a function receives the pieces and the values and decides how to write them."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "Braces in strings", "url": "text.html#braces-in-strings", "text": "A { in any string starts a hole, so a literal brace is written \\{. A } on its own is an ordinary character, and \\} is accepted too. This matters for C format strings and for text like code or JSON: A string literal with holes can't take a suffix: \"a{x}\"u8 is \"Cannot use 'u8' on interpolated strings\"."}, {"page": "Bool, char and strings", "title": "nameOf", "url": "text.html#nameof", "text": "nameOf(x) is a constant string holding the name of x as it's written in the code, without its qualifiers: nameOf(geometry.area) is \"area\". It takes anything with a name, such as a type, function, module, variable, parameter, field, method, enum case or label, and checks that the name exists. Like a string literal, it's usable as a str or a cstr. nameOf only looks the name up. Inside a type, its fields, methods and cases can be named on their own, as in nameOf(unwrap) or nameOf(none), even where using them would need a receiver or a known type. Type arguments pick the type or function and aren't part of the name: nameOf(Option[i32].none) is \"none\". A name that doesn't exist is reported as usual, and anything other than a name is an error:"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Variables and constants", "url": "variables.html", "text": "Variables hold values a program works with. This chapter covers the three kinds of declaration, val, var and const, how a variable's type is chosen, variables that start without a value, assignment, scopes, and variables and constants declared at module level."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "val and var", "url": "variables.html#val-and-var", "text": "val declares a variable that can't be changed after it gets its value. var declares one that can: Assigning to a val, or to any part of one such as a field of a record or an element of an array, is an error: Use val unless the variable needs to change. A var that is never changed gets a hint saying so, shown when the build is given --hints (see Projects and building):"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Types", "url": "variables.html#types", "text": "A type annotation after the name, separated by a colon, says what type the variable has. The value must convert to it: Numbers and arithmetic covers the number types and which literals fit in them, and Conversions covers which values convert to which types."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Inferred types", "url": "variables.html#inferred-types", "text": "Without an annotation, the variable takes the type of its initial value. A value that already has a type, such as the result of a call or another variable, gives it that type. A literal on its own, like 10 or 0.5, doesn't have a fixed type yet. A variable that starts from one takes its type from how the variable is used later in the function. When nothing decides it, an integer literal makes an i32 and a float literal an f64: count and ratio are only printed, so they keep the default types. total is passed where an i64 is expected, so it is an i64. level is given a u16, so it is a u16. The rules are: Where the variable is read as a certain type, such as an argument or the value of a typed variable, it takes that type. When it's read as several types, it takes the one of them that converts to all the others. Values assigned to it must convert to that type. A variable that is only assigned to takes the type that all the assigned values convert to. When no type works, the declaration is an error that lists the types that disagree: A declaration needs a type or a value. With neither, there is nothing to take a type from:"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Variables without a value", "url": "variables.html#variables-without-a-value", "text": "A var declared with a type and no value starts as all zeros, when zeros are a valid value of its type. That holds for numbers, bool, pointers, arrays of such values, and records made of them that don't declare their own constructors: A type whose values are made by its own constructors has no zero value of its own (see Records). A variable of that type starts uninitialized, and it must be assigned before it's used:"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Assigning a val later", "url": "variables.html#assigning-a-val-later", "text": "A val declared without a value gets it from a later assignment. Every path through the function must assign it exactly once before it's read. Here the paths are the two branches of an if (Control flow covers if): Reading it before it's assigned, reading it when only some paths assign it, and assigning it twice are errors: The last error also points at the first assignment, with a note \"Assigned on line\" and its line number."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "undef", "url": "variables.html#undef", "text": "undef as the initial value leaves a variable uninitialized on purpose, even when its type has a zero value. It saves the cost of zeroing storage that is about to be filled anyway: The compiler checks that undef storage is written before it's read: var x = undef without a type takes its type from later uses, like a variable that starts from a literal. undef is only allowed as the initial value of a variable: Pointers and raw memory shows how to fill undef storage through a pointer."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Assignment", "url": "variables.html#assignment", "text": "= stores a new value in a variable, a field or an array element. The compound assignments +=, -=, *=, /=, %= and the others combine an operator with assignment: total += 4 is total = total + 4, with total evaluated once. Numbers and arithmetic lists them all. An assignment is also an expression. Its value is the variable after the assignment, so assignments can be chained and used as arguments: Assignment groups from the right, so x = y = 10 assigns 10 to y and then y to x. Assigning a new value to a variable whose old value has a destructor runs that destructor; Ownership and destructors covers that."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Constants", "url": "variables.html#constants", "text": "const declares a constant: a value computed while compiling. Its initial value must be built from literals, other constants, operators on them and calls to constant functions: Constants can be used where a value must be known while compiling, such as the length of an array type. A val can't, even when its value is a literal: a val is storage that holds a value at run time, and a constant isn't. A constant needs a value, the value must be a constant, and its type can't have a destructor: A constant has no address, so @ can't be applied to it (see Pointers and raw memory):"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Scopes", "url": "variables.html#scopes", "text": "A variable can be used from its declaration to the end of the block it's declared in. A block is a list of statements in { }, such as a function body, the body of an if or a loop, or a block written on its own: When a block ends, its variables are dropped, in the reverse order of their declarations. Ownership and destructors covers what that means for values with destructors."}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Shadowing", "url": "variables.html#shadowing", "text": "A variable in an inner block may have the same name as one outside it. Inside the block, the name means the inner variable. After the block, it means the outer one again. The inner variable doesn't have to have the same type: A function's body is a block inside its parameters, so a variable in the body can reuse a parameter's name, as double does. The new variable's initial value still sees the old name, since the new one only starts after its declaration. Two variables with the same name in the same block are an error, reported at both:"}, {"page": "Variables and constants", "title": "Module-level variables and constants", "url": "variables.html#module-level-variables-and-constants", "text": "Variables and constants can also be declared at the top of a file, outside any function. They are written like other declarations, with the name first: name: var Type = value, name: val Type = value or name: const Type = value. The type and the value are both required. Every function in the module can use them: A const is a constant, as inside a function. A val is storage that can't be written. A var is storage that any function can change. It lives for the whole run of the program. Their initial values are set before the program starts, so each one must be a constant, for a var and a val too: Declaration order doesn't matter at module level. A constant can use one declared further down, as long as no constant depends on its own value: A local variable may have the same name as a module-level one. Inside its scope, the name means the local variable. Module-level declarations can be made visible to other modules or projects; Modules and visibility covers that. Constants and variables declared inside a type belong to the type; Methods and properties covers them."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Functions", "url": "functions.html", "text": "A function is a named piece of code that takes parameters and may return a result. This chapter covers declaring and calling functions, default values, overloading, functions that never return, the order arguments run in, and main, where a program starts."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Declaring a function", "url": "functions.html#declaring-a-function", "text": "A function is declared like everything else in Cella: a name, a colon, then what the name is. fun makes it a function. The parameters follow in parentheses, then -> and the result type, then the body in braces: A call names the function and passes its arguments in parentheses, separated by commas. ret ends the function and gives back its result. A function that takes no parameters leaves out the parentheses, as main does above. Empty parentheses, as in answer: fun() -> i32, are accepted too, and --hints reports them as redundant. A call always has parentheses, even when it passes nothing: A function's name without them is a function value, covered in Function values and closures."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Parameters", "url": "functions.html#parameters", "text": "Each parameter has a name and a type, written name: type. Parameter names in one function must be different, and _ names a parameter the body doesn't use: A parameter like these gets its own copy of the argument. The function can change it, and the caller's value stays as it was. Using one name for two parameters is an error: A parameter can also borrow its argument, with the modes imm and mut written before its name, and a parameter without a mode takes over a value that can't be copied. Parameter modes covers them."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Results and ret", "url": "functions.html#results-and-ret", "text": "The type after -> is the function's result. Every path through the body must end in a ret with a value of that type, or in something that never finishes, such as an endless loop: A function without -> returns nothing. Its body runs to its end, or to a ret with no value: Such a function can't return a value, and a call to it can't be used as one: A function with a result can be called as a statement, and its result is thrown away. That's how the examples in this documentation call printf, which returns an i32. Code right after a ret, which can never run, gets the warning \"Unreachable code\"."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Expression bodies", "url": "functions.html#expression-bodies", "text": "When a function's body is a single expression, => replaces the braces and the ret: In a function with a result, the expression is the result. In a function without one, like show, the expression runs and its value, if any, is thrown away. A long expression wraps after =>, never before it. The expression can be a match, with its arms on the lines below. Pattern matching covers match."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Default parameter values", "url": "functions.html#default-parameter-values", "text": "A parameter can have a default value, written = value after its type. A call may leave out trailing parameters that have defaults: Arguments fill parameters from the left, so a call can't skip a parameter and pass a later one. The rules for defaults: A default must be a constant: a literal, a named const, an enum case, null or a function's name. It can't call a function. Every parameter after one with a default needs a default too. mut parameters can't have defaults, since they need a place to change. Defaults belong to the declaration, not to the function's type. A call through a function value passes every argument:"}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Overloading", "url": "functions.html#overloading", "text": "Several functions can share a name if their parameters differ in number or in type. A call picks the one its arguments fit: An argument that matches a parameter's type exactly is the best fit. Otherwise the argument converts, and a call prefers the overload whose conversions are cheapest: an i16 reaches both i32 and i64, and the narrower i32 wins. A u32 doesn't fit in an i32, so it goes to i64. A literal takes the type of the overload it fits best, so 5 is an i32 and 5000000000, too large for one, is an i64. Conversions lists which conversions happen implicitly. When no overload is a better fit than the others, the call is ambiguous, and the error lists the candidates: A call that fits no overload says so:"}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Overloads that conflict", "url": "functions.html#overloads-that-conflict", "text": "Overloads must be told apart by the arguments of a call. Parameter names and result types don't count; parameter modes do, through the call's marks (Parameter modes). Two overloads also conflict when defaults let one call reach both. Each of these is an error on every declaration involved: log() could call either log, so the default on the first one could never be used."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Recursion and declaration order", "url": "functions.html#recursion-and-declaration-order", "text": "A function can call itself, and functions can call each other. The order of declarations in a file doesn't matter: a function can call one declared below it, and main can come first or last. The same holds across files: every function in a project can be used from anywhere the module rules allow. A function that calls itself on every path, such as count: fun(n: i32) -> i32 => count(n + 1), can never finish, and it gets the warning \"'count' recurses forever\". A path that returns, panics or calls a function that never returns, without calling itself first, is enough to avoid it."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Calling through a module", "url": "functions.html#calling-through-a-module", "text": "Every function belongs to the module named by its file's mod line. A function in another module is called through that module's name: Without the module name, area isn't found from example, unless the file imports it with use. Code can name a function of its own module the same way. Modules and visibility covers imports, nested modules and which declarations other modules can see."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Functions that never return", "url": "functions.html#functions-that-never-return", "text": "A function whose result type is never doesn't return to its caller: it ends the program, or loops forever. C's exit is one, declared that way in an ext block: divide has no ret after the call to fail, and it doesn't need one: since fail never returns, the compiler knows that path never reaches the end of divide. Code after a call to a never function gets the \"Unreachable code\" warning. A never function can't ret at all, and no path may reach the end of its body. never is only a result type; parameters, variables and fields can't have it: Checks and panics covers panic, the usual way to stop a program with an error."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Evaluation order", "url": "functions.html#evaluation-order", "text": "Arguments run from left to right, before the call. Operands of an operator run left to right too, whatever the operator's precedence: Arguments passed with mut or imm reach their place at the call, after the other arguments have run. Parameter modes shows where that matters."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "The main function", "url": "functions.html#the-main-function", "text": "A program starts in the function named main. A project that builds a program needs exactly one."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Results and exit codes", "url": "functions.html#results-and-exit-codes", "text": "main can return nothing, an i32 or never: With no result, the program exits with code 0 when main ends. With -> i32, the value main returns is the program's exit code. With -> never, main never returns, and the program ends some other way, such as a call to C's exit. This program exits with code 1."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Command-line arguments", "url": "functions.html#command-line-arguments", "text": "To read its command-line arguments, main takes a pointer to the first one and their count: Each argument is a str, UTF-8 text. The first one, at index 0, is the path of the program itself, so a program's own arguments start at index 1. Pointers covers ptr[T] and indexing through it, and Bool, char and strings covers str. main can instead take one parameter of any type that can be built from that pointer and count, that is, one with a constructor taking a ptr[str] and a usize. A record with those two fields works, through its field-by-field constructor: A type with a destructor is taken by value or with imm, as in main: fun(args: Arguments), and its destructor runs when main ends. Records covers constructors."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Rules for main", "url": "functions.html#rules-for-main", "text": "main must have one of the shapes above, with no defaults, no type parameters and no mut parameter: A program with no main at all fails with \"The program has no 'main' function\"."}, {"page": "Functions", "title": "Starting somewhere else with --entry", "url": "functions.html#starting-somewhere-else-with---entry", "text": "cella build and cella run take --entry with a function's full name, module first. The program then starts in that function instead of main: The first command prints main and exits with 0. The second prints self test and exits with 2. The entry function must be pub, take no parameters, have no type parameters, and return i32, never or nothing, like main. The project then doesn't need a main, and one that exists is ignored, whatever its shape. Projects and building covers the build commands and their options."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Control flow", "url": "control-flow.html", "text": "Cella's control flow is close to C's: if and else, while, do ... while, an endless loop, and for over ranges and arrays, with break and cont to leave or restart a loop. Conditions need no parentheses, and a body is a single statement or a block. This chapter covers each form, labels, jumps used as expressions, and blocks."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "if and else", "url": "control-flow.html#if-and-else", "text": "if takes a condition and a body. The condition is any bool expression, written without parentheses. The body is the next statement: one statement, usually on the next line and indented, or a block in braces. else adds a statement that runs when the condition is false: When one branch needs braces, the usual style gives every branch braces, as above."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "else if", "url": "control-flow.html#else-if", "text": "There is no separate elif keyword. else if is an else whose statement is another if, so chains read as they do in C:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Conditions", "url": "control-flow.html#conditions", "text": "A condition must be a bool. Numbers and pointers don't count as true or false on their own, so compare them explicitly: && and || combine conditions, and the right side runs only when it's needed; Bool, char and strings covers them. An is test, as in if shape is circle(val r), checks an enum's case and can bind its payload; Pattern matching covers it."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "if is a statement", "url": "control-flow.html#if-is-a-statement", "text": "if doesn't produce a value, and Cella has no ?: operator. For a value that depends on a condition, use a match on the condition: A body that is a single statement can't be a declaration, since the name would end with the body. Use a block instead:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "while", "url": "control-flow.html#while", "text": "while runs its body as long as its condition is true. The condition is checked before each pass, so the body may not run at all:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "do ... while", "url": "control-flow.html#do-while", "text": "do runs its body first and checks the condition after each pass, so the body always runs at least once. while and the condition follow the body: After a block, the usual style puts while on the closing brace's line. After a single statement, it goes on its own line."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "loop", "url": "control-flow.html#loop", "text": "loop repeats its body forever. A break leaves it, and a ret leaves the whole function: A loop with no break never finishes, so the compiler knows the end of firstPositive can't be reached, and the function needs no ret after it. The same holds for while true."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "for over ranges", "url": "control-flow.html#for-over-ranges", "text": "for with a range runs its body once for each integer in the range. a..b counts from a up to but not including b, and a..=b includes b: A range whose start is past its end, like 5..2, is empty, and the body doesn't run. Ranges only count up, one at a time. Both bounds run once, before the first pass. Changing a variable used as a bound doesn't change how many times the loop runs:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "The loop variable", "url": "control-flow.html#the-loop-variable", "text": "The loop variable is new on each pass and only exists inside the body. Its type comes from the bounds, as the type of a + b would: 0..count with a usize count counts in usize. ..= can count up to the largest value of its type without overflowing: Name the variable _ when the body doesn't use it: Bounds can also be char values, which step through the characters in order: The loop variable is read-only: Floats can't be range bounds, and the variable of a range loop can't take a mode such as mut:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "for over arrays", "url": "control-flow.html#for-over-arrays", "text": "for over an array walks its elements in order: Arrays covers the rest: borrowing elements with for imm x and for mut x, and how for x takes elements that can't be copied. Types can also define their own for loops; Operators shows how."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Counting passes", "url": "control-flow.html#counting-passes", "text": "A second name after a comma counts the passes, starting at 0. for x, i in xs gives each element along with its position. It works with every kind of for, and it counts every pass, including ones that cont cuts short: The count's type comes from how it's used, as with var count = 0. Indexing tens with it makes the first loop's i a usize, while the second loop's i is an i32, since nothing there settles it. Counting past the largest value of its type is an overflow, the same as count += 1. Like the loop variable, the count is new on each pass and can't be assigned."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "break and cont", "url": "control-flow.html#break-and-cont", "text": "break leaves a loop at once. cont skips the rest of the body and goes on with the next pass: in a for loop, to the next value; in while and do ... while, to the condition. Both act on the innermost loop around them: break and cont outside a loop are errors:"}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Labels", "url": "control-flow.html#labels", "text": "A label names a loop, so that break and cont can act on a loop further out than the innermost one. It's written before the loop keyword, with a colon, and the jump names it: break search leaves both loops as soon as a pair is found. cont rows leaves the inner loop and goes on with the next row, so a row with a negative value prints nothing. Every loop form takes a label: for, while, do and loop. Labels can't go on other statements, and a jump must name a label: Labels have their own names, apart from variables, so a label can share a name with a variable without hiding it."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Jumps as expressions", "url": "control-flow.html#jumps-as-expressions", "text": "ret, break and cont are expressions as well as statements. They never produce a value, since control leaves before one is needed, so they fit anywhere a value of any type is expected. This makes them useful in match arms and on the right of && and ||: In check, ok || ret 7 returns 7 from the function when ok is false. In the loop, each match either gives a value or leaves the pass."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "Blocks and scopes", "url": "control-flow.html#blocks-and-scopes", "text": "A block is a list of statements in braces. Function bodies and loop bodies are blocks, and a block can also stand on its own as a statement. Each block is a scope: names declared in it exist from their declaration to the closing brace, and a loop variable belongs to its loop's body in the same way. Variables and constants covers scopes and shadowing, and Ownership and destructors covers when values with destructors are destroyed."}, {"page": "Control flow", "title": "match and when", "url": "control-flow.html#match-and-when", "text": "match picks one of several arms by comparing a value against patterns, as a statement or as an expression; Pattern matching covers it. when keeps or removes code depending on build flags; Conditional compilation covers it."}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Arrays", "url": "arrays.html", "text": "An array is a fixed number of values of one type, stored one after another. Its type is array[T, N]: the element type T and the length N, which is part of the type and known when the program compiles. Arrays live wherever the variable holding them lives, on the stack or inside a record, and never allocate."}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Array literals", "url": "arrays.html#array-literals", "text": "An array literal lists its elements between brackets. Its type comes from the elements: primes is an array[i32, 4]. The elements of scores are an integer and two floats, so all three become f64 and the array is an array[f64, 3]. Integer literals that don't fit in an i32 make the elements a wider type, as they would in arithmetic. Elements that can't share one type are an error:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Writing the type", "url": "arrays.html#writing-the-type", "text": "A type on the declaration gives the elements their type, and the literal must have exactly that many elements: An empty literal [] has no elements to take a type from, so it needs one from the declaration:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Indexing", "url": "arrays.html#indexing", "text": "a[i] is the element at index i, counting from 0. The index is a usize. Unsigned integers narrower than usize convert to it, but signed ones don't, so an i32 index needs usize(i): Writing to an element changes the array, so the array must be a var. A val array can't be changed through any of its elements:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Bounds checks", "url": "arrays.html#bounds-checks", "text": "An index that is a constant is checked when the program compiles: Any other index is checked when the program runs. An index past the end stops the program with a panic instead of reading memory outside the array: The check runs in every build, release builds included. A project can turn it off with the BoundsChecks setting; Checks and panics covers panics and that setting."}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Length", "url": "arrays.html#length", "text": "a.length is the number of elements, as a usize. Since the length is part of the type, it's the same for every value of that type:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Sizes from constants", "url": "arrays.html#sizes-from-constants", "text": "The length in array[T, N] is a constant usize. It can be a literal, a named constant or any constant expression, including sizeOf. Here the arrays are fields of records, which group named values like a C struct (Records covers them): A variable can't be a length, even one that never changes. Constants of other integer types need a conversion: An array's size in memory is its length times its element's size, with no header or padding between elements:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Zeroed arrays", "url": "arrays.html#zeroed-arrays", "text": "An array declared without a value starts with every element zeroed, when its element type allows it: Numbers, bool, char and pointers start zeroed, and so do records whose fields all can, as long as they declare no constructor and no destructor. A record that declares a constructor, a new: op member, can't (see Records). An array of those starts uninitialized, and each element must be set before it's read: A loop that sets every element counts, as long as its bounds are constants:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "The array builder", "url": "arrays.html#the-array-builder", "text": "array[T, N](make) builds an array by calling make once for each index, in order from 0, and storing what it returns. make takes the index as a usize and returns a T. It's often a lambda (see Function values and closures), and it can use the variables around it: Any function that takes a usize and returns the element type works, named functions included. The builder works for every element type, including records that can't start zeroed: The function must have the right shape:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Nested arrays", "url": "arrays.html#nested-arrays", "text": "An array's elements can be arrays. array[array[i32, 3], 2] is two rows of three, and grid[r][c] indexes the row, then the element in it: The rows of a nested literal share one element type, so they must all have the same length. Their elements are typed together, so a float in one row makes every row f64:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Copying and moving arrays", "url": "arrays.html#copying-and-moving-arrays", "text": "An array copies when its element type copies. Assigning it, passing it to a parameter without a mode, or returning it makes a new array with the same elements, and changing one leaves the other alone: A destructor is a drop: op member that runs when a value is dropped (Ownership and destructors covers them). An array of values with a destructor can't be copied, since each element has one owner. It moves instead, and its elements are dropped when the array is, last element first: keep takes the array by value, so the array moves into it and is dropped when keep returns. A function that only reads the array takes it with imm. A single element can be moved out, as in keepOne(names[1]), when the index is a constant. The moved element is dropped by its new owner, and the rest of the array is still dropped when it goes out of scope: The other elements can still be used, but the array as a whole can't, since part of it is gone. An index computed when the program runs can't be used to move an element out: Ownership and destructors covers moves and drops in general, and Parameter modes covers imm and mut parameters, which let a function read or change an array without copying it."}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Looping over arrays", "url": "arrays.html#looping-over-arrays", "text": "for x in a runs its body once for each element, from the first to the last. How the loop variable holds the element depends on the word before it: | for x in a | the element by value: a copy, or the element moved out of the array | a can be moved, for elements that don't copy | | for imm x in a | a read-only borrow of the element | nothing | | for mut x in a | a mutable borrow of the element | a can be changed | A copy is enough for plain data. mut changes the elements in place: Elements with a destructor can't be copied, so for n in names would move them out of the array. To look at them, the loop takes them with imm:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Moving elements out", "url": "arrays.html#moving-elements-out", "text": "for x in a over elements that can't be copied moves each element out of the array into the loop variable. An element that the body doesn't move on is dropped at the end of its pass. If the loop ends early with break, the elements it didn't reach are dropped when the loop ends: After the loop the array has been moved and can't be used. An array the function doesn't own, such as an imm parameter, can't be moved out of, so a plain for x in a over it borrows each element instead, as for imm x in a would."}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "Loops over temporaries", "url": "arrays.html#loops-over-temporaries", "text": "The array can be any expression, such as a call. A temporary array lives until the loop ends, so imm elements stay valid for the whole loop:"}, {"page": "Arrays", "title": "What the loop rejects", "url": "arrays.html#what-the-loop-rejects", "text": "For an array, the word goes before the variable, never before the array: for mut changes the array, so the array must be a var: Records can define their own for loops with loop operators; see Operators."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Records", "url": "records.html", "text": "A record groups named values, its fields, into one type, like a struct in C. This chapter covers declaring records, making values of them, constructors and how records are laid out in memory. Functions that belong to a record are in Methods and properties, and destructors are in Ownership and destructors."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Declaring a record", "url": "records.html#declaring-a-record", "text": "A record is declared with rec, and its fields go between braces, one per line, each with a name and a type: p.x reads or writes the field x of p. Fields can be numbers, pointers, arrays, enums, function types and other records. A field that holds a borrow, such as a str, needs a ref rec, covered in Borrows. Fields can also share one line, separated by commas, as in Point: rec { x: i32, y: i32 }. Either way works, but one body uses one style: Lexical structure has the rules. A field can't have an initial value of its own. Values come from constructors, described below: Records can be declared in any order in a file, and a record can use a type declared after it. A record can't contain itself, directly or through an array, because its size would have no end. A pointer to itself is fine:"}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Empty records", "url": "records.html#empty-records", "text": "A record can have no fields. It is written either with empty braces or with no body at all, and its values take no space: Without a body, the declaration must end its line."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Creating values", "url": "records.html#creating-values", "text": "A record with no declared constructors gets two ways to make a value. Point(3, 4) passes one argument for each field, in the order the fields are declared. Point() makes a value with every field zeroed: The arguments convert to the field types as they would for a function's parameters, so 255 becomes a u8 and 0.5 an f32. Any other number of arguments is an error, and the compiler lists the fields: The error's note lists what Point takes: \"'Point' takes no arguments, or one for each field: x, y\". Arguments are matched to fields by position only."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Declarations without a value", "url": "records.html#declarations-without-a-value", "text": "A variable of a record type declared without a value is the same as one set to T(): every field starts zeroed. Fields can then be set one at a time:"}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Legal defaults", "url": "records.html#legal-defaults", "text": "A type has a legal default when all-zero memory is a valid value of it. Numbers, bool, char, pointers and str have one. A record has one when all of its fields have one, it declares no constructor and it has no destructor. Only types with a legal default get the built-in T() and start zeroed. Some types have no legal default, such as function types. A record with a field like that has none either, so T() isn't available and only the field-by-field constructor is left: A variable of such a type starts uninitialized instead of zeroed. Its fields that do have defaults are zeroed, and the others must be set before the whole value is used: Using the value before then is an error. A field that isn't set yet makes the record partly initialized:"}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Required fields", "url": "records.html#required-fields", "text": "req before a field's type removes that field's default, even when its type has one. Every value of the record must then set it explicitly, which suits fields where zero is a mistake, such as a handle or an ID: Since the record no longer has a legal default, File() isn't available, and reading b.handle before it's set is an error: When a required field is itself a record, writing any of its fields counts as setting it, and its other fields keep their own defaults. Fields whose type has no default, such as function types, are already required, and req on them gets the hint \"Redundant 'req'\"."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Constructors", "url": "records.html#constructors", "text": "A record can declare its own constructors. A constructor is a member named new, declared as op with parameters and a block body. It's called with the record's name, like the built-in ones: A constructor with no parameters is written new: op, with no parentheses, as a function without parameters is. Declaring any constructor replaces both built-in ones. The record no longer has a field-by-field constructor, T() exists only if a constructor without parameters is declared, and the record loses its legal default. This holds for every instantiation of a generic record, including constructors declared in an [impl block](generics.md#adding-members-under-bounds): A variable declared without a value starts uninitialized:"}, {"page": "Records", "title": "What self is", "url": "records.html#what-self-is", "text": "Inside a constructor, self is the value being built. Its fields that have a default start zeroed, so the constructor only has to set the others: A constructor must set every field without a default before it ends, on every path through it. An if that sets a field on only one branch isn't enough: The error's note names the field: \"'id' is uninitialized\". self can't be used as a whole, for example passed to a function or used to call a method, while some field is still unset. Once every field is set, it can: A constructor can end early with a bare ret, as long as every field is set by then. It can't return a value: A constructor's body is always a block; the => form of functions isn't available for constructors."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Assigning to self", "url": "records.html#assigning-to-self", "text": "A constructor can set self as a whole, for example to reuse another constructor or a function that builds the value: Assigning to self replaces the value it held. If the record has a destructor and every field has a default, self starts as a whole all-zero value, so that value is dropped when it's replaced: Slot.make is a static function, covered in Methods and properties, and undef is covered in Variables and constants."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Overloading constructors", "url": "records.html#overloading-constructors", "text": "A record can have any number of constructors, as long as no two can be called with the same arguments. The rules are the same as for overloaded functions (see Functions): constructors that differ only in parameter modes conflict, and so do constructors that accept the same arguments through default parameter values: A call picks the constructor whose parameters fit its arguments, as for functions."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Restoring the default", "url": "records.html#restoring-the-default", "text": "new: op = default declares a constructor without parameters that zeroes every field, and gives the record its legal default back. With it, T() works and declarations without a value start zeroed, alongside the record's other constructors: A call to it is a constant, so origin: const Vec2 = Vec2() works. = default is allowed only on the constructor without parameters, and only when every field has a default: The first error's notes list each field and why it has no default: \"'run' has no default\" and \"'id' is required\". A record with a destructor has no legal default either. new: op = default is allowed there too, and gives it one."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Nested records", "url": "records.html#nested-records", "text": "A field can hold another record. A field chain such as line.start.x reaches inside, and nested values are made by passing records to the outer constructor: Line() and zeroed declarations work here because Point has a legal default. An outer record has one only when every record inside it does."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Copying records", "url": "records.html#copying-records", "text": "A record whose fields can all be copied is copied whenever it's assigned, passed to a parameter without a mode or returned. The copy is independent of the original: A record copies however large it is. A record with a destructor, or with a field that can't be copied, is declared own rec and isn't copied: it moves. Ownership and destructors covers moves, and Parameter modes covers imm and mut parameters, which borrow a record instead of copying it."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "own rec", "url": "records.html#own-rec", "text": "own rec declares a record whose values move instead of copying. A record with a destructor, or with a field that owns a value, must be declared own rec (Ownership and destructors). It also works without a destructor, for values that stand for something that must have exactly one owner, such as a ticket or a handle owned by other code: redeem takes the ticket over, and main can't use it afterwards: A record with an own rec field must be declared own rec too."}, {"page": "Records", "title": "Layout and size", "url": "records.html#layout-and-size", "text": "A record's fields are laid out in memory in the order they're declared, the way a C compiler lays out a struct. Each field starts at a multiple of its alignment, with padding before it when needed, and the record's size is rounded up to a multiple of its largest alignment, so that records in an array stay aligned. sizeOf(T) gives a type's size in bytes and alignOf(T) its alignment; both are usize, and both also accept a value: Loose has seven bytes of padding after tag, so that value starts at offset 8, and seven more after flag. Tight puts the large field first and needs only six bytes of padding at the end. The compiler never reorders fields, which is what lets a record match a C struct field for field; Calling C covers passing records to C."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Methods and properties", "url": "methods.html", "text": "A type's body can hold functions as well as fields. Functions that work on a value of the type are methods, and functions that belong to the type without a value are static functions. Properties look like fields but run code when they're read or written. This chapter covers all three, the static fields and constants a type can hold, and the visibility words that hide members from code outside the type. The examples use records. Methods, static members, properties and member visibility work the same way in enums, as the last section says."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Methods", "url": "methods.html#methods", "text": "A method is a function declared inside a type whose first parameter is self. It's called on a value with a dot, and self is that value: self has no type written; it's always the type the method is declared in. Fields and other methods are reached through it, as self.width and self.area(). A bare name inside a method never means a field or a method: Members can be declared in any order, and a method can call methods declared after it, or itself. Methods can be overloaded like functions (see Functions): scaled: fun(self, factor: i32) and scaled: fun(self, factor: f64) can live side by side."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Receivers", "url": "methods.html#receivers", "text": "self is the method's receiver, and like any parameter it has a mode. The mode says what the method can do with the value it's called on: | self | the value itself: a copy, or the value moved into the method | read, change and consume it | | imm self | a read-only borrow of the value | read it | | mut self | a mutable borrow of the value | read and change it | The call is written the same way for every receiver: r.area(), r.grow(1). Unlike other arguments, the receiver never needs imm or mut at the call site. Parameter modes explains the modes in general. A mut self method changes the value it's called on, so the value must be something that can change, such as a var: It can't be called on a temporary either, such as Rect(2, 3).grow(1), since the change would be lost; that is the error \"Cannot mutably borrow unstored values\". The other receivers work on temporaries. With imm self, the method can't change the value. Assigning to one of its fields is an error: A self method has the value to itself, so it can change it; for a type that copies, the caller's value stays as it was."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Copy or borrow", "url": "methods.html#copy-or-borrow", "text": "For a type that copies, self and imm self both read the value, and the difference only shows when the value changes during the call. A copy keeps the value as it was when the call started, while a borrow sees it as it is now: A type that can't be copied moves into a self method instead, so a method that only reads such a value takes imm self."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Consuming the value", "url": "methods.html#consuming-the-value", "text": "A self method on a type that can't be copied takes the value over. The value is dropped at the end of the method unless the method moves it on, and the caller can't use it afterwards: Only a value the caller owns can be consumed. Through an imm or mut borrow, such a method can't be called: A method that consumes a value with a destructor can take a field out of self with own self.field. The value then counts as taken apart: its destructor doesn't run at the end of the method, and only the fields left in it are destroyed. This is how a value hands over what it holds: Without own, val letter = self.letter would only look at the field, since a field of a value with a destructor can't be moved out any other way."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Calling through pointers and borrows", "url": "methods.html#calling-through-pointers-and-borrows", "text": "A method call through a pointer or a borrow reaches the value it points to, like -> in C: Pointers and raw memory covers @ and pointers."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Static functions", "url": "methods.html#static-functions", "text": "A function in a type's body without a self parameter is a static function. It belongs to the type rather than to a value, and it's called through the type's name. Static functions are the usual way to give a constructor a name: Inside the type, a static function can be called without the type's name, and it hides a module-level function with the same name: origin() in reset calls Point.origin, while origin() in main calls the function outside the type. Static functions are called through the type and methods through a value, never the other way around. A static function has no self to use: Since the two are called differently, a method and a static function can share a name:"}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Static fields and constants", "url": "methods.html#static-fields-and-constants", "text": "A var or val member is a static field: one variable shared by the whole program, stored once rather than in each value. It's written like a module-level variable, with a type and an initial value, and it's reached through the type's name, or without it inside the type. A const member is a named constant in the same way: sizeOf(Enemy) counts only health; static fields and constants take no space in the values. The initial value must be a constant, and a val can't be changed after it. Variables and constants covers the difference between val, var and const. A static field belongs to the type, not to a value, so it can't be reached through one: The other rules for static fields:"}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Properties", "url": "methods.html#properties", "text": "A property is a member that is used like a field but runs a getter when it's read and a setter when it's written. prop declares one with both accessors: hero.health = v calls the setter with v as value. A compound assignment such as hero.health -= 50 calls the getter, then the setter with the result. Every other use calls the getter. Inside prop, the accessors' types can be left out, since they come from the property's type. When they're written they must match it: get(self) -> i32 and set(mut self, value: i32). Either accessor's body can be => expr or a block. A prop needs both accessors, and each at most once:"}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Getters and setters on their own", "url": "methods.html#getters-and-setters-on-their-own", "text": "A property with only one accessor is written as a standalone get or set member, with its types written out. A getter takes only the receiver and returns a value; a setter takes the receiver and one value and returns nothing: A getter-only property can't be written and a setter-only property can't be read. That includes compound assignments, which do both: One name has one form: two standalone accessors with the same name conflict, and so do a property and a field. For a property with both a getter and a setter, use prop."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Accessor rules", "url": "methods.html#accessor-rules", "text": "Either accessor's receiver can be self, imm self or mut self, and self only for a type that copies. A setter usually takes mut self, since it changes the value. A getter with mut self can change the value while it's read, so reading it needs a value that can change, as with a mut self method. Getters take no parameters, and setters take exactly one, which can't have a mode: A property isn't stored anywhere, so it can't be borrowed, moved or have its address taken. Passing mut hero.health to a function is the error \"Cannot mutably borrow properties\". Read it into a variable, change that, and assign it back. An assignment to a property has no value, so val x = (hero.health = 5) is an error, unlike an assignment to a field."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Static properties", "url": "methods.html#static-properties", "text": "Without a receiver, an accessor is static: it belongs to the type, like a static function, and it's used through the type's name: A prop is either static or not: both accessors take a receiver, or neither does. \"Cannot combine static and instance accessors\" is the error for mixing them."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Visibility", "url": "methods.html#visibility", "text": "Every member is visible wherever its type is, unless it says otherwise. Two words restrict a member: | pvt | only inside its type: in the type's own methods, static functions, constructors, properties and operators | | mod | anywhere in its module, including the module's other files | The word goes after the colon, before the rest of the member: hp: pvt i32, helper: pvt fun(self) -> i32, new: pvt op(value: i32). A private constructor, as here, means values can only be made by the type's own code, such as the static function empty. The built-in constructors follow the fields. Outside the type, the field-by-field constructor can't set a pvt or pvt set field (see Read-only fields), so it's unavailable there; T() still works when the record has a legal default. There is no pub for members: a member of a pub type is exported along with it. Modules and visibility covers visibility for top-level declarations, and the rule that a member can't use a type less visible than itself in its signature."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Read-only fields", "url": "methods.html#read-only-fields", "text": "pvt set makes a field readable everywhere but writable only inside its type. mod set makes it writable only inside its module. This is the usual way to expose a value that the type keeps consistent: Writing the field outside the type, or anything that could change it, such as passing it as mut or calling a mut self method on it, is an error: pvt set and mod set work on fields and on var static fields. A val can't be written anywhere, so they aren't allowed on one, and they aren't allowed on functions."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Restricting one accessor", "url": "methods.html#restricting-one-accessor", "text": "In a prop, one accessor can be restricted, which makes the property read-only or write-only outside the type: Only one of the two can be restricted; restricting both is the error \"Cannot restrict both accessors\". To hide the whole property, put the word on the property: health: pvt prop i32."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "mod members", "url": "methods.html#mod-members", "text": "A mod member is visible to every file of its module, and hidden from other modules. Here game.ce declares a module game, and main.ce uses it: reward, in the same module as Score, can write bonus. In main, even reading it is an error: \"'bonus' is private to its module\". Modules and visibility covers modules and use."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Visibility blocks", "url": "methods.html#visibility-blocks", "text": "When several neighboring members share a visibility, a pvt { } or mod { } block inside the type applies it to all of them: Members in a block can't repeat or contradict its word: a pvt block can't hold mod members, and a mod block can't hold pvt ones. A mod block can still hold pvt set fields. A destructor is never restricted, so it can't be pvt or mod, or sit in such a block."}, {"page": "Methods and properties", "title": "Methods in enums", "url": "methods.html#methods-in-enums", "text": "Enums take methods, static functions, static fields and properties the same way records do, and in a method self is the enum value. Enums can't declare fields or constructors. Enums shows them."}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Enums", "url": "enums.html", "text": "An enum is a type whose values come from a fixed list of cases. A case can be a plain name, like a C enum constant, or it can hold values of its own, called its payload. This chapter covers declaring enums, creating their values, testing which case a value is and converting enums to and from integers. Taking payloads apart is mostly the job of match, covered in Pattern matching."}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Declaring an enum", "url": "enums.html#declaring-an-enum", "text": "An enum declaration lists its cases, one per line or separated by commas on one line: A case is written Type.case. Two values of a plain enum compare with == and !=; they have no ordering, so < and the other comparisons are errors. Case names are camelCase by convention. An enum needs at least one case, and each case name can appear only once:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Case values", "url": "enums.html#case-values", "text": "Every case has an integer value. The first case is 0 and each case after it is one more than the case before. A case can set its own value with =, and the cases after it count on from there: A value can be any constant integer expression. It can use constants and earlier cases of the same enum by their bare names, and cases of other enums once they're converted to an integer: The value has to be known when the program is compiled, and it has to be an integer. Two enums whose values depend on each other are an error: Two cases with the same value are two names for the same case. Here stop and halt are interchangeable:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "The tag type", "url": "enums.html#the-tag-type", "text": "An enum is stored as an integer, called its tag. Without a tag type, the compiler picks the smallest integer type that holds every case value. Writing an integer type in parentheses after enum sets the tag type yourself: The tag type can be any integer type. With a tag type, every case value must fit in it, and a typed value must convert to it without loss:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "The default case", "url": "enums.html#the-default-case", "text": "The case with the value 0 is the enum's default. A variable declared without a value starts as that case, and so does an enum field of a record built with T(): A case with the value 0 is required. Only an ext enum, covered in Calling C, and the tagless enums at the end of this chapter can do without one:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Payloads", "url": "enums.html#payloads", "text": "A case can hold values. Its payload is written like a parameter list, with a name and a type for each value. A case with a payload is created by calling it with one argument per value: The match in area picks the arm for the case and binds the payload's values to r, w and h. Pattern matching covers this in full. A case must be given exactly the values it holds, and a case without a payload takes no parentheses: Each payload value needs a name, and names can't repeat within a case. A case can't hold its own enum directly, since the value would have to contain itself; it can hold a pointer to one instead: Values of an enum with payloads have no ==; test them with is or match instead. Such an enum is stored as its tag followed by room for its largest payload: If the case with the value 0 holds values, the enum has no default, and a variable of it must be given a value before it's read: Cases that share a value must also hold the same payload types:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Bare case names", "url": "enums.html#bare-case-names", "text": "Where the compiler already knows which enum it needs, a case can be written without the type name. That covers typed declarations, arguments, return values, assignments, array elements and comparisons with a value of the enum: Without an expected type, a bare case name is an error. A name that could also mean a variable or function of the expected type is ambiguous, and has to be written in full:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Testing for a case", "url": "enums.html#testing-for-a-case", "text": "value is case is true when the value is that case. For a case with a payload, the payload goes in parentheses: _ accepts any value, and val name binds the value to a name that can be used where the test is known to be true: The case after is can be bare or qualified. is accepts every pattern match does; Pattern matching lists them."}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Converting to and from integers", "url": "enums.html#converting-to-and-from-integers", "text": "An enum without payloads converts to any integer type with T(x), which gives its case value. An integer converts back with the enum's name: These conversions are never implicit. A constant that matches no case is a compile error, and a value that matches no case at run time panics: An enum with payloads has no conversion to or from integers. An enum can't declare its own conversions to or from integer types either, since those are built in (Conversions covers declared conversions):"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Methods", "url": "enums.html#methods", "text": "An enum can declare methods, static functions, properties and static fields, as a record does; Methods and properties covers them. Inside a method, self is the enum value, so match self is the usual way to look at it, and a mut self method can replace self with another case: An enum can't declare fields, constructors or a destructor, and a method can't share a name with a case:"}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Owned enums", "url": "enums.html#owned-enums", "text": "A plain enum copies like an integer. own enum declares an enum whose values can't be copied, only moved, the way own rec does for records. After a move, the old variable can't be used: An enum whose payloads hold owning values, such as a record with a destructor, must be declared own enum (\"Cannot store owning values in enums that aren't 'own'\"). Ownership and destructors explains moves."}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Generic enums", "url": "enums.html#generic-enums", "text": "An enum can take type parameters, written after its name. Each use names the type, or lets the compiler infer it: Generics covers type parameters and their constraints."}, {"page": "Enums", "title": "Tagless enums", "url": "enums.html#tagless-enums", "text": "enum match T declares an enum that stores a value of type T and nothing else. Its cases sort the values of T into groups: a case lists the values it stands for after =, and one case written = else takes every other value. A case can have a payload written as a name with no type, which is the value itself, and the else case must have one: A tagless enum takes no more room than T, and converting between it and T never changes the value. Converting from T can't fail, since the else case accepts everything the other cases don't. A case with a payload can be created with a value, which has to be one that case stands for; a constant that isn't is a compile error, and a value that isn't panics at run time: T can be an integer type, char, bool, a pointer type or a function type. For pointer and function types the only value a case can list is null, which makes an enum for pointers that may be null: A case without a payload needs a value, the values must be constants, and no value can be listed twice. The else case is needed unless the listed values cover every value of T, and there can be only one: A tagless enum has no tag type, so enum(u8) match i32 is an error, and listing several values or writing else is only allowed in an enum match."}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Pattern matching", "url": "pattern-matching.html", "text": "A pattern describes a shape a value can have: a particular number, a range, an enum case with certain payload values, a record with certain fields. match compares a value against a list of patterns and runs the first one that fits, and is tests a value against one pattern. Patterns can also bind names to the parts of the value they look at."}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "The match statement", "url": "pattern-matching.html#the-match-statement", "text": "match takes a value and a block of arms. Each arm is a pattern, =>, and a statement. The arms are tried from top to bottom, and the first one whose pattern fits runs: An arm's statement can be a block. Arms go on separate lines, or on one line separated by commas, but not both: An else arm runs when no other arm fits. It must be the last arm, and a match has at most one. A match statement doesn't have to handle every value: when no arm fits and there is no else, it does nothing. The compiler rejects arms that can never run: an arm after else, a second else, or a value that an earlier arm already matched:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Match expressions", "url": "pattern-matching.html#match-expressions", "text": "A match can also give a value. Each arm is then a pattern, =>, and an expression, and the match's value is the value of the arm that fits. A match expression works anywhere an expression does, including as a function's expression body: The arms must agree on a type. When the match is used where a type is expected, as in a typed declaration or an argument, the arms are checked against that type; otherwise each arm's value has to convert to a common type, and a literal takes the type of the others: An arm can leave the function or loop instead of giving a value, since ret, break and cont are expressions: A match expression can be followed by an index, a member access or a call, which apply to its value:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Covering every value", "url": "pattern-matching.html#covering-every-value", "text": "A match expression must handle every value its input can have, since it has to give a value either way. The compiler counts enum cases, the values of bool, integer types and char, and record fields. For other types, such as floats, only an else arm covers the rest, and an else arm always does: The last match misses 100. Ranges and relational patterns that together cover an integer type's whole range need no else: A match statement in which every arm returns counts as returning when it covers every value, so the function needs no ret after it:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Value patterns", "url": "pattern-matching.html#value-patterns", "text": "Any expression can be a pattern. A value pattern fits when the input is == to it, so it works for any type that has ==: literals, constants, variables and parameters are all values: A name in a pattern is always a value, never a new variable: low compares with the parameter low. Binding a new name takes a keyword, as shown in Bindings. A record with its own == operator can be matched by value too. Operators shows how to declare one. A type without == can't: Two constant patterns with the same value in one match are an error, whichever way they're written:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Ranges", "url": "pattern-matching.html#ranges", "text": "a..b fits values from a up to but not including b, and a..=b includes b. Ranges work for any type with < and <=, such as integers and char:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Relational patterns", "url": "pattern-matching.html#relational-patterns", "text": "A comparison operator followed by a value fits the values that compare that way: < x, <= x, > x, >= x and != x. Since the first fitting arm wins, the order of arms matters:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Several patterns in one arm", "url": "pattern-matching.html#several-patterns-in-one-arm", "text": "An arm can list several patterns separated by commas. It fits when any of them does:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Enum cases", "url": "pattern-matching.html#enum-cases", "text": "A case name is a pattern that fits values of that case. It can be bare or qualified with its type. For a case with a payload, each payload value gets a pattern of its own in parentheses, in order: Any pattern can go inside a payload, including another case pattern, so nested enums are matched in one step: A case pattern must give one pattern per payload value, and it has to be a case of the input's enum:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Bindings", "url": "pattern-matching.html#bindings", "text": "A binding gives a name to a part of the value. It starts with a keyword: val name reads the part. A part that can be copied is copied. Any other part is moved out when the match takes its input, and borrowed read-only when it only looks at it; see Moving parts out. var name makes a copy that the arm can change. The change doesn't reach the matched value. When the match takes its input, it takes the part instead of copying it. mut name borrows the part mutably, so changing it changes the matched value. mut needs a value that can be changed, and a val binding can't be assigned: A pattern followed by a binding tests and binds at once: 1..10 val n fits values from 1 to 9 and names the value n: Bindings go inside parentheses, in a payload or a record pattern. Apart from a type test, a pattern can't bind at the top of an arm. A name can be bound only once per pattern, and an arm with several patterns can't bind names:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Ignoring values with _", "url": "pattern-matching.html#ignoring-values-with", "text": "_ fits any value and binds nothing. It stands in for payload values and fields an arm doesn't care about:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "The is operator", "url": "pattern-matching.html#the-is-operator", "text": "value is pattern is a bool expression that is true when the pattern fits. It accepts every pattern a match arm does: Bindings made by is can be used wherever the test is known to have passed: on the right of &&, and in the body of the if or while it controls. They can't be used in an else or after ||. The loop here follows a chain of pointers; @ takes an address and * reads through it, as Pointers and raw memory explains:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Guards", "url": "pattern-matching.html#guards", "text": "An arm can add a condition with if after its pattern. The arm runs only when the pattern fits and the condition is true; otherwise the next arms are tried. The condition can use the pattern's bindings: An arm with a guard doesn't count toward covering every value, since its condition can be false. It can't move values out of the input either:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Record destructuring", "url": "pattern-matching.html#record-destructuring", "text": "A record pattern is the record's type name with fields in parentheses. Each field is named, followed by a pattern for its value; fields that aren't mentioned aren't checked. val x alone binds field x under its own name, and field: val name binds it under another: Record patterns nest inside each other and inside payloads, and a binding can follow a whole record pattern to name the record as well as its parts: Parentheses with no field names and no bindings, such as Point(1, 1), are a value pattern compared with ==, as shown in Value patterns. Enum payloads are always positional, so payload values can't be named. The compiler checks the fields and the type: A record pattern that leaves fields unchecked doesn't cover every value, so a match expression over records usually needs a final arm that binds or ignores every field, or an else:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Type tests", "url": "pattern-matching.html#type-tests", "text": "When the input is a trait object, a dyn value, a pattern can test which type it holds and bind it: Circle c fits when the value is a Circle. These patterns are covered in Trait objects. On any other value, a type name alone isn't a pattern:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "match mut", "url": "pattern-matching.html#match-mut", "text": "By default, a match takes its input or looks at it, as Moving parts out explains. match mut borrows the input mutably instead, so every binding, val ones included, refers to the part inside the input, and assigning to a binding changes the input. is takes the same mut: A var binding under match mut is a copy, as it is elsewhere. The input of match mut must be something that can be borrowed mutably: a var, a mut parameter, a field or element of one, or a place reached through a pointer:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Moving parts out", "url": "pattern-matching.html#moving-parts-out", "text": "A match takes its input the way a declaration does. When the input is a value the function owns, such as a local variable, a parameter taken by value or a temporary, val and var bindings take the parts they bind: a part that can be copied is copied, and one that can't is moved out, which moves the whole input into the match. Parts that aren't bound are dropped at the end of the arm. Ownership and destructors explains moves and destructors. After such a match, the input has been moved and can't be used. To look at a value without taking it, match imm of it, as in match imm slot; its bindings then borrow the parts. A value the function doesn't own, such as an imm or mut parameter, is only looked at the same way, so its bindings can't be moved on: match imm keeps the input for later: A temporary, such as the result of a call, belongs to the match the same way: Record patterns work the same way: a val binding moves its field out of a record the match takes, and the fields that aren't bound are dropped at the end of the arm. A record with its own destructor can't have fields moved out of it, so bindings of its fields borrow them:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "when arms", "url": "pattern-matching.html#when-arms", "text": "A when block inside a match holds arms that exist only in builds where a flag is on, with an optional else block for the other builds. Conditional compilation covers flags."}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Float patterns", "url": "pattern-matching.html#float-patterns", "text": "Floats can be matched like integers. Ranges and relational patterns are the usual way: A float value pattern, such as 0.0 or != 2.0, compares exactly, which rounding makes unreliable. The compiler accepts it with the warning \"Float patterns compare exactly\". Patterns never cover every float, so a match expression over floats needs an else:"}, {"page": "Pattern matching", "title": "Order of evaluation", "url": "pattern-matching.html#order-of-evaluation", "text": "The input of a match is evaluated once, before any arm. Arms are then tried from top to bottom, and a pattern's values and guard are evaluated only when that arm is tried:"}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Unions", "url": "unions.html", "text": "A union stores several members in the same memory. Writing one member and reading another reads the same bytes as a different type, which is how C programs look at the bits of a float or describe data that can take one of several layouts. Unions work like C's unions and have the same layout, so they can be shared with C code."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Declaring a union", "url": "unions.html#declaring-a-union", "text": "A union is declared like a record, with union in place of rec. T() creates a union with every byte zero, as does declaring a variable without a value: Writing bits.float stores the four bytes of the float, and reading bits.raw gives those same bytes as an integer. Nothing records which member was written last; every member can be read at any time."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Creating a union from a member", "url": "unions.html#creating-a-union-from-a-member", "text": "T(value) with one argument creates a union with the member whose type is exactly the type of value set to it. Bytes the member doesn't cover are zero: The member is picked by type alone, so the argument's type must be known. An untyped literal like 1 has none and needs a suffix, as in 1u32. A union with two members of the argument's type can't pick one either: The first error's note lists what Bits accepts: \"'Bits' takes no arguments, or one value of a field's type: f32, u32\"."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Members share one address", "url": "unions.html#members-share-one-address", "text": "Every member of a union starts at offset 0, so they all have the same address: A member smaller than the union covers only its first bytes. On the 64-bit Windows and Linux targets, the lowest byte of an integer comes first in memory, which is why bytes[0] is 0x44."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Reading and writing members", "url": "unions.html#reading-and-writing-members", "text": "Members are read and written with ., like fields, and so are the parts of a member that is a record or an array. A union can be a field of a record, and a record can be a member of a union: mouse.x and keyboard.key start at the same offset, so mouse.x reads the key code in its low bytes. Reading a member whose type allows only some bit patterns makes the value valid first. A bool read from a union is true when its byte is not zero: A char read from a union is checked: bytes that aren't a valid character panic."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Sizes", "url": "unions.html#sizes", "text": "A union is as large as its largest member, rounded up to a multiple of its alignment, and its alignment is the largest alignment of its members:"}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Constructors, methods and properties", "url": "unions.html#constructors-methods-and-properties", "text": "A union can declare constructors, methods and properties, as a record does; Records and Methods and properties cover them. A constructor sets the members it wants through self. Declaring a constructor removes T() and T(value), the same as for records. Properties can give names to the bits of an integer member: A union can't declare a destructor or req members and can't be a ref union:"}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "What a union can hold", "url": "unions.html#what-a-union-can-hold", "text": "A union holds only plain data: integers, floats, bool, char, pointers, cstr, ext enum values, and arrays and records made of them. A member can't have a destructor, hold a borrow, or be a type with no all-zero value such as a function value. Plain enums can't be members either, and neither can records with fields that the union's module can't see or write, such as pvt and pvt set fields: All members of a union share one visibility. A member can't be less visible than the others, and a member can't restrict who may write it:"}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Unions in constants", "url": "unions.html#unions-in-constants", "text": "Reading a member of a union built from a constant is a constant too, so constants can be written as raw bits. The bits are kept exactly, including those of infinities and NaNs: A constant can read a member whose type is an integer, a float, bool, char or an array of them. A bool read this way must be 0 or 1, and a char must be a valid character; otherwise the value isn't a constant. A constant can also hold a whole union, as in infinity: const F32Bits = F32Bits(0x7F800000u32)."}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Generic unions", "url": "unions.html#generic-unions", "text": "A union can take type parameters. A type parameter used in a member must have the ext and default constraints, since a union holds only plain data that is valid when zeroed (see What a union can hold). One generic union can then reinterpret the bits of any such type as another: When the two types have different sizes, the union is as large as the larger one: a smaller From leaves the rest of to zero, and a smaller To reads only the first bytes of from. Without the constraints, the member is rejected:"}, {"page": "Unions", "title": "Using unions with C", "url": "unions.html#using-unions-with-c", "text": "A union has the layout of the C union with the same members in the same order, so a union or a record that contains one can be passed to C functions, by value or through a pointer, and C can fill it in. Here C's memset writes the bytes of a union through its address: The record-with-a-union shape from Reading and writing members, a tag field followed by a union, is how C APIs describe a value that can take one of several forms. In Cella's own code, an enum with payloads does the same job and checks which case a value is. Calling C covers declaring C functions and the types that can cross into C."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Ownership and destructors", "url": "ownership.html", "text": "Cella has no garbage collector, and it doesn't make you free everything by hand either. Every value has exactly one owner, and when the owner goes away, so does the value. A type can declare a destructor, code that runs when one of its values goes away, so a record that holds memory or a handle can release it at the right moment without any call in the code that uses it. This chapter explains who owns a value, when destructors run, and how a value moves from one owner to another."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "One owner per value", "url": "ownership.html#one-owner-per-value", "text": "A local variable owns the value stored in it. When the variable's scope ends, the value is destroyed. For a number that means nothing happens, but a record can say what destroying it does with a destructor, written drop: op in its body: b belongs to the inner block, so it's destroyed when that block ends. a lives until the end of main. No code calls the destructor; the compiler inserts each call where the owner's scope ends. Most examples in this chapter use this File record. Later examples hide its declaration to stay short, but it's always the same: one id field and a destructor that prints close and the id."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Destructors", "url": "ownership.html#destructors", "text": "A destructor is a member named drop, declared as drop: op with a body. It takes no parameters and returns nothing. Inside it, self is the value being destroyed, and the destructor can read and change its fields and pass it to other functions. The usual job of a destructor is to give back something the value holds. This Buffer gets memory from C's malloc in its constructor and returns it with free: A destructor can't take parameters: It can't replace self or move it away, since the value is being destroyed and has nowhere to go: Only records declare destructors, and only in the record's own body: enums, unions and impl blocks can't (\"Cannot declare destructors in enums\"). A constant can't hold a value whose type has a destructor (\"Cannot store values with destructors in constants\")."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "When destructors run", "url": "ownership.html#when-destructors-run", "text": ""}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "At the end of a scope", "url": "ownership.html#at-the-end-of-a-scope", "text": "The values a scope owns are destroyed when it ends, in the reverse of the order they were declared. The last one created is the first one destroyed. A scope can end in other ways than reaching its closing brace. ret, break and cont destroy every value owned by the scopes they leave, at the point where they jump: Each pass through the loop body is a scope of its own, so File(0) is closed at the end of the first pass. The ret in the second pass closes File(1) and then log."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Fields, elements and payloads", "url": "ownership.html#fields-elements-and-payloads", "text": "A value that contains other values destroys them along with itself. A record runs its own destructor first, while its fields are still intact, and then destroys its fields in the reverse of their declared order. An array destroys its elements from the last to the first, and an enum destroys the payload of the case it holds: A record doesn't need a destructor of its own for this. A record without drop: op whose fields have destructors still destroys those fields when it's destroyed; it's declared own rec because of them."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Module variables", "url": "ownership.html#module-variables", "text": "A module variable is owned by the program. Module variables are destroyed after main returns, in the reverse of the order they were declared:"}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Copying and moving", "url": "ownership.html#copying-and-moving", "text": "Some types copy and others move. A copy makes a second, independent value, and both the old and the new one keep working. A move hands the one value over to a new owner, and the old place can't be used anymore. These types copy: Numbers, bool and char. str, cstr and pointers (ptr[T]). Copying a pointer copies the address, not what it points to. Function values with a plain function type, such as fun[i32 -> i32]. Records, arrays and enums whose fields, elements or payloads all copy, unless they're declared own. Everything else moves: the records and enums declared own rec and own enum, and any array or generic record that holds one of them. A record of plain data copies, so changing the copy leaves the original alone: A value with a destructor can't be copied, since two copies would both run the destructor and, for the Buffer above, free the same memory twice. Cella moves it instead."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Owning types", "url": "ownership.html#owning-types", "text": "A type whose values must move says so in its declaration. A record with a destructor is declared own rec, like File, and so is a record or enum that holds an owning value, since destroying it destroys that value too. Leaving out own is an error: An own rec doesn't need a destructor. Its values still move instead of copying, which suits a value that stands for something unique, like a ticket that must be used once. A generic record whose fields have its type parameters' types needs no own; each use follows its type arguments. Pair[A, B] with fields of types A and B copies as Pair[i32, i32] and moves as Pair[File, File]. A field whose type owns whatever the arguments are, such as an Option[File] or a field of another own type, still needs own. A record that both holds borrows and owns a value is declared own ref rec (Borrows)."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Moving values", "url": "ownership.html#moving-values", "text": "A value that can't be copied moves wherever it's taken by value: into a parameter without a mode, into a constructor or an enum payload, into an element of an array literal, into the left side of an assignment, or into a new variable. Nothing extra is written at the move: archive owns the file once it's called, so the file is closed when archive returns, not at the end of main. main no longer owns it, so it doesn't close it a second time. Constructors, assignments and declarations move the same way: current = next destroys File(3), the value current held, before the new one is stored; assignment is covered below. A declaration moves only out of a place the function owns: a local variable, a parameter taken by value, or a field or element of one. Anywhere else, such as an imm or mut parameter or a value reached through a borrow, val view = report borrows the value instead, which Borrows explains. To borrow a value the function does own, write val view = imm report. The keyword own marks a move only where a plain use wouldn't move: taking a value out of a mut parameter (Parameter modes), out of memory behind a pointer, and out of a field of self in a method that consumes a value with a destructor (Methods)."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Using a moved value", "url": "ownership.html#using-a-moved-value", "text": "After a move, the old place holds nothing, and using it is an error. The message points to the move: A moved variable declared with var can be given a new value, and is usable again from there on: A type that copies is copied in all these places, and the source stays usable."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "What can't be moved", "url": "ownership.html#what-cant-be-moved", "text": "A module variable can't be moved out of, since the program owns it until it exits:"}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Returning values", "url": "ownership.html#returning-values", "text": "ret moves the value it returns to the caller. A function can create a value and hand it out: The file isn't closed when open returns, because open no longer owns it. Writing ret own file is accepted, but the compiler points out the own with the hint \"Redundant 'own'\", as it does for own anywhere a value would move anyway. Returning a field moves just that field. The rest of the value is destroyed when the function ends:"}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Temporaries", "url": "ownership.html#temporaries", "text": "A value that isn't stored in a variable is a temporary: the result of a call used as an argument, a record built inline, or a call whose field is read, as in open(1).id. A temporary is destroyed at the end of the statement that made it, after everything else in the statement has run. Several temporaries in one statement are destroyed in the reverse of the order they were made: A call whose result is thrown away, like open(3) on its own line, destroys the result right away. A temporary passed to a parameter that takes it by value isn't destroyed at the end of the statement. It moves into the function, which then owns it. A temporary in the condition of an if, while or do ... while is destroyed after the condition is checked, before the body runs. When an is pattern in the condition binds part of the temporary, it lives until the end of the body instead, so the binding stays valid:"}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Assignment destroys the old value", "url": "ownership.html#assignment-destroys-the-old-value", "text": "Assigning to a place that already holds a value destroys the old value. The right side is computed first, then the old value is destroyed, then the new one is stored, so the right side can still read the old value: The same holds for fields, array elements and places reached through a mut parameter (see Parameter modes). A place that holds nothing yet, such as a moved variable, has nothing to destroy, and the assignment only stores."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Destroying a value early", "url": "ownership.html#destroying-a-value-early", "text": "drop destroys a value only through a pointer, as in drop *p (see Pointers). Variables are always destroyed by the compiler when their owner lets go of them, so drop on a variable is an error: To destroy a variable's value before the end of its scope, put the variable in a block of its own, assign it a new value, or move it into a function that takes it by value."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Values moved on only some paths", "url": "ownership.html#values-moved-on-only-some-paths", "text": "A value can be moved on one path through a function and kept on another. The program then checks at run time whether to destroy it: if the move happened, the new owner destroys it, and if not, the scope's end does. After the if, file might be moved, so the code after it can't use it. The error lists every move that might have happened: In a loop, a move in one iteration leaves the value moved for the next one: The message adds \"Moved here in an earlier iteration\". Giving the variable a new value before the next iteration fixes it:"}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Moving fields out", "url": "ownership.html#moving-fields-out", "text": "A field can be moved out of a record on its own. The record is then partly moved: its other fields are still there and are destroyed as usual, but the record as a whole can't be used until the field is filled again: show takes the pair with imm, which lends it to the function without moving it (Parameter modes covers this). Using the whole record while a field is missing is an error: A field can't be moved out of a record that has its own destructor, because the destructor would then run on a record with a hole in it: Array elements can be moved out the same way when the index is a constant. Moving one out at an index computed at run time is an error (Arrays covers this)."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Destructors that move fields", "url": "ownership.html#destructors-that-move-fields", "text": "The one place where fields can be moved out of a record with a destructor is that destructor. The destructor can hand a field to another owner, and the fields it moves are skipped when the record's fields are destroyed afterwards: The destructor may also put a new value into a field it moved; that value is then destroyed with the other fields. After moving a field, the destructor can't use the field, or self as a whole, until it's filled again (\"Cannot use moved values\", \"Cannot use partly moved values\")."}, {"page": "Ownership and destructors", "title": "Definite initialization", "url": "ownership.html#definite-initialization", "text": "A variable must hold a value before it's read. The compiler follows every path through the function, and reading a variable that might not have been given a value is an error: A val declared without a value can be given one later, once on each path: A record can also be filled one field at a time. It counts as a whole value once every field is set; before that, the fields that are set can be used on their own, and the record as a whole can't: The destructors follow the same tracking. Only what was given a value is destroyed, so a record left half filled when its scope ends destroys just the fields that were set: Variables covers declarations without a value and undef in general. The rules for parameters, including what a function must do with a mut parameter it moves out of, are in Parameter modes."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Parameter modes", "url": "parameter-modes.html", "text": "A parameter's mode says how an argument gets into a function. There are three: no mode gives the function the value itself, imm lends it the caller's value to read, and mut lends it the caller's value to change. The mode is part of the function's signature, and the call site writes the borrowing modes too, so a call shows which arguments are lent and which can be changed. | none | the value: a copy, or the value itself moved in | f(x) | yes, its own value | any | | imm | the caller's value, read-only | f(imm x) | no | any | | mut | the caller's value | f(mut x) | yes, and the caller sees it | any | Examples that need a value that can't be copied use the File record from Ownership, with its declaration hidden: one id field and a destructor that prints close and the id."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Taking a value: no mode", "url": "parameter-modes.html#taking-a-value-no-mode", "text": "A parameter without a mode takes its argument by value. For a type that copies, the function gets its own copy, and nothing it does affects the caller: The parameter belongs to the function, so countDown can change n while the caller's start stays 3. A type that can't be copied moves into the function instead. The function owns the value from then on: it can change it, move it somewhere else, or let it be destroyed when the function returns, and the caller can't use it afterwards: Neither call writes anything extra: the parameter already says the value is taken. Ownership has the rules for moves and the errors for using a moved value."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Borrowing with imm", "url": "parameter-modes.html#borrowing-with-imm", "text": "An imm parameter borrows the argument read-only. The function reads the caller's own value, not a copy of it. The call site marks a named argument with imm: main still owns file after the calls, so it can lend it again, and the file is closed once, at the end of main. A temporary like File(2) needs no imm; it's lent for the call and destroyed at the end of the statement. imm works with every type, including types that copy. An imm i32 parameter reads the caller's variable instead of a copy of it. Leaving out imm on a named argument is an error, so a borrow always shows at the call: Writing imm on an argument whose parameter isn't imm is an error too, as it is for mut: the message names the parameter, as in \"'p' isn't an 'imm' parameter\". The function can't change an imm parameter or move it away, since the value belongs to the caller:"}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Changing the caller's value with mut", "url": "parameter-modes.html#changing-the-callers-value-with-mut", "text": "A mut parameter borrows the argument so the function can change it. Every change goes straight to the caller's value. The call site writes mut: Any place that can be assigned can be passed to a mut parameter: a variable, a field, an array element, a value behind a pointer (mut *p), or another mut parameter, as shift does with p.y. The caller's value must be changeable. These are errors: A val can't be changed, and a literal or the result of a call isn't stored anywhere the change could land. The argument must also have exactly the parameter's type: a value that would only convert to it can't be lent, since the function writes to it as the parameter's type."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Replacing a mut parameter's value", "url": "parameter-modes.html#replacing-a-mut-parameters-value", "text": "Assigning to a mut parameter replaces the caller's value. As with any assignment, the old value is destroyed first: A function may move a value out of a mut parameter, but the caller expects to get a value back, so the function must put one there again before it returns, on every path. A declaration like val current = a only looks at a mut parameter, so taking the value out is written own a. This swap moves both values around and leaves each parameter filled: Leaving the parameter moved is an error: The same holds for a field moved out of a mut parameter, which gives \"Cannot leave 'mut' parameters partly moved\"."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Receivers", "url": "parameter-modes.html#receivers", "text": "A method's receiver, self, takes one of the same three modes: self for the value itself, imm self and mut self. The call x.m() doesn't repeat the mode; the method's declaration shows it. Methods covers methods in full; the mode rules are the same as for parameters: account.finish() moves account into the method, and account can't be used after it. For a type that copies, a self method gets a copy instead. A mut self method needs a receiver that can be changed, so calling one on a val or on a temporary is an error (\"Cannot mutably borrow values\", \"Cannot mutably borrow unstored values\")."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Modes in function types", "url": "parameter-modes.html#modes-in-function-types", "text": "A function type lists each parameter's mode along with its type: fun[mut i32, i32] takes an i32 to change and an i32 by value. A call through a function value writes the modes as a direct call does: A function converts to a function type only when the modes match: There is one exception: a function that takes a parameter by value can be used where the type says imm, as long as the parameter's type copies. The other direction is an error. Function values and closures covers function types in full."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Overloads and modes", "url": "parameter-modes.html#overloads-and-modes", "text": "Overloads can differ only in their parameter modes. The call's marks pick one: f(x) calls the overload that takes the value, f(imm x) the one that borrows it, and f(mut x) the one that changes it. A temporary has no mark and goes to the overload that takes the value: A function value picks an overload by the modes of the type it's given, as in val look: fun[imm File] = describe; without a type, naming an overloaded function this way is ambiguous. Two overloads that a call could reach the same way are an error. Here a call with two temporaries would take the first argument by value under one overload and the second under the other, so neither is a better match: Receivers and operator operands carry no marks at the call, so overloads can't differ only in the mode of self or of an operand: When a trait declares a member twice with different modes, a type implementing it must declare both, with exactly those modes."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Aliasing isn't checked", "url": "parameter-modes.html#aliasing-isnt-checked", "text": "The same variable can be passed to several parameters of one call, in any mix of modes. The compiler doesn't check this, and the function sees one value through each borrow. A change made through one is visible through the others at once, while a copy keeps the value from the time of the call: A function that takes two mut parameters can't assume they're different values. Moves are still checked: a value can't be lent and moved in the same call."}, {"page": "Parameter modes", "title": "Evaluation order", "url": "parameter-modes.html#evaluation-order", "text": "Arguments are evaluated from left to right. An argument without a mode is read when its turn comes. A mut or imm argument runs its own expressions in that order, such as the index in values[pick()], but the place itself is reached only when the call starts, after all the other arguments. A borrow is never held while other arguments run, so an argument that replaces the borrowed value doesn't leave the borrow pointing at the old one: The copy reads values[1] before reset runs and sees 2. The mut argument evaluates pick() first too, but reaches values[1] only after reset has replaced the array, so add changes the new array's element."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Borrows", "url": "borrows.html", "text": "A borrow refers to a value that is stored somewhere else. Parameter modes showed borrows that last for one call: an imm or mut parameter borrows the caller's variable until the function returns. This chapter covers borrows as values of their own, kept in local variables, returned from functions and stored in records. The compiler checks one rule for all of them: a borrow can't be used after the value it borrows is gone."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "What a borrow is", "url": "borrows.html#what-a-borrow-is", "text": "In C, a function that wants to change a caller's variable takes its address, and code that wants a second name for a variable keeps a pointer to it: A Cella borrow does the same job. mut score borrows score, and the borrow can be kept in a variable: Underneath, a borrow is an address, the same size as a pointer. It differs from a C pointer in three ways: A borrow always refers to a value. There is no null borrow. A borrow is used as if it were the value. points += 5 changes score, with no * to write. The compiler knows which variable each borrow came from, and rejects any use of the borrow after that variable's value is moved or destroyed. A borrow never owns what it refers to. When a borrow goes out of scope, nothing happens to the value it borrowed."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Making a borrow", "url": "borrows.html#making-a-borrow", "text": "imm place makes a read-only borrow and mut place a writable one. The place can be a local variable, a parameter, a field, an array element, a module variable or a value behind a pointer: view borrows all of point, so it sees the change made through x. A read-only borrow can't be written through, and can't be turned into a writable one. A val can't be borrowed with mut, and only a stored value can be borrowed: a call's result or a literal has no place to refer to."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Using a borrow", "url": "borrows.html#using-a-borrow", "text": "A borrow reads as the value it refers to. Arithmetic, comparisons and . work on it directly, and passing it to a parameter with no mode passes a copy of the value. To lend it on to a mut or imm parameter, write the mode at the call, as for a variable: val before = counter doesn't borrow again. i32 is plain data, so the declaration copies the value counter refers to at that moment, and the later += 10 doesn't change it. To make a second borrow of the same place, write imm counter or mut counter."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Borrow types", "url": "borrows.html#borrow-types", "text": "The type of imm x is imm[T] and the type of mut x is mut[T], where T is the type of x. They can be written in declarations, results, fields, arrays and type arguments. In a declaration with an imm[T] type, the imm on the value can be left out; a mut[T] declaration needs its mut written: A mut[T] converts to an imm[T], never the other way around. A borrow doesn't convert to its value's type either: val n: i32 = imm a is an error, while val n: i32 = reader copies the value. Parameters never have borrow types. A parameter borrows through its mode instead, and the compiler suggests the mode to write: The first error has the hint \"Did you mean 'mut counter: i32'?\", and the second \"Did you mean 'imm value: i32'?\". A borrow has no default, since it can't be null. A borrow variable declared without a value must be assigned before it's used, and a record with a borrow field has no T():"}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Assigning to a borrow", "url": "borrows.html#assigning-to-a-borrow", "text": "What an assignment does to a borrow depends on what is assigned. Assigning a borrow repoints the variable at a new place. Assigning a value to a mut borrow writes through it, as any other use does: A read-only borrow can't be written through, so assigning a place to an imm[T] variable always repoints it, and reader = b borrows b. Assigning it something that isn't stored, like reader = 5, is \"Cannot borrow unstored values\". A borrow declared with val can't be repointed:"}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Borrowing declarations", "url": "borrows.html#borrowing-declarations", "text": "Plain data copies, so val b = a makes a copy when a is an i32 or a record of numbers. A value whose type moves instead, such as a record with a destructor, can't be copied (see Ownership and destructors), so val b = a moves a into b. To borrow it instead, write val b = imm a: The texture is freed once, when moved goes out of scope. same never owned it. A declaration moves only out of a place the function owns. A value it can't take, such as one behind an imm or mut parameter, a loop variable or a value reached through another borrow, is borrowed instead, so val b = a there makes b an imm borrow. Writing imm in such a declaration changes nothing, and the compiler gives the hint \"Redundant 'imm'\". The same rule applies wherever a value is bound: elements in for loops (see Arrays), val bindings in patterns (see Pattern matching), and values reached through * and [] operators (see Operators). A borrowing declaration is a read-only borrow, so it can't be moved out of or written through, even when it's declared with var: var on a borrow only allows repointing it."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "A borrow can't outlive its value", "url": "borrows.html#a-borrow-cant-outlive-its-value", "text": "The compiler follows every borrow from where it's made to each place it's used. A use is an error when, on some path to it, the value the borrow came from has been: moved, by passing it to a parameter that takes it by value or by any other move; dropped at the end of its scope, including a scope inside a loop that ended in an earlier pass; reassigned, when its type has a destructor, since that drops the old value. Each error has a note with the line where the value was moved, dropped or reassigned. In the loop, the note says the value was dropped \"in an earlier iteration\". Only uses are checked. A borrow whose value is gone is fine as long as nothing reads it again, and repointing it at a live value makes it usable again: The check follows fields and elements separately. A borrow of one field stays valid when another field is moved out: Assigning a new value to plain data runs no destructor, so it doesn't end borrows of it. The borrow sees whatever the variable holds when it's read:"}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Returning borrows", "url": "borrows.html#returning-borrows", "text": "A function can return a borrow. The returned borrow must come from one of these: an imm or mut parameter, directly or through a call the parameter was lent to; a borrow that a parameter holds, such as a field of a ref rec parameter; a module variable or value. The caller then treats the result as a borrow of the arguments it lent: A call that returns a mut[T] is a place: it can be assigned to, as in kills(mut bat) += 2, or borrowed again with mut. Every other source is an error, since the value would be gone once the function returns. A parameter with no mode is the function's own value, a copy or a value moved in for the function to destroy, so it doesn't outlive the call: In a method, a returned borrow may come only from self: The caller's side follows the same rules as any other borrow. Here leader borrows referee, so replacing referee ends it: Module values live for the whole program, so a function can return a borrow of one:"}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Records that hold borrows", "url": "borrows.html#records-that-hold-borrows", "text": "A record with a field that holds a borrow is declared ref rec, and an enum with such a payload ref enum. Types that hold borrows are the borrow types, str (it borrows its bytes; see Bool, char and strings), ref records and enums, arrays of any of these, and closures kept as ref fun (see Function values and closures). A mut field takes its argument with mut, as Tally(mut kills, 5) does. An imm field takes a place with or without imm. A plain rec or enum can't hold borrows, and the compiler suggests the fix. Unions can't hold borrows at all. The hints read \"Did you mean 'Label: ref rec'?\" and \"Did you mean 'Target: ref enum'?\". A ref value borrows whatever its fields borrow, so it follows the rule of the previous sections: it can't be used after those values are gone. A ref rec can have a destructor, and the destructor can use the borrows. This makes a guard that undoes something when its scope ends: The destructor counts as a use, so the value a Scope borrows must still be there when the scope's destructor runs. Building a ref value from a temporary is an error, since the temporary is gone at the end of the statement: The first error points at the closing brace of early, with the notes \"'scope' is dropped here\" and the line where depth was moved."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "View records", "url": "borrows.html#view-records", "text": "A ref rec or ref enum whose fields can all be copied is a view record. A view record is a window onto something else: borrowing from it means borrowing what it borrows, not the record. The difference shows in a method that hands out borrows. This lexer borrows an array, and each token it returns borrows an element of that array: next takes mut self, so its token could have borrowed the lexer. Since Lexer is a view record, the token borrows items instead, and kept stays valid after inner is gone. Several tokens can be held at once, and a view record can be copied freely; every copy borrows the same values. The same rule lets a function return a borrow that a view record parameter holds, even when the parameter is a copy: The other side of this rule is that a view record can't lend out its own fields. A method returning a borrow of self or of one of its fields is an error: A ref rec with a field that can't be copied, such as a field whose type has a destructor, isn't a view record. Borrows taken from it borrow the record itself, so its methods can return borrows of its fields:"}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Storing borrows", "url": "borrows.html#storing-borrows", "text": "A local variable can hold any borrow, under the rules above. Storing a borrow where the caller can see it, in a mut parameter or a constructor's self, is limited, so that the caller's borrows stay valid after the call. A mut parameter can receive only borrows that came from that same parameter. A function can rearrange what its argument borrows, but can't make it borrow something new: Storing a borrow from anywhere else, including another parameter, is an error, and so is storing through a borrow that a parameter holds: A constructor's self is checked like a result. It can store borrows of imm and mut parameters, and borrows that its parameters hold, like a str parameter's bytes. Borrows of locals and by-value parameters would end when the constructor returns: A destructor can't store new borrows in self either: \"Cannot store new borrows in 'self'\"."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "A value can't borrow itself", "url": "borrows.html#a-value-cant-borrow-itself", "text": "A value can't hold a borrow of its own storage. Moving the value would move the borrow along with it, still pointing at the old place: The same holds in constructors (self.alias = mut self.value), between elements of one array, and through payloads. Structures that point into themselves, like a circular list, use pointers, which the compiler doesn't check."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Module variables can't hold borrows", "url": "borrows.html#module-variables-cant-hold-borrows", "text": "A module variable or static field declared with var can't hold a borrow, since it outlives every local. A module val can hold one only when its value is a constant, like a string literal: Borrows of module variables can be made, kept in locals and returned, as maxLevel showed above. They follow the same rules: reassigning a module variable whose type has a destructor ends the borrows of it."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "Borrows in closures", "url": "borrows.html#borrows-in-closures", "text": "A lambda that uses a local variable captures it by reference, which makes the closure a borrow of that variable. The rules in this chapter apply to it: calling a closure after a variable it captured is moved or out of scope is an error. Function values and closures covers the details."}, {"page": "Borrows", "title": "What isn't checked", "url": "borrows.html#what-isnt-checked", "text": "The compiler checks that a borrow never outlives the value it borrows. It doesn't check how borrows overlap. Several mut borrows of one variable can exist at once, and the variable can be written while an imm borrow of it is held: The same goes for calls: one variable can be passed to two mut parameters (see Parameter modes). An imm borrow promises only that code using it doesn't write through it. The value can still change through other names. Also unchecked, as in C: Invalidation. A borrow of memory reached through a pointer, such as an element of a heap buffer, isn't tied to that memory. Freeing or reallocating it leaves the borrow pointing at freed memory. See Pointers and raw memory. Data races. Nothing stops two threads from using borrows of the same value at once. See Atomics."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Pointers and raw memory", "url": "pointers.html", "text": "ptr[T] is a raw pointer to a T, like T * in C. Pointers are for the places borrows can't go: memory from an allocator, structures that point into themselves, and data shared with C. The compiler doesn't check what a pointer points to. A pointer can be null, can outlive its target, and can be freed twice, and what happens then is undefined, as in C."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Addresses and dereferencing", "url": "pointers.html#addresses-and-dereferencing", "text": "@place gives the address of a place: a variable, a field, an array element, or a value behind another pointer. *p is the value p points to, which can be read, assigned or borrowed: The type of @score is ptr[i32]. A pointer is plain data: it copies, it can be stored in any record or module variable, and it has no destructor. Constants have no storage, so they have no address. Taking the address of a value that was moved away, or of a variable that hasn't been given a value, is an error too: [Filling undef storage](#filling-undef-storage) shows the one way to take the address of storage that has no value yet."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Members through pointers", "url": "pointers.html#members-through-pointers", "text": ". looks through one pointer, like C's ->. Fields, methods and properties of the target are reached directly, and they can be assigned, borrowed and have their address taken: Chains of pointers work the same way, one level per .: . looks through exactly one pointer. A ptr[ptr[Point]] needs a * first, as in (*pp).x:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Null", "url": "pointers.html#null", "text": "null is the pointer that points nowhere. It converts to every pointer type, and a pointer compares with it using == and !=. A pointer starts as null when its storage is zeroed, as in a var declared without a value or a record built with T(): Nothing stops a null pointer from being dereferenced. Reading or writing through one is undefined and usually crashes the program, as in C."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "The untyped pointer ptr", "url": "pointers.html#the-untyped-pointer-ptr", "text": "ptr without a type argument is an untyped pointer, like C's void *. Every ptr[T] converts to ptr implicitly, and ptr[T](raw) converts back. An untyped pointer has no target type, so it can't be dereferenced, indexed or used with .:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Pointer arithmetic", "url": "pointers.html#pointer-arithmetic", "text": "Adding an integer to a ptr[T] moves it by that many elements, as in C, and subtracting one moves it back. p[i] is *(p + i), and the index can be negative: The offset can be any integer type that converts to isize or usize without loss on every target: the integer types up to 32 bits, isize and usize. An i64 or u64 offset needs a conversion, as in p + isize(n). Subtracting two pointers of the same type gives the number of elements between them, as an isize: Arithmetic on the untyped ptr counts bytes. A difference between pointers to a type of size zero has no answer, so it's an error where the size is known and a panic in generic code where it turns out to be zero:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Comparing pointers", "url": "pointers.html#comparing-pointers", "text": "Pointers of the same type compare with ==, !=, <, <=, > and >= by address. The untyped ptr compares with any pointer using == and !=. Pointers to different types don't compare or subtract:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Converting pointers", "url": "pointers.html#converting-pointers", "text": "Conversions between pointer types are written out, except to ptr: | ptr[T] | ptr | implicit, or ptr(p) | | ptr | ptr[T] | ptr[T](raw) | | ptr[T] | ptr[U] | ptr[U](ptr(p)) | | ptr[T] | usize or isize | usize(p) | | usize or isize | ptr[T] | ptr[T](address) | | cstr | ptr[u8] | ptr[u8](text) | | ptr[u8] | cstr | cstr(bytes) | Going from one typed pointer to another directly is an error:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Allocating memory", "url": "pointers.html#allocating-memory", "text": "Cella has no allocator of its own. Programs call C's malloc and free, declared in an ext block (see Calling C), and size the request with sizeOf: squares converts to ptr implicitly when it's passed to free."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Values with destructors", "url": "pointers.html#values-with-destructors", "text": "Assigning through a pointer is an assignment like any other: *p = v drops the value that was there before storing the new one. Memory fresh from malloc holds no value yet, so dropping it would run a destructor on garbage. Four forms handle values with destructors in raw memory: own *p = v stores v without dropping anything first. own *p moves the value out, leaving the memory without one. drop *p runs the value's destructor in place. *p = v drops the old value, then stores v. The compiler doesn't track what the memory holds. After own *p or drop *p, reading *p again, or forgetting to destroy what's there before free, isn't an error. The same forms work on fields and elements reached through a pointer, as in own h.buffer = Buffer(4) and own items[0] = Buffer(5). own assignment only works through pointers, and never on compound assignments. Reading *p without own borrows the value, as a declaration does for any value the function doesn't own (see Borrows), so moving it needs own: A generic function can do the same with a ptr[T] for any T, as in drop *memory on a memory: ptr[T] parameter. drop *p on a pointer to a trait object is covered in Trait objects."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "Filling undef storage", "url": "pointers.html#filling-undef-storage", "text": "A variable declared = undef has storage but no value (see Variables and constants). Taking its address with @ doesn't count as reading it. Once its address has been taken, the compiler treats the storage as filled, so C functions with out-parameters, like memset, and loops through a pointer can fill it: The address of an element covers the whole array, and the address of a field covers only that field. Elements with destructors are stored with own, as in own first[i] = Buffer(i32(i)). Anything else that would read the storage before it's filled is still an error, including a mut borrow and a fill that happens on only some paths:"}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "sizeOf and alignOf", "url": "pointers.html#sizeof-and-alignof", "text": "sizeOf(X) is the size of a type or of an expression's type in bytes, and alignOf(X) its alignment. Both are compile-time constants of type usize, so they can size arrays and module constants, and they print with %zu: place rounds an offset up to the alignment of T, which is what an allocator other than malloc, such as an arena, needs before handing out memory for a T. Since they're usize, storing them in a narrower type needs a conversion, as in i32(sizeOf(Mixed)). How records are laid out is covered in Records."}, {"page": "Pointers and raw memory", "title": "What isn't checked", "url": "pointers.html#what-isnt-checked", "text": "The compiler doesn't follow pointers. This program compiles, and calling dangle returns the address of a variable that no longer exists: None of these are errors, and all of them are undefined at run time, as in C: dereferencing a null or dangling pointer; reading or writing past the end of an allocation, through * or []; freeing memory twice, or using it after free; writing through a pointer to storage that is read-only elsewhere; reinterpreting memory through a converted pointer. Pointers also take no part in ownership: a ptr[T] doesn't own its target, and nothing drops what it points to unless the code does it with drop *p or own *p. A borrow made through a pointer, like imm *p, counts as a borrow of a variable when p was taken from that variable with @ in the same function, as in val p = @b. A borrow through a pointer that came back from a call, or from an allocator, is tied to nothing, so it stays usable after the memory is freed. Structures that point into themselves, like circular lists, are built with pointers for this reason. A borrow could never point at its own record (see Borrows), but a pointer can: The list stays correct only while head, first and second stay where they are. Moving any of them would leave the others pointing at the old place, and the compiler wouldn't notice."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generics", "url": "generics.html", "text": "A generic declaration takes types as parameters. One generic function or record works for every type you give it, and the compiler builds a separate copy of it for each type it's used with. This chapter covers generic functions, records and enums, the constraints that say which types a parameter accepts, value parameters, which take compile-time integers instead of types, and defaults for both."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generic functions", "url": "generics.html#generic-functions", "text": "Type parameters go in brackets after the function's name. Inside the function, a type parameter is a type like any other: pick takes its values without a mode, so it gets them by value: a copy for a T that copies, and the value itself for one that has to move. Generic code that keeps using a T after passing it on needs every T to be copyable, which the type parameter says with T: copy (see Constraints). See Parameter modes for what imm and mut mean."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Type arguments", "url": "generics.html#type-arguments", "text": "A call can write its type arguments in brackets, as pick[cstr] does above. Usually it doesn't need to: the compiler infers them from the arguments, and when the arguments don't settle them, from the type the call's result is expected to have: When several arguments share a type parameter, the type they all convert to wins. A literal fits whatever type the other arguments give it, and two integer types widen to the larger one: Here wide is an i64 and mixed is an f64. When nothing settles a type parameter, or the arguments disagree, or the call writes the wrong number of type arguments, the call is an error:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "How generic bodies are checked", "url": "generics.html#how-generic-bodies-are-checked", "text": "A generic body is checked once, where it's declared, against what every possible T allows, so its errors show up there and not at the calls. Without constraints, that means generic code can move a T, borrow it, store it in fields and arrays, point to it and ask for sizeOf(T), but it can't call methods or operators on it: Calling add(2) with an i32 doesn't help, since the body has to work for every type. To use operators and methods on a T, give it a trait bound. Each type a generic is used with gets its own compiled copy, with its own layout and code. A generic function that calls itself with a bigger type each time would need endless copies, which is an error:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generic and plain overloads", "url": "generics.html#generic-and-plain-overloads", "text": "A generic function can share its name with plain functions and other generic ones. A call goes to the plain overload when the argument already has the type its parameter takes. Otherwise the generic overload, which takes any type as it is, wins over a plain one that would need a conversion: describe(imm small) passes the u8 to the generic overload rather than converting it to an i32. Overloads follow the usual rules from Functions. Two generic overloads that differ only in the names of their type parameters have the same signature:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generic records", "url": "generics.html#generic-records", "text": "Records take type parameters the same way. Fields, constructors, methods and destructors can all use them. A type argument can be written, as in Pair[u8, bool], or inferred from the constructor's arguments: Each instantiation is its own type with its own layout, as the sizes show: a Pair[u8, bool] takes 2 bytes and a Pair[i64, u8] takes 16. Static functions and static fields are reached through an instantiation, as in Counted[i32].made. Every instantiation has its own copy of each static field:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generic methods", "url": "generics.html#generic-methods", "text": "A method or static function can take type parameters of its own, beside its type's. Here map turns a Cell[T] into a Cell[U], with U inferred from the function it's given or written out: Function types and lambdas are covered in Function values and closures."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Generic enums", "url": "generics.html#generic-enums", "text": "Enums take type parameters too, which is how an optional value or a result can be written once for every type: Where the enum type is known, as in ret some(i), a bare case name is enough. A case can name its arguments, as in Option[f64].some(0.5), or leave them to be inferred, as in Option.some(true). Cases and matching are covered in Enums and Pattern matching."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Constraints", "url": "generics.html#constraints", "text": "A constraint after a type parameter's name limits the types it accepts, and in return lets the generic code do more with it. [T: copy] accepts only types that can be copied, so the body can copy a T: A type argument that doesn't meet a constraint is an error where it's used. Inside other generic code, a type parameter meets a constraint only if its own constraints say so: Several constraints are joined with +, as in [T: copy + noref]. Each one can appear only once in a list, and some can't be combined: The constraints are: | copy | types that can be copied | | noref | types that hold no borrows | | drop | types with a destructor | | default | types whose all-zero value is valid | | new, new(...) | types with a matching constructor | | null | types that have a null value | | ext | types that can cross into C | | atomic | types the target can access atomically | | a trait | types that implement the trait | | const and a trait | types with an impl const of the trait | | a function type | functions and closures of that type | ext is covered in Calling C, atomic in Atomics, trait bounds in Traits, const bounds in Constant functions and function types as constraints in Function values and closures."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "copy", "url": "generics.html#copy", "text": "A type can be copied when it isn't declared own and everything it holds can be copied (see Ownership and destructors). T: copy lets a val copy a T and lets the code use a T after passing it on."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "noref", "url": "generics.html#noref", "text": "A type holds borrows when it is a borrow type like imm[i32], a str, a ref rec or ref enum, or anything that contains one (see Borrows). T: noref accepts only types that hold none, which a container that keeps its values needs. Without it, a type parameter may hold borrows, so it can't be passed on to something that requires noref: Holder(imm count) would make a Holder[imm[i32]], which holds a borrow."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "drop", "url": "generics.html#drop", "text": "T: drop accepts only types with a destructor, written or inherited from a field:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "default", "url": "generics.html#default", "text": "T: default accepts types that have a legal default: types whose all-zero memory is a valid value, as Records describes. The body can then declare a T without a value, which starts as zeros, and call T(): Without the constraint, a T declared without a value has to be assigned before it's read, and a type without a legal default doesn't meet it:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "new", "url": "generics.html#new", "text": "T: new asks for a constructor without parameters, and T: new(usize) for one that takes a usize; the generic code then calls it as T() or T(64). A type meets it with a declared constructor that takes exactly those parameter types, or, when it declares no constructor, with its field-by-field constructor or, for new, with T(). Built-in conversions count too, so f64 meets new(usize): The parameter list can name several types and give each one a mode, as in new(i32, i32), new(imm T) or new(Text, mut i32). Calls pass arguments the way the modes say. A type that has no such constructor doesn't meet the constraint: T: new doesn't promise that zeros are a valid T. A generic body that declares a T without a value needs T: default instead."}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "null", "url": "generics.html#null", "text": "T: null accepts types that have a null value, such as pointers and cstr. The generic code can then use null as a T and compare a T with it:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Value parameters", "url": "generics.html#value-parameters", "text": "A value parameter takes a compile-time integer instead of a type. It's declared with const before its name and an integer type after it, as in const length: usize, and inside the declaration it's a constant: it can size an array, appear in expressions, or be passed on as another generic's argument. Like a type parameter, it's inferred from the arguments or written out: Each value gets its own compiled copy, as each type does. Records and enums take value parameters too, mixed with type parameters in any order. A value argument can be any constant expression, such as a named constant times 2: A value parameter must have an integer type, and the value given must fit it. Inside a generic argument, a value parameter must stand alone: array[i32, length] works, but array[i32, length + 1] doesn't, while expressions of plain constants, like capacity * 2, still do. A value parameter isn't a type, and a type isn't a value:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Default arguments", "url": "generics.html#default-arguments", "text": "A type or value parameter can name a default after =. Arguments left off the end of the list take their defaults, so a parameter that most uses don't change doesn't have to be written each time: Pair[i32] is Pair[i32, i32]: a default can use the parameters before it, but not itself or the ones after it. Parameters with defaults come last. A type whose parameters all have defaults is written by its name alone, so Name here is Name[Utf8]. Name[] means the same and gets a \"Redundant '[]'\" hint: Defaults work the same way for functions and methods. A method's default can use its type's parameters too. Inference comes first: a default only fills a parameter that neither the arguments nor the expected type settles. Here Cell(5) is a Cell[i32], since the literal settles T, while the declared type of written makes it a Cell[i64]: A default must meet its parameter's constraints, which is checked where it's declared. Messages leave out trailing arguments that are the same as their defaults, as the last error shows: Two overloads that a call can reach the same way, with or without the defaulted argument, are an error:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Types with the same name", "url": "generics.html#types-with-the-same-name", "text": "Types can share a name when their parameter lists differ, either in how many arguments they take or in whether an argument is a type or a value. The same goes for traits. A type and a trait can't share a name. The arguments pick the declaration: Grid[bool] is the one that takes a type and Grid[3] the one that takes a value. The name alone means the declaration that takes no arguments, counting one whose parameters all have defaults. With no such declaration, the name needs arguments, even where only one declaration could fit. An impl block picks its type the same way, so Size: impl Shape and Size[T]: impl Shape implement it for different types. Importing a name brings in every declaration with that name. Two declarations that could take the same arguments are an error, whether they have the same parameters or only overlap through defaults:"}, {"page": "Generics", "title": "Adding members under bounds", "url": "generics.html#adding-members-under-bounds", "text": "An impl block on a generic type with stronger constraints adds members that exist only for the instantiations that meet them. A block can also name a concrete instantiation, so its members exist only for that one type: Inside Pair[T: copy]: impl, the body knows T: copy, so self can be copied. Using a member on an instantiation that doesn't meet the block's constraints is an error at the use. In the type's own module, a block that adds no constraints to the declaration's is an error, since those members belong in the type's own body: A block in the type's own module can also declare constructors. Like its other members, they exist only for the instantiations that meet the block. This lets a type offer a shorter constructor where a parameter's value can start as zeros: A constructor in a block counts as declaring a constructor, so the type loses its built-in constructors for every instantiation, as Records describes. Calling a block's constructor on an instantiation that doesn't meet the block is an error at the call. A block names every parameter of its type, including the ones with defaults: A block can also name a generic type inside its target and declare that type's parameters there. Its members then exist for every instantiation of that shape, and the body can use the inner parameters like any other: The inner parameters must meet the inner type's constraints, and like the block's other parameters they can't have defaults. Blocks like these most often add members under a trait bound. Blocks that implement a trait, and blocks outside the type's own module, are covered in Traits."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Traits", "url": "traits.html", "text": "A trait names a set of members that several types can provide: methods, properties, static functions, operators, constructors and constants. A type implements a trait by saying so and supplying those members. Generic code can then require the trait and use its members on any type that implements it."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "What a trait is", "url": "traits.html#what-a-trait-is", "text": "A generic function can't call methods on a T, since most types don't have them. A trait solves this. It lists what a type must have, here an area method and a name method: Circle: rec impl Shape declares that Circle implements Shape, and its body supplies the members Shape requires. [T: Shape] is a trait bound: report accepts any type that implements Shape, and its body can call Shape's members on a T. Each type report is called with gets its own compiled copy, so the calls go straight to Circle.area and Square.area, with no lookup at run time. When the type is only known at run time, use a trait object instead. A type implements a trait only by saying so. Having methods with the right names isn't enough:"}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Requirements", "url": "traits.html#requirements", "text": "A trait's body lists its requirements, written like the members of a record without their bodies. Self stands for the implementing type. Besides methods, a trait can require properties, static functions, operators, constructors and constants."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Properties and static functions", "url": "traits.html#properties-and-static-functions", "text": "A property requirement is a standalone get or a prop with both accessors. A function without a receiver is a static function; generic code calls it through the type parameter, as in T.start(): Self exists only inside traits. An implementation writes its own type's name, as start: fun -> Clicks does:"}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Operators", "url": "traits.html#operators", "text": "Operator requirements are written like operator members. Built-in types meet them with their built-in operators, so i32: impl Sum only has to add empty: The noref bound matters here: for a T that could hold borrows, sum + value could borrow from sum, and sum couldn't then be reassigned with it. See Generics. A built-in operator also takes the place of a default body the trait gives for it. An implementation can't declare an operator a type already has built in:"}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Constructors", "url": "traits.html#constructors", "text": "A constructor requirement lets generic code build a T with T(...). A type meets it the way it meets a [new(...) constraint](generics.md#new): with a declared constructor that takes the same parameters, or, when it declares none, with its field-by-field constructor. Built-in conversions count, so u64 meets new: op(capacity: usize) with its conversion from usize: Constants are covered in Trait constants below."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Default bodies", "url": "traits.html#default-bodies", "text": "A requirement can have a body. Types that implement the trait get it as it is, or replace it with their own. A trait can also have pvt members, helpers with bodies that only the trait's own bodies can use: A pvt member needs a body, and a trait can't hold fields, static fields or destructors."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Implementing a trait", "url": "traits.html#implementing-a-trait", "text": "A type implements a trait in one of two places: in its own declaration, as in Circle: rec impl Shape or Color: enum impl Show, or in a separate impl block, as in Point: impl Show. Several traits are joined with +, as in rec impl Hash + Show. An impl block can name any type, built-in types included: The trait's members become members of the type, so Point(1, 2).show() works outside generic code too. An impl block holds only the trait's members and pvt helpers, which only that block can use. It can't add fields, static fields, constructors or destructors. Members the type already has count toward the requirements, and an impl block only supplies the rest. A member that takes self by copy meets a requirement that takes imm self, when the type can be copied: A missing member, a member whose signature differs from the requirement, a trait implemented twice, and a member the trait doesn't have are all errors: A generic type can implement a trait for every instantiation, as in Box[T]: impl Show, for those that meet extra bounds, as in Box[T: Show]: impl Show, or for one instantiation, as in Box[i32]: impl Show. Two of these that cover the same type, like Box[T] and Box[i32], implement the trait twice."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Where an implementation can go", "url": "traits.html#where-an-implementation-can-go", "text": "An implementation must be in the project that declares the trait or the project that declares the type. A program can implement its own traits for a library's types and a library's traits for its own types, but not a library's trait for a library's type: Library projects are covered in Modules and visibility."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Using traits in generic code", "url": "traits.html#using-traits-in-generic-code", "text": "A bound gives generic code every member of the trait: methods and properties on values, static functions and constants on the type parameter, operators, and constructors as T(...). Several bounds are joined with +, and they mix with the other constraints, as in [T: Sum + noref]. Records and enums take bounds on their type parameters too, as in Set[T: Hash + noref]: rec. Inside generic code, a type parameter meets a bound only if its own bounds include that trait or a trait that requires it (see Traits that require other traits), or a blanket implementation covers it. A member declared const, an impl const implementation and a T: const Trait bound let generic code call a trait's members while compiling; see Constant traits."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Picking a trait's member", "url": "traits.html#picking-a-traits-member", "text": "When a type implements two traits with members of the same name, a call by that name is ambiguous. Naming the trait picks its member: Area(s).size() calls Area's method on s, and Area(Square).unit() calls Area's static function for Square. Generic code picks the same way: The pick works for properties too: with a property count in both traits, Area(s).count = 5 calls Area's setter. A trait on its own isn't a value:"}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Generic traits", "url": "traits.html#generic-traits", "text": "A trait can take type parameters, declared on its name like other generics. An implementation names its arguments, as in impl Iterator[i32], and a bound does too, as in [I: Iterator[i32]]. A bound can leave an argument to a type parameter, as count does with T; the compiler infers T from the type's implementation: A type can implement several instantiations of one trait, each with its own members: A trait's type parameters can have defaults. A default can use Self, which stands for the type the trait is applied to: the implementing type in an implementation, and the type parameter in a bound. Here Combine with no argument means \"combine with another value of the same type\", while Offset names a different right-hand type: T: Combine is T: Combine[T], and i32: impl Combine implements Combine[i32]. Where there's no type for Self to stand for, as in a dyn type, the argument has to be written:"}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Traits that require other traits", "url": "traits.html#traits-that-require-other-traits", "text": "Ord: trait req Eq says that every type that implements Ord implements Eq as well. Ord's default bodies can then use Eq's members, and a bound [T: Ord] gives generic code both traits' members and meets [T: Eq] bounds: Implementing Ord implements Eq too, when the type doesn't have it yet, so Version: rec impl Ord is enough, and the same declaration or block holds the members of both. Naming Eq as well, as in rec impl Ord + Eq, gets the hint \"Redundant 'Eq'\". Requirements chain: a trait that requires Ord requires Eq too. A trait can require several, as in req Eq + Hash, and a trait can't require itself, directly or through others: A trait with nothing of its own to require, like Even here, needs no body."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Defaults from a requiring trait", "url": "traits.html#defaults-from-a-requiring-trait", "text": "A trait can give a default body to a member of a trait it requires, by declaring the member again, with the same signature, and a body. Types that implement it get that body unless they write their own: The redeclared member is still Eq's ==, not a second one, so same and every other use of Eq get Card's == from Ranked. When several traits on the way give a default, the one from the trait that requires the others wins. Two unrelated traits that both give one are an error for a type that implements both: Declaring the member again without a body changes nothing, and it gets the hint \"Redundant '=='\", with a note naming the trait that declares it."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Removing a default", "url": "traits.html#removing-a-default", "text": "req before fun or op in such a redeclaration removes the default the required trait gives, so types that implement the trait must write the member themselves: The error's note says \"Default removed by 'Fast'\". A req on a member that has no default to remove gets the hint \"Redundant 'req'\". req can't have a body, and outside such a redeclaration a function or operator can't be req (\"Cannot declare required functions\"). For a type that implements two unrelated traits, one giving a default and the other removing it, the conflict is an error: \"'Eq.!=' has a default in 'Loose' that 'Fast' removes\"."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Constraints on Self", "url": "traits.html#constraints-on-self", "text": "req takes the other constraints as well, and applies them to every type that implements the trait. Bounded: trait req copy accepts only types that can be copied, so its constants can hold a Self and code with a [T: Bounded] bound can copy a T. Built: trait req new(i32) requires a constructor, which generic code calls as T(7): A type that doesn't meet the trait's constraints can't implement it: Constraints and traits mix in one list, as in req copy + Ord. Writing [T: Bounded + copy] repeats what Bounded already requires and gets the hint \"Redundant 'copy'\"."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Blanket implementations", "url": "traits.html#blanket-implementations", "text": "A blanket implementation implements a trait for every type at once. It's written with a type parameter and no type name, as [T]: impl Describe, and can be narrowed by constraints, as [T: Eq]: impl Describe. A type's own implementation always wins over a blanket one. Between blankets, the more constrained one wins for the types it covers: show has no bound at all, yet it can call describe, because [T]: impl Describe covers every T. A type parameter gets a blanket's trait when its bounds meet the blanket's constraints. A blanket body is checked once, like any generic body, so it can only use what its constraints allow: the body of a plain [T]: impl can use sizeOf(T) but no members of self. Two blankets that can't be ranked, because neither one's constraints include the other's, are both errors, and a blanket takes exactly one type parameter: Only the project that declares a trait can give it a blanket implementation; anywhere else, [T]: impl Shape is \"'Shape' is declared in another project\"."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Extension methods", "url": "traits.html#extension-methods", "text": "An impl block with no trait adds members to a type declared somewhere else: a built-in type, a type from another module, or every type at once with [T]. These are extension methods. They can be methods, static functions, properties, operators and constants, but not fields, constructors or destructors: Extensions apply only in the module that declares them and in files that import that module, with use or the project's Imports list. Here measure extends geometry.Point, and main.ce uses the extension because it says use measure: A file that doesn't import the module doesn't see its extensions: Each member follows the visibility rules for top-level declarations: pvt keeps it to its file, mod to its module, no keyword to the project, and pub exports it from a library (see Modules and visibility). A word before impl, as in i32: mod impl { ... }, applies to every member of the block. When a type's own member and an extension have the same signature, the type's member is used; extensions with other signatures overload it. Two imported extensions with the same signature make a call ambiguous, so a file can avoid a clash by not importing one of them. In the type's own module, an impl block without a trait isn't an extension: it adds members under extra bounds, as Generics describes, and a block that adds no bounds is an error."}, {"page": "Traits", "title": "Trait constants", "url": "traits.html#trait-constants", "text": "A trait can require constants. bits: const u32 is a requirement every implementation must give a value, and bytes: const u32 = bits / 8 is a default that an implementation may replace. A constant is read through a type, as in i32.bits or T.bits, or by its bare name inside the trait and its implementations. It folds to a constant for each type, so T.bytes can size an array: Trait constants are always const, and an implementation's constant must have the requirement's type. In a generic argument, T.bytes must stand alone, like a value parameter: Extension blocks and blanket implementations can declare constants too:"}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Trait objects", "url": "trait-objects.html", "text": "A trait object, written dyn[Shape], is a value of some type that implements Shape, where the type is only known when the program runs. Calls through it look up the right member at run time. This chapter covers making trait objects, what can be called through them, testing which type one holds, and owning one through a pointer."}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "From generics to trait objects", "url": "trait-objects.html#from-generics-to-trait-objects", "text": "A function with a trait bound, like report[T: Shape], is compiled once for each type it's called with, and each copy calls that type's members directly. That works when the type is known where the code is compiled. A trait object handles the other case: one compiled function that works on any Shape, whichever type it turns out to be. A dyn[Shape] refers to a value that lives somewhere else, together with a table of that value's Shape members. A call like shape.area() reads the table to find the member to call: report and double are each compiled once. Since a trait object always refers to a value somewhere else, it's taken by a parameter with imm or mut, which borrows the value for the call, as described in Parameter modes. double changes square itself."}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Making a trait object", "url": "trait-objects.html#making-a-trait-object", "text": "Passing a value to a dyn[Shape] parameter converts it, as above. The value must implement the trait, and the call site writes imm or mut as for any borrowed parameter. A temporary, such as Circle(5.0), can be passed as it is. dyn[Shape](circle) converts explicitly, and borrows circle read-only. dyn[Shape](mut circle) borrows it writably: Built-in types can implement traits too, so a literal can become a trait object: A type that doesn't implement the trait doesn't convert. A named value needs imm or mut at the call, a trait object made without mut is read-only, and one made from a temporary can't be kept and used after its statement ends:"}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "What can be called through one", "url": "trait-objects.html#what-can-be-called-through-one", "text": "Through a dyn[Shape], code can call the trait's methods and use its properties, default bodies included. A read-only trait object allows only members that take imm self or self, as with any read-only borrow. Some members can't go through the table at all: static functions, which have no value to look up; members that take or return Self in other places, since the type is unknown; generic methods, which have no single compiled copy; and members that take self, since a trait object doesn't own its value, unless the trait requires Self: copy with req copy and the call can take a copy: Members of a generic trait are called through dyn[Write[i32]] like any others, using the trait's own signature: Console.write takes its value by copy, but Write's requirement takes imm value, so the call through the trait object passes imm value."}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Trait objects aren't values", "url": "trait-objects.html#trait-objects-arent-values", "text": "A dyn[Shape] has no fixed size, since the value it refers to can be any type. It can't be stored, returned, copied or put in an array by value. A borrow of one can: imm[dyn[Shape]] and mut[dyn[Shape]] work as locals, results and ref rec fields, as Borrows describes, and a value or borrow of a type that implements the trait converts to them: These borrows follow the usual rules: they can't outlive the value they refer to. Using dyn[Shape] itself as a value is an error, and so is using it in an ext signature, even behind a pointer: A generic function with a bound, like measure[T: Shape], can pass its T on as a dyn[Shape], but a dyn[Shape] can't be its T, since that would make T a type without a size:"}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Required traits", "url": "trait-objects.html#required-traits", "text": "When a trait requires another, as Shape: trait req Named does, a dyn[Shape] reaches Named's members too, and converts to a dyn[Named]: The conversion only goes that way. A dyn[Named] might hold a type that isn't a Shape, so it doesn't convert to a dyn[Shape]: To find out what type a trait object holds, test for the type itself, as the next section shows."}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Testing the type", "url": "trait-objects.html#testing-the-type", "text": "is and match test which type a trait object holds, and can bind the value as that type with a name after it. A test can look at fields as well, as Circle(radius: > 10.0) does. A match on a trait object needs an else arm, since any number of types can implement the trait. match mut and if mut ... is bind writable borrows, so the arm can change the value: A tested type can be a generic instantiation, as in shape is Box[Circle], or a type parameter, as in shape is T. Testing works through required traits too: a dyn[Named] made from a Circle is still a Circle. Patterns are covered in full in Pattern matching. A type that doesn't implement the trait can never match, and a trait object can't be tested for another trait object type:"}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Pointers to trait objects", "url": "trait-objects.html#pointers-to-trait-objects", "text": "A ptr[dyn[Shape]] is a raw pointer to a value of any type that implements Shape, along with its table. A pointer to a Circle converts to one. Calls go through it with ., like other pointers, and sizeOf(*p) and alignOf(*p) give the size and alignment of the value it points to, read from the table: As with any raw pointer, nothing checks that the value is still there. *p is still a dyn[Shape], so it can't be moved out or stored by value, and since the size of each value is only known at run time, the pointer can't be indexed:"}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Owning a trait object", "url": "trait-objects.html#owning-a-trait-object", "text": "drop *p runs the destructor of the value a ptr[dyn[Shape]] points to, found through its table, and a ptr[dyn[Shape]] converts to a plain ptr, so it can be freed. With these, a record can own a trait object on the heap. Boxed below works for any trait: K: trait makes K a trait parameter, and of[T: K] takes any value whose type implements it: Each Boxed[Shape] has the same size whatever it holds, so values of different types fit in one array. own *memory = own value moves the value into memory that holds nothing yet, * lets shape.area() reach the object as a dereference operator, and the destructor drops the object, the Tagged one's Label included, before freeing its memory."}, {"page": "Trait objects", "title": "Trait parameters", "url": "trait-objects.html#trait-parameters", "text": "A type parameter declared K: trait takes a trait instead of a type. Inside the declaration, dyn[K] is a trait object of that trait, and T: K bounds another type parameter by it. A trait parameter can't be combined with other constraints, and its argument must be a trait: A bound is written with the trait itself, as T: Shape; dyn[Shape] is a type, not a constraint."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Operators", "url": "operators.html", "text": "Records and enums can declare their own operators. An operator member is a function written with op and named by its symbol, and a + b on that type calls it. The same form declares indexers (v[i]), the dereference operator (*p) and the operators that let for walk a type."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Declaring an operator", "url": "operators.html#declaring-an-operator", "text": "A binary operator is a static member that takes both operands as parameters: The member's name is the operator, followed by a colon and op. The body is a block or => and an expression, as for any function. Operators keep the precedence and associativity of the built-in ones (see Operators and precedence)."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Binary operators", "url": "operators.html#binary-operators", "text": "These binary operators can be declared: | Arithmetic | + - * / % | | Wrapping | +% -% *% | | Bitwise | & \\| ^ | | Shifts and rotations | << >> <<< >>> | | Comparisons | == != < <= > >= | A binary operator takes exactly two parameters, and at least one of them must have the declaring type. The other can be any type, and either one can come first, so a type can declare both v * 2.0 and 2.0 * v: The literal 3 in v * 3 becomes an f64, the type the parameter asks for, as a literal does for any call. The rules on the parameters: Declared comparisons chain like the built-in ones, so a < b < c means a < b && b < c, with b evaluated once:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Unary operators", "url": "operators.html#unary-operators", "text": "-, + and ~ take one operand, which the operator receives as self: - and + can be declared both ways: with self as unary operators and with two parameters as binary ones. ~ is only unary. The receiver can be self or imm self, but not mut self: a unary operator gives a new value and leaves its operand as it was. Some operators can't be declared at all: !, &&, ||, = and .."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Compound assignment", "url": "operators.html#compound-assignment", "text": "A compound assignment such as += changes its left side in place. It takes mut self and one parameter, and returns nothing: Every compound form of the binary operators can be declared: +=, -=, *=, /=, %=, +%=, -%=, *%=, &=, |=, ^=, <<=, >>=, <<<= and >>>=. Since the operator borrows its left side mutably, that side must be something that can change, such as a var:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Nothing is derived", "url": "operators.html#nothing-is-derived", "text": "Each operator stands alone. Declaring + doesn't give +=, == doesn't give !=, and < doesn't give >, <= or >=. Each one a type supports has to be declared: A record has no operators of its own, not even ==. An enum whose cases have no payloads compares with == and != already, and declaring either is an error (\"Operator '==' between 'Color' and 'Color' is built in\"), as is declaring any operator that a built-in type already has for the same operands. An enum with payloads has neither until it declares them. Enums declare operators the same way records do:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Operands and parameter modes", "url": "operators.html#operands-and-parameter-modes", "text": "An operand without a mode is taken by value, as for any parameter (see Parameter modes): a copy, or the value itself for a type that can't be copied. Such a type usually takes its operands with imm to borrow them. Operands carry no marks, so a + b borrows or moves each operand as the operator's declaration says: c - b hands c to the operator, which drops it when it returns, and c can't be used afterwards. A compound assignment's parameter takes modes the same way, except mut."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Calling an operator through its type", "url": "operators.html#calling-an-operator-through-its-type", "text": "Type.op(...) calls an operator like a static function. The operands are the arguments, receiver first, and a compound assignment's left side is passed with mut: Built-in types work the same way, so i32.+(1, 2) adds two i32s and f64.*(1.5, 2.0) multiplies two f64s: i32.+(small, small) converts both i8s to i32 first, so the sum doesn't overflow. An operator can't be called through a value:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Ambiguous operators", "url": "operators.html#ambiguous-operators", "text": "Both operand types can declare an operator for the same pair. Using it is then an error, because neither declaration wins: Calling the operator through one of the types picks its declaration: Two declarations in the same type with the same parameter types conflict, and both are reported:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Visibility", "url": "operators.html#visibility", "text": "An operator is visible wherever its type is. pvt limits it to the type's own members, like any other member (see Methods and properties):"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Generic operators", "url": "operators.html#generic-operators", "text": "An operator can have type parameters, written after its symbol. They must be inferable from the operands: A type parameter that only appears in the result can't be inferred, since an operator use has no place to write type arguments: Generics are covered in Generics."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Indexers", "url": "operators.html#indexers", "text": "An indexer makes value[i] work on a type. It's an operator named [], and it usually comes as a pair: one with imm self that lends the element for reading, and one with mut self that lends it for writing. The read-only indexer returns imm[T] and the writable one mut[T], borrows of the element (see Borrows). scores[i] then works like an array element: it can be read, assigned, changed with +=, passed as mut scores[i], and used with . to reach the element's fields and methods."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Which indexer a use picks", "url": "operators.html#which-indexer-a-use-picks", "text": "The compiler picks the indexer from how the element is used. Reading the element, borrowing it with imm or calling a method on it that doesn't change it uses the imm self indexer. Assigning to it, changing it with a compound assignment, passing it as mut, assigning to one of its fields or calling a mut self method on it uses the mut self indexer: A type with only a writable indexer uses it for reads too, as long as the value is writable. A type with only a read-only one can't have its elements changed: When the writable indexer is pvt, the elements are read-only outside the type, while the type's own members can still write them:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Indexers that return values", "url": "operators.html#indexers-that-return-values", "text": "The read-only indexer can return the element by value instead of lending it. This is how a type gives out elements that aren't stored anywhere, such as single bits: A value that an indexer returns isn't stored in the type, so it can't be assigned, borrowed or changed: A by-value indexer can be paired with a writable one, which handles every write while the by-value one handles reads."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Several parameters", "url": "operators.html#several-parameters", "text": "An indexer takes any number of parameters of any type, so m[row, column] and table[color] work: A bare case name like green works as an index because the parameter expects a Color."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Indexer rules", "url": "operators.html#indexer-rules", "text": "An indexer needs a receiver and at least one parameter, and none of its parameters can be mut. A type has at most one read-only and one writable indexer, and the two must take the same parameters and give the same element type. The read-only one takes self or imm self and returns imm[T] or T; the writable one takes mut self and returns mut[T]: Indexing with the wrong number of arguments says how many the type takes:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "The dereference operator", "url": "operators.html#the-dereference-operator", "text": "A type that holds a pointer to a value can make * reach that value. The operator is * with a receiver and no parameters. Like indexers, it comes as a read-only form with imm self returning imm[T] and a writable form with mut self returning mut[T]: The use picks the form the same way as for indexers: reads take the imm self one, and writes, mut borrows and mut self calls take the mut self one. A read-only * can also return its target by value, as in *: op(imm self) -> i32."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": ". looks through *", "url": "operators.html#looks-through", "text": ". on a value whose type declares * reaches the target's members, so boxed.health means (*boxed).health: The type's own members come first: boxed.describe() calls EnemyBox.describe, and (*boxed).describe() reaches the one on Enemy. . looks through one level only. If the target also declares *, its members are reached with (*outer).field, not outer.field."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Dereference rules", "url": "operators.html#dereference-rules", "text": "* takes a receiver and no other parameters, and a self receiver needs a type that copies. A type has at most one form of each kind, and both must give the same target type:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Custom for loops", "url": "operators.html#custom-for-loops", "text": "for walks integer ranges and arrays on its own (see Control flow and Arrays). Any other type takes part through two operators: for: op(mut self) -> bool and *. A type with both is a cursor. Before each pass, the loop calls for; when it returns true, the loop reads the element with * and runs the body, and when it returns false, the loop ends. for takes mut self and nothing else, and returns bool. break, cont and ret work as in any loop."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Stepping a cursor in place", "url": "operators.html#stepping-a-cursor-in-place", "text": "A cursor in a variable is copied into the loop, like an argument, so the variable doesn't change. for x in mut c steps the variable itself, so a loop that stops early leaves it where it stopped, and a second loop carries on from there: Without mut, for x in c takes the cursor by value instead: it copies a cursor that copies and moves one that doesn't."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Collections", "url": "operators.html#collections", "text": "A collection hands out a cursor through in. in: op(imm self) -> Cursor makes the cursor for loops that read; a second in: op(mut self) -> Cursor makes one for loops that change the elements. A cursor that refers back to its collection holds a borrow of it, so it's a ref rec (see Borrows): The binding picks the in: for x in bag and for imm x in bag use the imm self one, and for mut x in bag uses the mut self one. for x in bag copies each element, which needs a copyable element type, while for imm x in bag borrows it. Writing to a mut binding goes through the cursor's writable *."}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "When a loop ends", "url": "operators.html#when-a-loop-ends", "text": "The cursor lives until the loop ends, however it ends, and is dropped then. A cursor with a destructor finds out that the loop is over, which a collection can use to check, for example, that it wasn't changed during the loop:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Loop operator rules", "url": "operators.html#loop-operator-rules", "text": "in takes a receiver, imm self, mut self or, for a type that copies, self, and no other parameters, and returns a cursor rather than a borrow. for takes mut self only and returns bool. A type declares each form once:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "The ? operator", "url": "operators.html#the-operator", "text": "Postfix ? unwraps a value or leaves the function early. A type declares it as ?: op(self) -> T with an expression body. When a function writes x?, the body runs in that function: its value becomes the value of x?, and a ret in the body returns from the function that wrote x?. The usual use is a result type that holds either a value or an error. ? gives the value, or returns the error to the caller: In sumTwo(-1, 2), parse(-1)? runs the err arm, which returns Result.err(-1) from sumTwo; parse(2) never runs. The function that uses ? must be able to return what the body returns. Using it in a function with another result type, with no result, or that never returns is an error, and so is ? on a type that doesn't declare it: A ? operator takes self and nothing else, has a return type and a => body, and can't be generic or required by a trait:"}, {"page": "Operators", "title": "Operators in traits", "url": "operators.html#operators-in-traits", "text": "A trait can require operators, indexers, * and in like any other member, and generic code can then use them on a type parameter. ? is the exception. See Traits."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Conversions", "url": "conversions.html", "text": "A conversion turns a value of one type into a value of another. Some happen on their own, such as an i32 passed where an i64 is expected. Others have to be written out as T(x). The built-in types come with their conversions, and records and enums declare their own as members: as for conversions that happen on their own and new for ones that have to be written."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Writing a conversion", "url": "conversions.html#writing-a-conversion", "text": "T(x) converts x to the type T. It's written like a call to a constructor of T, and a constructor that takes one value is a conversion into T. The built-in conversions cover: numbers to other number types, in every direction, as described in Numbers and arithmetic; bool to any integer type, giving 0 or 1; char to and from integer types; enums without payloads to and from integer types (see Enums); pointers to other pointer types, cstr to and from ptr (see Pointers and raw memory); values to trait objects, as in dyn[Shape](circle) (see Trait objects). A conversion that can lose information, like u8(x) for an i32 x, is checked. A constant that doesn't fit is a compile error, and other values are checked when the program runs; Numbers and arithmetic and Checks and panics cover both."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Implicit conversions", "url": "conversions.html#implicit-conversions", "text": "A conversion happens on its own wherever a value of one type is used as another: a declaration with a type, an assignment, an argument, a returned value or an operand. Only conversions that can't lose anything happen on their own. Among the built-in types, those are: a number to a numeric type that holds every value of its type exactly, like i32 to i64, u8 to i16, i32 to f64 or f32 to f64 (Numbers and arithmetic lists them all); cstr and any ptr[T] to ptr, and null to any pointer. Everything else has to be written as T(x): i64 to f64 isn't implicit because an f64 can't hold every i64 exactly."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Types decide, not values", "url": "conversions.html#types-decide-not-values", "text": "Whether a value converts on its own depends only on its type, never on the value it happens to hold. A constant is no exception: limit below is an i32, and an i32 doesn't convert to usize on its own, even though 3 would fit. Literals are the one case where the value matters. A literal has no type until it's used, so it takes the type that's expected, as long as its value fits:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Declaring conversions", "url": "conversions.html#declaring-conversions", "text": "Records and enums declare conversions as members. There are four forms, one for each direction and kind: | as: op(self) -> T | out of the type, to T | on its own, or as T(x) | | as: op(value: T) -> Type | into the type, from T | on its own, or as Type(x) | | new: op(self) -> T | out of the type, to T | only as T(x) | | new: op(value: T) | into the type, from T | only as Type(x) | Type stands for the declaring type. The last form is an ordinary constructor (see Records)."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "as out of a type", "url": "conversions.html#as-out-of-a-type", "text": "as with a receiver converts the type to another one wherever that other type is expected: id + 1 converts id to i32 because + has no other way to take an Id."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "as into a type", "url": "conversions.html#as-into-a-type", "text": "A static as, with one parameter and no receiver, converts the parameter's type into the declaring type. It lets a function take several kinds of values through one parameter:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "new out of a type", "url": "conversions.html#new-out-of-a-type", "text": "A conversion that shouldn't happen on its own, because it loses information or does real work, is new with a receiver. It only runs when written as T(x): Fixed(0x28000) is the field-by-field constructor, which sets raw. Using it where an i32 is expected without writing i32(...) is an error:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Receivers", "url": "conversions.html#receivers", "text": "The receiver of an outward conversion says how the value is taken, as for any method: self takes the value, a copy or the value itself, and returns a new one. imm self borrows the value. It can return a borrow of part of it, imm[T], or a type that borrows, such as str. mut self borrows the value writably and returns mut[T]. A type can give out a borrow of something it holds this way. Slot below converts to a Sprite by lending its own: a read-only borrow where a Sprite is read, a writable one where it's passed as mut. A string type is a common case. It owns its bytes and lends them as a str, so it can go wherever a str is expected: The str borrows from name, so it follows the rules for borrows (see Borrows): it can't outlive name, and it can't be kept from a temporary that's dropped at the end of its statement: A self conversion on a type that can't be copied consumes the value, so converting a named variable moves it, as any move does (see Ownership and destructors): The receiver and the result have to agree:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "How conversions are used", "url": "conversions.html#how-conversions-are-used", "text": ""}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Exact matches come first", "url": "conversions.html#exact-matches-come-first", "text": "When a call has overloads, one that takes the argument's own type wins over one that needs a conversion. A generic type parameter is inferred from the argument's own type, never from what it converts to:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": ". doesn't look through as", "url": "conversions.html#doesnt-look-through-as", "text": ". only reaches a type's own members, never the members of what it converts to. A conversion is also not a member that can be called by name: Write Sprite(slot).hp or i32(id) instead."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "A type's own constructor comes first", "url": "conversions.html#a-types-own-constructor-comes-first", "text": "A type can declare both a constructor and an as from the same type. T(x) then calls the constructor, and the as runs only where the conversion happens on its own: Meters(value) inside the as calls the constructor too, so the as doesn't call itself. The same holds for a record's field-by-field constructor, which is why a short as can build its result directly:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Conversions don't chain", "url": "conversions.html#conversions-dont-chain", "text": "A value converts at most once. When no single conversion leads from its type to the one expected, there is no conversion, even if two in a row would get there. Below, an i32 would have to widen to i64 and then go through Arg's as, and an Id would have to become an i32 and then widen to i64: log(5) works, since the literal 5 becomes an i64 directly, and log(i64(count)) converts in two written steps."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Ambiguous conversions", "url": "conversions.html#ambiguous-conversions", "text": "Both types can declare a conversion between them: the source with as out, and the target with as in or a constructor. Neither wins, so every use is an error: A value with several as conversions can also leave an operator with more than one way to go:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Declaration rules", "url": "conversions.html#declaration-rules", "text": "An outward conversion takes only its receiver, and an inward one exactly one parameter. A type can't convert to itself, can't declare two conversions for the same pair of types, and can't declare both an as and a new to the same target: An inward as must return the declaring type, so it can't be used to add a conversion between two other types:"}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Built-in conversions can't be redeclared", "url": "conversions.html#built-in-conversions-cant-be-redeclared", "text": "Where the language already converts between two types, a type can't declare its own conversion between them. An enum without payloads converts to and from every integer type, so it can't declare one: A case's number is set with a case value instead (see Enums)."}, {"page": "Conversions", "title": "Conversions in generic code", "url": "conversions.html#conversions-in-generic-code", "text": "A constraint like [T: new(Source)] asks for a way to write T(source). Constructors, a record's field-by-field constructor and new conversions meet it; as conversions don't: Traits can require as and new conversions too, and each requirement is met only by its own kind. Constraints are covered in Generics and requirements in Traits."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Constant functions", "url": "constant-functions.html", "text": "A constant function is a function the compiler can also run while compiling. Called where a value must be known at compile time, such as a constant's initial value or the length of an array type, it runs then and its result becomes a constant. Called anywhere else, it's an ordinary function call."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Declaring a constant function", "url": "constant-functions.html#declaring-a-constant-function", "text": "const before fun makes a function constant: side and area are worked out while compiling, and the type of cells uses square(2) as its length. square(n) in main runs when the program does, like any call. A constant function's arguments must be constants where a constant is needed:"}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "What a body can do", "url": "constant-functions.html#what-a-body-can-do", "text": "A constant function's body is an ordinary function body. It can declare val, var and const locals, assign to them, branch, loop, match, take mut and imm parameters and call other constant functions: The body may only call constant functions and operators. Built-in operators and conversions are always constant. It can't call other functions, including ext functions and the standard library's panic, read or write module and static values that aren't constants, use pointers, take addresses, call function values or use values with destructors: These rules are checked once, where the function is declared, whether or not anything calls it."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Operators, constructors and methods", "url": "constant-functions.html#operators-constructors-and-methods", "text": "Operators, constructors, conversions and methods are constant the same way, with const before op or fun: A constructor declared new: op() = default is always constant, and so is building a record field by field. Destructors can't be constant."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Functions without parameters", "url": "constant-functions.html#functions-without-parameters", "text": "A constant function that takes no parameters and isn't generic works like a constant. The compiler runs it once, where it's declared, and every call to it is that value. Its errors are reported where it's declared, even if nothing calls it: It's still compiled as a function when something needs one: when it's pub, when it's used as a function value, or when it meets a trait's requirement. Otherwise only its value is kept."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Errors while running", "url": "constant-functions.html#errors-while-running", "text": "A constant function that fails while compiling stops the build. The error points at the call, and its note shows where in the function it happened: A function that runs for too long or recurses too deeply also stops with an error, such as \"Evaluating 'spin' takes too many steps\". The same function called while the program runs fails the way any call does, with a panic."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Generic constant functions", "url": "constant-functions.html#generic-constant-functions", "text": "A constant function can be generic. Since it can't use values with destructors, a type parameter it stores or copies needs copy:"}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Constant traits", "url": "constant-functions.html#constant-traits", "text": "Generic code can call a trait's members at compile time only when every type that meets the bound has constant ones. There are two ways to promise that."}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Constant requirements", "url": "constant-functions.html#constant-requirements", "text": "A trait member declared const is constant for every type that implements the trait. Each implementation must give a constant member, and built-in operators count:"}, {"page": "Constant functions", "title": "Constant implementations and bounds", "url": "constant-functions.html#constant-implementations-and-bounds", "text": "A trait whose members aren't const can still be implemented as constant. impl const promises that every member of that implementation is constant, and T: const Trait accepts only types with such an implementation. Inside the generic function, the trait's members, and those of the traits it requires, can then be called at compile time: Gem's implementation is constant, so total accepts it while compiling. Rock's isn't, so it doesn't meet const Ranked. An impl const checks its members, a default it would use must itself be const, and the traits it requires must be implemented as constant too: A constant implementation is only ever the written impl const: a conformance whose members happen to all be constant doesn't meet const Trait."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Function values and closures", "url": "closures.html", "text": "Functions are values in Cella: they can be stored in variables and fields, passed to other functions and returned from them. A lambda writes a function in the middle of an expression, and a lambda that uses the variables around it is a closure."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Function types", "url": "closures.html#function-types", "text": "A function type lists the parameter types and the result type between brackets, with -> before the result: | fun[i32, i32 -> i32] | takes two i32s and returns an i32 | | fun[str] | takes a str and returns nothing | | fun[-> bool] | takes nothing and returns a bool | | fun[] | takes nothing and returns nothing | A parameter can have a mode, as in a declaration: fun[mut i32, i32] takes its first argument as a writable borrow, and fun[imm Text -> i32] borrows its argument to read it (see Parameter modes)."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Functions as values", "url": "closures.html#functions-as-values", "text": "A function's name, without a call, is a value of its function type: var f = double gives f the type fun[i32 -> i32]. Calling a function value looks like calling a function. Function values go anywhere other values do, such as arrays, record fields and module variables: handler.run(4) calls the function stored in the field. A function value is 16 bytes: the address of the code and a pointer to the data a closure carries, which is null for a plain function. A function value always holds a function, so it can't be null. Function values also can't be compared, can't be passed to C's variadic functions, and a declared function can't be assigned to:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Overloaded functions", "url": "closures.html#overloaded-functions", "text": "The name of an overloaded function stands for the overload whose type is expected. A local without a type waits for its uses to decide (see Variables and constants): When nothing settles it, or no overload has the expected type, the name is an error:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Modes in function types", "url": "closures.html#modes-in-function-types", "text": "A function value with mode parameters is called the way the function is, with imm or mut written at the call: The modes are part of the type: damage is a fun[mut i32, i32], and it doesn't fit a fun[i32, i32]."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "ext fun types", "url": "closures.html#ext-fun-types", "text": "ext fun[A -> R] is a C function pointer: the address of a function that uses C's calling convention, 8 bytes, with no closure data. It's the type for callbacks that C code calls, like qsort's comparison. A function declared with ext fun, a function from an ext block and any Cella function named directly all give ext fun values: An ext fun value converts to the matching fun type, as applyFun(abs, -7) shows. The other way, a fun value can't become an ext fun, since it may be a closure: Calling C covers callbacks in C APIs."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Lambdas", "url": "closures.html#lambdas", "text": "A lambda is a function without a name, written where it's used: fun, the parameters, and then => and an expression or a block: A lambda with no parameters leaves out the parentheses: fun => 42, fun { ... }. A block body returns with ret, like a function's."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Types come from the expected function type", "url": "closures.html#types-come-from-the-expected-function-type", "text": "A lambda passed where a function type is expected, or stored in a variable of a function type, takes its parameter types, their modes and its result type from that type. They can also be written out, as long as they match: In the first call, value is a mut i32 because bump is a fun[mut i32], so value += 10 changes the array's element. A lambda that doesn't fit the expected type is an error: A lambda whose written parameter types don't match keeps its own type, and without -> R that type returns nothing, so the second error calls it a fun[i64]. The ref fun in that message is covered under Closure types."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Lambdas without an expected type", "url": "closures.html#lambdas-without-an-expected-type", "text": "A lambda stored in a local without a type has nothing to learn its types from, other than the local's later uses. Its parameters take their types from those uses or from the lambda itself, and without -> R it returns nothing: triple gets its type from apply, the one place it's used. When nothing gives a parameter its type, or a lambda without -> R has a value as its body, that's an error:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Lambdas and generic functions", "url": "closures.html#lambdas-and-generic-functions", "text": "In a call to a generic function, the other arguments settle the type parameters first, and a lambda with an expression body supplies the rest from what it returns: A block body's result isn't used for inference, so it needs -> R:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Captures", "url": "closures.html#captures", "text": "A lambda can use the local variables and parameters of the function around it. It captures them, which makes it a closure: each takes its callback as imm visit: fun[i32], which lends it a closure for the call; Closure types explains why a callback that may capture is taken with imm. A closure captures by reference: it refers to the variable itself, not a copy. It sees the variable as it is when the closure is called, and when the variable is a var, it can change it, as total += ... does above: A lambda inside a method can capture self the same way. A closure can change only what its function could change: a var, a mut parameter, or something reached through them. It can't move a captured variable out, since the variable stays with the function that owns it:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Closure types", "url": "closures.html#closure-types", "text": "A closure borrows the variables it captures, so it's a borrow itself, and a local that holds one has the type ref fun[A -> R]. A plain function fits a ref fun too. A ref fun follows the rules for borrows: it can be held in locals and in the fields of a ref rec (see Borrows), but not in a plain record or a module variable, and it doesn't convert to fun: A lambda that captures nothing is a plain function, of type fun[...]. Parameters are never written as ref fun. A parameter that takes a closure is written imm f: fun[...]: it borrows the closure for the call, as imm does for any value (see Parameter modes). That's why it accepts closures that capture, and why the function can call it but can't keep it. A named closure is passed to it with imm, as in apply(imm add, 1); a lambda written in the call needs nothing:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Where closures can go", "url": "closures.html#where-closures-can-go", "text": "Since a closure borrows its captures, it can't outlive them. It can't be returned from the function that made it, or stored anywhere that lasts longer than its captures, and it can't be used after a captured variable is moved away: A fun[...] parameter without a mode takes its argument by value and can keep it, so it accepts plain functions and lambdas that capture nothing, but no closures that capture. Neither does an ext fun, which has no room for captured data: The same lambdas without base work in both places. To keep a closure past the function that made it, make it an owned closure, below."}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Closures in loops", "url": "closures.html#closures-in-loops", "text": "A closure made in a loop's body can capture the loop's variable, and can be used during that pass: Each pass has its own variables, which end with the pass, so a closure from one pass can't be used in a later one or after the loop:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Owned closures", "url": "closures.html#owned-closures", "text": "own fun makes a closure that holds its captures itself instead of borrowing them, so it can live on after the variables it used are gone. It takes each capture in by its kind: a val or a parameter is copied; a value with a destructor is moved in, and dropped with the closure; a var is taken over: the closure owns it from then on, and the function can't use it again until it assigns a new value. add has its own step, still 3, and the function's step is a new value. next keeps its count between calls. Using count in main after the closure took it is an error: A captured value with a destructor lives as long as the closure: An owned closure is moved like any value with a destructor. Its type has no name: each own fun lambda has a type of its own, so two of them can't be stored in the same variable, and one doesn't convert to fun. It can be passed to an imm f: fun[...] parameter, which borrows it for the call: An owned closure copies or takes in its captures, so it can't change a captured val, and it can't capture a read-only parameter whose type can't be copied:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Function types as constraints", "url": "closures.html#function-types-as-constraints", "text": "A function type also works as a constraint on a type parameter. [F: fun[i32 -> i32]] accepts any value that can be called that way: a function, an ext fun, a borrowing closure or an owned one. Generic code can call it, and keep it in a field: Counter holds an owned closure without knowing its type, which is how a record keeps one. Generics covers constraints in general. A value that doesn't fit the function type is an error at the call:"}, {"page": "Function values and closures", "title": "Calling through *", "url": "closures.html#calling-through", "text": "A call looks through a type's dereference operator (see Operators). When *v gives a function, v(...) calls it, the same as (*v)(...): This is how a box type that owns a closure on the heap is called like the closure itself. A function type works as a trait, so dyn[fun[-> i32]] is a trait object for any function with that signature (see Trait objects), and a box of one can hold any owned closure. Returned from a function, it keeps the closure, and the variables the closure took over, alive: Boxed.of moves the closure into memory from malloc, and the destructor drops it there and frees the memory. A borrowing closure can't go into the box, because of requires T: noref and a ref fun holds borrows."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Variadic functions", "url": "variadics.html", "text": "A variadic function takes any number of arguments in its last position. Cella has two kinds. C variadics, written ... in an ext declaration, follow C's calling convention, as printf does. Typed variadics, written ...name: T, convert every extra argument to one element type, and the function receives them all as one value."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "C variadics", "url": "variadics.html#c-variadics", "text": "An ext function whose parameter list ends in ... takes any number of extra arguments after its fixed ones: The C function finds out how many arguments it got and what types they have from its fixed arguments, here the format string. Nothing checks that the format matches the arguments, as in C. A bare ... needs at least one parameter before it:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Promotions", "url": "variadics.html#promotions", "text": "Extra arguments follow C's default promotions. Integers narrower than 32 bits and bool are widened to a 32-bit int, and f32 is widened to f64. Every other integer up to 64 bits, f64, char, pointers and cstr are passed as they are. An integer literal that fits in an i32 is passed as one, and a float literal as an f64. So %d reads a u8, i16 or bool, and %f reads an f32, as in C. Values of ext enum types are passed as their integer values, and ext fun values as function pointers; Calling C covers both."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "What can't be passed", "url": "variadics.html#what-cant-be-passed", "text": "Other types can't go through ...: i128 and u128, str, records, arrays, enums that aren't ext, and Cella function values are errors. Pass the parts C understands instead. A str goes to %.*s as its length and its data pointer, and a record goes field by field:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Only in ext declarations", "url": "variadics.html#only-in-ext-declarations", "text": "A bare ... belongs to C functions declared in ext blocks. A Cella function can't take one, and neither can an ext fun written in Cella for C to call: Cella functions take typed variadics instead."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Typed variadics", "url": "variadics.html#typed-variadics", "text": "A typed variadic parameter is written with ... before its name, as the last parameter. Its type collects the extra arguments: At each call, the compiler stores the extra arguments in a temporary array and builds the parameter from the array's address and its length. The parameter's type can be any type with a constructor that takes a ptr[T] and a usize, the constraint new(ptr[T], usize) from Generics; its T is the element type. Cella has no built-in type for this, so the program declares one. View here is a record holding a pointer and a count. Its field-by-field constructor takes exactly those two values, and its [] operator reads one element. Declaring it as a ref rec makes the compiler check that a View never outlives the arguments it points to; Borrows explains how. The examples below use this View without showing it again. The type must meet that constraint exactly once:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Arguments", "url": "variadics.html#arguments", "text": "Each extra argument is converted to the element type, the way the elements of an array literal are. A literal that doesn't fit is an error: Since every argument has the element type, a lambda argument gets its parameter types from it: apply also shows fixed parameters before the variadic one. They're filled first, and the rest of the arguments go to the variadic parameter. A fixed parameter with a default value can be left out as usual. Arguments are evaluated left to right, as in any call: A variadic argument is passed like a parameter without a mode, so writing mut at the call is an error, and so is passing a value of the parameter's own type. To pass one, spread it, as the next section shows."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Mixed argument types", "url": "variadics.html#mixed-argument-types", "text": "All arguments share one element type, but an enum with implicit conversions into it accepts values of several types. Each argument converts to the enum through its as member: Arg is a ref enum because its text case holds a str, which borrows its bytes."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Values with destructors", "url": "variadics.html#values-with-destructors", "text": "The temporary array lives until the end of the statement that made the call. Arguments with destructors are moved into it, and the array drops its elements, last first, when the statement ends: A named argument moves into the array too, so it can't be used after the call: Because the array is gone after the statement, a function can return its variadic parameter, but the caller can't keep the result:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Declaration rules", "url": "variadics.html#declaration-rules", "text": "The variadic parameter comes last, a function has at most one, and it takes no mode: Operators can't be variadic, and neither can ext functions, whether written in Cella or declared in ext blocks, which take C's ... instead:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Methods, constructors and generics", "url": "variadics.html#methods-constructors-and-generics", "text": "Methods, static functions and constructors take variadic parameters too, and generic functions infer their type arguments from the variadic arguments: A trait can require a variadic method or constructor. The implementation must be variadic too, and calls through a bound or a dyn value pass their arguments the same way."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Overloads", "url": "variadics.html#overloads", "text": "A variadic function can be overloaded like any other. Since a variadic parameter takes any number of arguments, an overload that accepts the same arguments conflicts with it: An overload taking a different type is fine, and so is one that takes the parameter's type directly, which only a value of that type reaches:"}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Spreading", "url": "variadics.html#spreading", "text": "... before an argument spreads it into the variadic part of a call. The spread value has the variadic parameter's own type and is passed as it is, so its elements become the arguments. This is how one variadic function forwards its arguments to another. A value that converts to the parameter's type spreads too; here View gets an as member that converts any array of T into a view of its elements, so arrays can be spread: A spread is the whole variadic part of a call: it can't be mixed with other variadic arguments, it doesn't fill fixed parameters, and it only goes into a typed variadic parameter."}, {"page": "Variadic functions", "title": "Variadic function types", "url": "variadics.html#variadic-function-types", "text": "Variadic functions are values like other functions, and their function types keep the .... A C variadic type ends in a bare ... after at least one parameter, and a typed one writes ... before the parameter type. Calls through a value work as calls through the name do, spreads included: A lambda takes the ... from the type it's given, like its parameter types and modes. Its parameter receives the built value: The ... is part of the type, so a variadic function type and the same type without it don't convert either way: The rules for declarations apply to types as well: the ... comes last, takes no mode, and a typed one can't appear in an ext fun type."}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "String templates", "url": "templates.html", "text": "A template is a string with holes, handed to a function that writes it out. The language doesn't format anything itself. It gives the receiving function the literal pieces of the string, a format spec for each hole, and a borrow of each hole's value, and that function decides how to turn the values into text and where to put it."}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "A first template", "url": "templates.html#a-first-template", "text": "A parameter of type fstr[K] takes a template. K is a trait that every hole's value must implement, and it's how the receiving code works with values it knows nothing else about: The string passed to say has three holes, each an expression in braces. say walks them in order: it prints the literal piece before each hole, then the hole's value, and finally the piece after the last hole. The pieces are str values, so say prints them through Display as well. str and i32 take part because the program implements Display for them. A template only accepts holes whose types implement its trait, built-in types included."}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Reading a template", "url": "templates.html#reading-a-template", "text": "An fstr[K] has four members: | holes | usize | the number of holes | | piece(i) | str | the literal text before hole i; piece(holes) is the text after the last hole | | spec(i) | str | the format spec of hole i, empty when it has none | | value(i) | imm[dyn[K]] | a read-only borrow of hole i's value | There is always one more piece than there are holes, and a piece can be empty. This function prints a template's parts instead of its text: The indexes are usize values, and an index past the end panics like any other bounds check: The members can't be assigned to:"}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Holes", "url": "templates.html#holes", "text": "A hole holds any expression: a name, arithmetic, a call, a comparison, a string literal or a match. Holes are evaluated left to right when the template is made, before the receiving function runs: Every hole's type must implement the template's trait: A hole can't be empty, and its braces must close in the same string. A literal { is written \\{, and a } on its own is ordinary text, as in any string (see Braces in strings)."}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Format specs", "url": "templates.html#format-specs", "text": "A hole may end in a format spec: a : followed by any text up to the closing brace, as in {price:.2}. The spec is passed along as text through spec(i), and what it means is up to the receiving code. Spaces are kept, and escape sequences work as in the rest of the string, so \\} puts a brace in a spec. A hole without a :, or with nothing after it, has an empty spec. This Display takes the spec as a parameter, and i32 prints in hex when the spec is x:"}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Strings without holes", "url": "templates.html#strings-without-holes", "text": "A template parameter also takes a string literal without holes or a str value. Either becomes a template with no holes and one piece: Nothing else converts to a template, not even a cstr: Outside a template, a string with holes is a constant: every hole must be a constant string, and the compiler joins the pieces into one string (see Interpolated strings). When a function is overloaded for str and for a template, a string without holes goes to the str overload, and a string with holes goes to the template, even when its holes are constants:"}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Templates and traits", "url": "templates.html#templates-and-traits", "text": "value(i) gives each hole as a borrowed trait object, so the receiving code can call the trait's members on it, and the members of the traits it requires. A record takes part by implementing the trait, and its implementation can use a template itself: A hole can hold a generic value whose bound is the template's trait, as in log, or a dyn value of that trait, as in relay. The argument of fstr must be a trait, exactly one: A generic function can leave the trait open with a K: trait parameter, written out at the call:"}, {"page": "String templates", "title": "Templates as values", "url": "templates.html#templates-as-values", "text": "fstr[K] is an ordinary type. A template can be made explicitly with fstr[Display](...), copied, passed on and returned: A template borrows its hole values, so it follows the rules for borrows. It can't outlive the values in its holes, a record that holds one must be a ref rec, and a module variable can't hold one: The last error is about compute(): its result is a temporary that ends with its statement, so a template that keeps it can't be used afterwards. Since a template holds borrows of Cella values, it can't appear in an ext signature:"}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Modules and visibility", "url": "modules.html", "text": "A module is a named group of declarations. Every source file belongs to one, and a module can span any number of files. Code reaches another module's declarations through qualified names like geometry.area or by importing them with use. Visibility keywords decide who can see a declaration: its file, its module, its project, or every project that uses it."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "The mod line", "url": "modules.html#the-mod-line", "text": "Every source file starts with a mod line that names its module. Only comments and blank lines may come before it: A file names exactly one module, so a second mod line is an error, as Lexical structure shows. The name of a file and the folder it sits in don't matter to the compiler. Only the mod line decides which module a file belongs to; which files belong to the project is covered in Projects and building. By convention, a file in game/physics/ declares mod game.physics."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Modules across files", "url": "modules.html#modules-across-files", "text": "All files with the same mod line form one module. Their declarations are visible to each other as if they were written in one file, so main.ce here calls twice from helpers.ce without anything extra: Declaration order doesn't matter within a file, and it doesn't matter across files either."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Qualified names", "url": "modules.html#qualified-names", "text": "A declaration in another module is reached by writing the module's name, a ., and the declaration's name. This works for functions, types, constants, module variables and enum cases alike: Inside geometry, its own declarations need no qualifier. A module can also name itself, as in example.main, which helps when a local name hides one of its declarations. Without a qualifier or an import, a name from another module isn't found: A module isn't a value, so its name can only be used in front of a .:"}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Nested module names", "url": "modules.html#nested-module-names", "text": "A module name can have several parts separated by dots, as in mod game.physics. The parts make a tree of names: game.physics is reached as game.physics.fall, whether or not a module named game exists. game and game.physics are separate modules, each with its own declarations: Inside game, its nested module game.physics can be written physics. A module can't have a member with the same name as a nested module:"}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Imports", "url": "modules.html#imports", "text": "A use line after the mod line imports names from another module, so the file can use them without a qualifier. use names what it imports: use geometry.area imports one name. use geometry.[area, Point] imports several. use geometry.* imports everything in geometry. use game.physics imports a nested module, so the file can write physics.fall instead of game.physics.fall. An import applies to the file it's written in, not to the rest of the module. Imported names can still be written with their qualifier. Imports come right after the mod line, before any declaration: An import that names something that doesn't exist is an error, with a suggestion when a close spelling exists: Importing the same name twice is \"'area' is imported more than once\"."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Imports and the module's own names", "url": "modules.html#imports-and-the-modules-own-names", "text": "A file sees its module's declarations and its imports together, and neither one wins over the other: Functions from both form one set of overloads. When the module declares a function with the same name and parameters as an imported one, the module's own function hides the import; the imported one is still reachable through its qualifier. A type, constant or variable that comes in through * is hidden by a module declaration with the same name. Naming a type, constant or variable in an import while the module declares the same name is an error, since the import could never be used by its plain name. Both places are reported: The first error is on the import, with the note \"Also declared in 'example'\", and the second on the declaration, with \"Also imported from 'geometry'\". Imports never hide each other. When two imports bring in the same name, using it is ambiguous; for functions, only when they have the same parameters: The qualified names geometry.area and shapes.area still work."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "mod. paths", "url": "modules.html#mod-paths", "text": "A local variable, parameter or declaration can have the same name as a module and hide it. A path starting with mod. always starts from the top of the module tree, whatever is in scope:"}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Visibility", "url": "modules.html#visibility", "text": "Every top-level declaration has one of four visibility levels: | pvt | its own file | | mod | its own module, in every file | | none | its own project | | pub | every project that references this one | The keyword goes after the colon, before what the declaration is, as in helper: pvt fun or Point: pub rec. A declaration without one is visible in the whole project, which is all a program made of one project needs. pvt keeps a declaration to its file, even from other files of the same module: mod keeps a declaration to its module. A nested module is a different module, so game.physics can't see mod declarations of game, and the other way around: The same rules apply to imports: a hidden declaration that comes in through * still can't be used, and naming one in an import is an error. Types, functions, constants, module variables, traits and ext declarations all take visibility. Local variables don't: Members of records, enums and other types have their own visibility rules, covered in Methods and properties."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Visibility blocks", "url": "modules.html#visibility-blocks", "text": "A block gives every declaration in it the same visibility: pvt { ... }, mod { ... } or pub { ... }. It can hold any top-level declarations, ext blocks included, and pvt ext { ... } is short for an ext block inside a pvt block: A declaration inside a block can't name a visibility of its own, and blocks of different levels can't be nested:"}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Signatures can't expose hidden types", "url": "modules.html#signatures-cant-expose-hidden-types", "text": "A declaration can't mention a type that is less visible than the declaration itself, since code that can see the declaration couldn't name the type. This covers parameter and result types, field types, payloads and the types of module variables: keep is fine: it's pvt like Node, so it's never visible where Node isn't. The same holds for types built from a hidden type, like ptr[Node] or array[Node, 4], and for traits named in bounds and dyn types."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Libraries", "url": "modules.html#libraries", "text": "A project can reference a library project, as Projects and building shows, and use its declarations. Only the library's pub declarations cross over; everything else is private to the library's project. The library's modules are reached by name, exactly like the program's own: Using scale from the program would be \"'shapes.scale' is private to its project\". A pub declaration also can't expose a type that isn't pub, by the rule in the previous section."}, {"page": "Modules and visibility", "title": "Project imports", "url": "modules.html#project-imports", "text": "A project's .celp file can list imports that apply to every source file in the project, so each file doesn't need its own use lines. Imports takes the same forms as use, written as strings: Project imports combine with the module's own declarations by the same rules as use lines. An import listed there and in a file's use line is \"'area' is imported more than once\", and errors in the list point into the .celp file. A library's .celp can list a Prelude instead: imports that every project referencing the library gets automatically. A use line that repeats one of them gets the hint \"Redundant import\"."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Conditional compilation", "url": "conditional-compilation.html", "text": "Some code belongs only in some builds: a Windows call that doesn't exist on Linux, logging that a release build should leave out, a check that only debug builds pay for. Cella decides this at compile time with build flags. A when block keeps its contents only in builds whose flags match, a use when line keeps or drops a whole file, and a function marked when disappears, calls and all, from builds where its flag is off."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "when blocks", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#when-blocks", "text": "when is followed by a flag condition and a block. In a build where the condition holds, the block's contents are compiled as if the when weren't there. Otherwise they're left out: Built with --release, the same program prints the other line: The braces are required, even around a single statement. A when block doesn't start a scope, so a declaration inside it stays visible after it, as the next section shows."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "else when and else", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#else-when-and-else", "text": "else when and else chain further branches. The first branch whose condition holds is kept, and else is kept when none does: Each branch declares mode, and only one of them is ever compiled, so the printf after the chain sees exactly one mode."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Conditions", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#conditions", "text": "A condition is made of flag names, !, &&, || and parentheses, with the same meaning and precedence as in ordinary expressions. Nothing else can appear in it: no values, no comparisons, no calls. A name that isn't a flag is an error, with a suggestion when a flag has a close spelling:"}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Where when blocks go", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#where-when-blocks-go", "text": "when blocks can surround top-level declarations, members of records, enums, traits and other types, enum cases, the members of an ext block, statements, and the contents of visibility blocks: Step.finish is 2 in a debug build, because traced takes the value 1. In a release build traced doesn't exist and finish is 1. Inside a match, when groups arms, with else for the other builds. The match's else arm stays the last arm:"}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Code in other builds is only parsed", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#code-in-other-builds-is-only-parsed", "text": "A branch that isn't kept is parsed but not checked. It can call functions that don't exist in this build, use types that don't exist and name symbols from other platforms, so platform-specific code compiles on every platform: On Windows, only the first branch is checked and built; on Linux, only the second. Building with --target, as described in Projects and building, checks the branches for another platform. Syntax errors are reported in every branch, since every branch is parsed:"}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Built-in flags", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#built-in-flags", "text": "These flags are always defined: | windows, linux, macos | building for that operating system | | x64, x86, arm64, wasm64 | building for that processor | | debug | building without --release | | release | building with --release | | boundsChecks | the project's BoundsChecks setting is on | | overflowChecks | the project's OverflowChecks setting is on | boundsChecks and overflowChecks follow the settings of the project that declares the code, so code that checks its own bounds or overflow can do it exactly when the built-in checks are on. OverflowChecks is on by default in debug builds only: Checks and panics covers both settings."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Project flags", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#project-flags", "text": "A project declares its own flags in a [Flags] table in its .celp file, each with a default value: The table can also be written on one line, as Flags = { logging = true, profiling = false }. A flag name must be a valid identifier and can't be the name of a built-in flag. --flag sets a flag for one build, overriding its default. --flag profiling turns it on, and --flag profiling=false turns it off: Built with --flag profiling, the program above prints both lines: --flag only sets flags the project declares. A name that isn't declared is \"Flag 'missing' not found\", and setting a built-in flag such as debug is \"Cannot set built-in flag 'debug'\". Flags belong to the project that declares them: a library's code is built with the flags and defaults in the library's own .celp."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Leaving out whole files", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#leaving-out-whole-files", "text": "use when with a condition, written right after the mod line, keeps or leaves out the whole file. A file left out is only parsed, like a when branch that isn't kept: Each build sees exactly one mode. The use when line must directly follow the mod line, before any other import (\"'use when' must directly follow the module name\"), and a file has at most one:"}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "when functions", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#when-functions", "text": "A function can carry a condition of its own, written after its signature: trace: fun(value: i32) when logging. Unlike a when block, a when function is checked in every build. In builds where its condition holds, it's an ordinary function. In other builds it doesn't exist, and every call to it is removed along with its arguments, so they aren't evaluated: The call to measure and its argument count(2) are gone from this debug build, so count ran once. Because the arguments are still checked as if the call ran, a program means the same in every build. Here log would use text after it was moved, which is an error even in a release build where log is never called: To leave code out unchecked, put it in a when block instead. Methods and static functions can be when functions too, and generic functions and functions taking templates work the same way. Since calls to them may vanish, when functions are limited to what a removed call can't miss: They return nothing, and they can't be -> never. Their parameters without a mode must have types that copy, since a removed call would leave a moved value unmoved. They can't be used as values. main, constructors, destructors, getters, setters, operators and ext functions can't be when functions, a trait can't require one, and one can't implement a trait's member."}, {"page": "Conditional compilation", "title": "Conditional project settings", "url": "conditional-compilation.html#conditional-project-settings", "text": "A .celp file can hold tables named [When.\"condition\"], with a condition written as in when. Their settings apply only to builds where the condition holds and override the settings at the top of the file. A condition that is a single flag can drop the quotes, as in [When.debug]: Built with --release --flag fast, the same program prints unchecked. A conditional table can also add project references, which are added to the ones at the top level: OutputType and [Flags] can't be set in a conditional table. When two tables that both apply set the same setting, the build fails with an error naming both tables. Project settings lists which settings can be conditional."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Calling C", "url": "calling-c.html", "text": "Cella calls C functions directly, with no wrappers or generated bindings: you declare a C function's signature in Cella types, and calls to it follow the platform's C calling convention. The same works the other way, so C code can call Cella functions and read Cella globals. Everything that crosses between the two languages is marked with the ext keyword."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "ext blocks", "url": "calling-c.html#ext-blocks", "text": "An ext block declares functions that are defined in C. Each member is a name and a signature, with no body: These three come from the C runtime, which every program links. Functions from other libraries need the library named, as Linking describes. The declarations are ordinary module names: a module can have any number of ext blocks, and code in any of its files can call them. A block with a single member can drop the braces, as in ext puts: fun(text: cstr) -> i32. The signature must be complete. Every parameter needs a name and a type, and the result follows -> as for any function; a C function that returns void has no ->. A body is an error, since the function is defined elsewhere: ext functions can't be overloaded. Two declarations with one name are an error on each of them: A C function that never returns, such as exit or abort, is declared -> never, and calls to it end their path like ret:"}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Visibility", "url": "calling-c.html#visibility", "text": "Like other top-level declarations, the names in an ext block are visible to the whole project. A visibility word in front of the block applies to every member: pvt ext { ... } keeps them in one file, mod ext { ... } in the module, and pub ext { ... } lets projects that reference this one call them, as in lib.abs(-3). Visibility words can't appear inside the block: Modules and visibility covers the visibility levels."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "C types in Cella", "url": "calling-c.html#c-types-in-cella", "text": "Each C type has a Cella type with the same size and layout: | signed char, unsigned char | i8, u8 | | short, unsigned short | i16, u16 | | int, unsigned int | i32, u32 | | long long, unsigned long long | i64, u64 | | long, unsigned long | i32, u32 on Windows; i64, u64 on Linux | | size_t, ptrdiff_t | usize, isize | | float, double | f32, f64 | | bool | bool | | const char* to a zero-terminated string | cstr | | void* | ptr | | T* | ptr[T] | | struct | rec | | union | union | | enum | ext enum | | function pointer | ext fun[...] | | __int128 | i128 | ptr[T] points to a T and ptr points to anything; Pointers covers both. A C function with a variable argument list, such as printf, ends its parameters with ...; Variadic functions covers what can be passed through it."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Renaming with ext(\"name\")", "url": "calling-c.html#renaming-with-extname", "text": "A declaration takes the C name from its own name. ext(\"name\") before fun gives the C name separately, so the Cella name can follow Cella's style or avoid a clash: The C name must be a plain identifier:"}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Several signatures for one C function", "url": "calling-c.html#several-signatures-for-one-c-function", "text": "Since the C name and the Cella name are separate, one C function can be declared several times with different Cella signatures, in one module or in several. Each call uses the signature of the declaration it calls. This is how a C function that takes a void* can be given typed parameters for each use:"}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Strings", "url": "calling-c.html#strings", "text": "C strings are pointers to zero-terminated bytes, and Cella's type for them is cstr. A string literal converts to a cstr where one is expected, with the terminating zero added. A cstr can also be null, which C functions often return to mean \"not found\": A cstr has no length, so indexing one reads a byte without a bounds check, and it has no arithmetic. Cella's own string type, str, holds a pointer and a length and can't be passed to C; its data property gives the pointer to its bytes. Bool, char and strings covers both types."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Records and unions", "url": "calling-c.html#records-and-unions", "text": "A record crosses into C by value with the same layout as a C struct with the same fields in the same order, and a union crosses like a C union. C's div returns a struct of two ints: C functions that take a pointer to fill in can be declared with a ptr[T] parameter and called with @x, or declared with a mut parameter and called with mut x, which passes the address the same way. An imm parameter passes the address of a value C only reads. C's modf splits a number and stores the whole part through its second parameter: An array can't be passed by value, as in C. Pass its address with imm, mut or a pointer instead. Parameter modes covers imm and mut."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "ext enum", "url": "calling-c.html#ext-enum", "text": "A C enum isn't limited to its named values: C code can store any integer in one, and new versions of a library add values. A plain Cella enum can only hold its own cases, so it can't cross into C. An ext enum is the open version: any value of its tag type is valid, named or not. Converting an integer to an ext enum never fails, and a match on one must have an else arm even when it names every case: The tag is an i32, like a C enum, unless written ext enum(T) with another integer type. The default value is the one whose bits are all zero, whether or not a case has it. Constant values must fit in the tag type: Cases can carry payloads, as in a plain enum. An ext enum with payloads is laid out like the C struct { T tag; union { ... } payloads; }, which matches C APIs that pass events as a tag and a union: ext goes before enum, and an ext enum keeps its Cella name; ext(\"name\") is an error on it. There are no ext rec declarations: every record already has C's layout. Enums covers what ext enum shares with plain enums: case values, payloads and matching."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Callbacks with ext fun", "url": "calling-c.html#callbacks-with-ext-fun", "text": "ext fun[A, B -> R] is the type of a C function pointer: a function that takes an A and a B, returns an R, and uses the C calling convention. A function declared in an ext block is one, and so is a Cella function declared with ext fun and a body; any other Cella function named directly converts to one. C's qsort takes one to compare elements: A lambda works too, as long as it captures nothing, since a C function pointer has nowhere to keep captured values: ext fun values can be stored in variables, fields and arrays and called like functions. An ext fun converts to the matching Cella function type fun[...], but a fun value doesn't convert to an ext fun, since it may be a closure: ext fun declarations with bodies can't be members of a type, and an ext fun value can't be null. A C API whose callback is optional can be declared with a tagless enum over the function type, whose none case is the null pointer. C's signal takes and returns such a callback: Enums covers tagless enums, and Function values and closures covers function types in general."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "The T: ext constraint", "url": "calling-c.html#the-t-ext-constraint", "text": "A type parameter can appear in an ext signature, by value, behind a pointer or in an ext fun type, only when it has the ext constraint. T: ext means T can cross into C, and every type argument is checked for it: Without the constraint, the signature is rejected: A type that can't cross into C can't be the type argument: The error's note says why: \"'Token' can't be used in 'ext' signatures\". Types that cross into C can always be copied and never hold borrows, so a T: ext meets copy and noref bounds, and ext can't be combined with drop."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "What can't cross into C", "url": "calling-c.html#what-cant-cross-into-c", "text": "C knows nothing about Cella's ownership, borrows, traits or closures, so types that depend on them can't appear in ext signatures. That covers ext block members, ext fun definitions and ext fun types, and every field, element and payload of the types they use: types with destructors, since C would copy them bit by bit and never drop them plain enums, since C can hand back a value that isn't a case str, borrow types (imm[T], mut[T]) and records that hold borrows Cella function types (fun[...], ref fun[...] and owned closures), trait objects (dyn[...]) and templates (fstr[...]) arrays by value When the problem is inside a field, the error's note names it, as in \"'Holder' contains 'Buffer'\". A pointer to a type with a destructor can cross, since C only passes the address along: keep: fun(holder: ptr[Holder]) is fine."}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Exporting Cella to C", "url": "calling-c.html#exporting-cella-to-c", "text": "A Cella function declared ext fun with a body uses the C calling convention, so C can call it through a pointer, as qsort called descending above. To give it a symbol that C code can link against by name, make it pub: a pub ext fun gets a plain C symbol with its own name, or with the name in ext(\"name\"). Globals work the same way with ext val and ext var: The ext block here imports the program's own exports by their C names, the way C code would see them. An ext var or ext val in an ext block declares a C global and has no initializer; one defined at the top level has one. An ext val always lives in memory, so C can read it, and its value is never folded into the code that uses it. pub items of executables and shared libraries are exported, so a program can export functions for plugins it loads, and a shared library's pub ext funs can be called from C. Other pub items keep Cella's own symbol names, which include the module, so overloads and equal names in different modules stay apart. Two ext definitions with the same C name in one program, pub or not, are an error on each of them. An ext fun that isn't pub still uses the C calling convention, and with ext(\"name\") it still defines that symbol for C code linked into the same program, but nothing outside the program sees it. The rules for ext signatures apply to exported functions and globals too:"}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Linking", "url": "calling-c.html#linking", "text": "An ext block with a library name, ext(\"name\") { ... }, takes its functions from that library, and the build links it: On Windows, the name is an import or static library, name.lib, found in the project's folder or the linker's library paths. ext(\"user32\") links user32.lib, which makes the program load user32.dll. On Linux, the name m means libm.so or libm.a, found in the project's folder or the system's library paths. A name can be a path relative to the project's folder, such as ext(\"lib/bin/plugin\"). Without a name, functions come from the C runtime and the system libraries that every program links. Since library names differ between systems, a [when block](conditional-compilation.md#when-blocks) picks the right one: A missing library and a missing function are both reported at their declarations:"}, {"page": "Calling C", "title": "Linking the C runtime", "url": "calling-c.html#linking-the-c-runtime", "text": "On Windows, programs built with Visual Studio's tools link the C runtime either as a DLL or into the program itself. The SystemLinkPreference setting picks one: \"Dynamic\", the default, uses the DLL, and \"Static\" copies the runtime into the program, so it runs without the runtime installed: Every part of a program has to use the same C runtime, so a program that links it statically can't use a shared library project, and a library used by several programs that link it differently is an error. Project settings lists the setting with the others."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Checks and panics", "url": "panics.html", "text": "Some mistakes can't be found until a program runs: an index past the end of an array, a sum too large for its type, a division by zero. Cella checks for these as the program runs, and when a check fails, the program panics: it stops with a message that says what went wrong and where. A program can also panic on purpose with a panic expression. What a panic does is decided by a panic operator that a library or the program itself declares."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "What a panic looks like", "url": "panics.html#what-a-panic-looks-like", "text": "This program reads past the end of an array: The second call fails the bounds check, so the program stops before it prints done. The message names the file, line and column of the checked expression, here values[index]. The panic in this program comes from the standard library, which every project uses unless its StandardLibrary setting is \"Core\" or \"None\". Its panic first flushes output the program has buffered, such as printf's, then writes Panic at, the location and the message to standard error, and then ends the program as a crash, so the operating system's crash handling and an attached debugger see it."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Checks at compile time", "url": "panics.html#checks-at-compile-time", "text": "When the compiler can see that a check would fail, it reports an error instead of building a program that panics. This happens when the values involved are constants: The rest of this chapter is about the checks that run, which are the ones on values the compiler doesn't know."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "The built-in checks", "url": "panics.html#the-built-in-checks", "text": "| Indexing an array or a str | index out of bounds | with bounds checks | | +, -, * on integers, and their compound assignments | addition overflow, subtraction overflow, multiplication overflow | with overflow checks | | - on a signed integer | negation overflow | with overflow checks | | A numeric conversion that loses information | conversion overflow | with overflow checks | | Arithmetic on char past the last code point | char overflow | with overflow checks | | Reading a char that isn't a valid code point | invalid char | with overflow checks | | / and % by zero | division by zero | always | | The smallest signed integer divided by -1 | division overflow | always | | An integer converted to an enum with no case for it | 'Code' has no case with this value | always | | A tagless enum case given a value it doesn't cover | 'Parity.odd' can't hold this value | always | Bounds checks are on in every build. Overflow checks are on in debug builds and off in release builds. Both can be changed in the project's settings, as described below."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Bounds checks", "url": "panics.html#bounds-checks", "text": "Every index into an array or a str is checked against its length. Indexing a str reads a byte: Indexing through a pointer is never checked; see Pointers."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Overflow checks", "url": "panics.html#overflow-checks", "text": "With overflow checks on, integer arithmetic whose result doesn't fit in its type panics instead of wrapping around, and so does a conversion T(x) whose value doesn't fit in the new type. Numbers shows both, along with the wrapping operators +%, -% and *%, which never panic. Negating the smallest value of a signed type has no result that fits, so it panics too: char holds a Unicode code point, so arithmetic that leaves the range of code points is char overflow, and converting an integer that isn't a code point to char is conversion overflow: A char read from a union member whose bytes aren't a valid code point is invalid char."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Division checks", "url": "panics.html#division-checks", "text": "Integer division and remainder by zero panic with division by zero, and dividing the smallest value of a signed type by -1 panics with division overflow. Both checks run in every build. Float division by zero doesn't panic; it gives an infinity or a NaN. Numbers has an example."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Enum checks", "url": "panics.html#enum-checks", "text": "Converting an integer to an enum checks that some case has that value: A tagless enum (enum match) case lists the values it covers, and creating the case with any other value panics: An ext enum, which mirrors a C enum, accepts every value of its tag type, so converting to one never panics. Enums and Calling C cover both kinds."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Turning checks on and off", "url": "panics.html#turning-checks-on-and-off", "text": "Two project settings control the checks: | BoundsChecks | true | index checks | | OverflowChecks | true in debug builds, false in release builds | arithmetic, negation, conversion and char checks | Division and enum checks don't depend on either setting. With overflow checks off, integer arithmetic wraps around, integer conversions keep the low bits, float conversions saturate to the smallest or largest value of the type, and char values aren't checked. Built with cella build --release, this program wraps where a debug build would panic, but still stops at the division by zero: The setting overrides the build's default. This project turns overflow checks off in every build, debug builds included: OverflowChecks = true keeps the checks in release builds too. With BoundsChecks = false, an index past the end reads or writes whatever memory is there, as in C. Each setting applies to the code of the project that sets it, so a library keeps its own checks whatever the program that uses it chooses. Code can test the same settings with the boundsChecks and overflowChecks flags in a when block; see Conditional compilation."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Panicking on purpose", "url": "panics.html#panicking-on-purpose", "text": "panic followed by a message stops the program the same way a failed check does: panic is a keyword, not a function, so the message follows it without parentheses. The location in the message is where the panic is written. With the standard library's panic, the message is a str, and any str value works, not only a literal: The location here is inside fail, since that is where the panic is. The message is optional. A panic on its own stops the program with only the location:"}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "panic and never", "url": "panics.html#panic-and-never", "text": "A panic expression never produces a value, and its type is never. A path that reaches it ends there, like ret, so it can end a function that returns a value, or fill a match arm of any type: A function that always panics, like fail above, is declared -> never. Calls to it end their path the same way. Its body must not return or reach its end: never is only a return type, so a panic can't be stored in a variable. Functions covers -> never in general."}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Declaring a panic operator", "url": "panics.html#declaring-a-panic-operator", "text": "What a panic does is decided by panic operators, declared at the top level of a module in one of two forms: panic: op(message: str, location: str) -> never takes a message and the location. panic: op(location: str) -> never takes only the location. The location is a str such as main.ce:11:3 that gives the file's path in its project, the line and the column. Neither form may return. panic expr calls the form with a message, passing expr, and a bare panic calls the form without one. Built-in checks call the form with a message, passing their fixed messages, or the location-only form when it's the only one. A module can declare both, as the standard library does: they are overloads of one operator. A project that doesn't use the standard library can declare its own. This one prints the panic to standard output and exits with code 3: A panic operator can use when debug and when release to behave differently in each build, for example exiting with a code in debug builds and crashing in release builds. Each form must end with a str location and return never. The message's type is the operator's choice, but it must accept a str, since the built-in checks pass their messages as str: A panic operator belongs to a module, not to a type: A message type other than str lets panic take other values. Here the operator is generic over any type with a print method, so panic 42 works: Traits and impl blocks are covered in Traits. A message needs the form that takes one. Where only the location-only form is visible, panic with a message is an error:"}, {"page": "Checks and panics", "title": "Which panic a file uses", "url": "panics.html#which-panic-a-file-uses", "text": "panic is an ordinary name, found like any other: a file uses the panic its module declares or that it imports. Built-in checks behave exactly like a panic written at the same spot, so they use the panic visible in the file where the checked code is written. A library's code keeps using its own panic, whichever program uses the library. The standard library's panic is visible in every file of a project that uses the whole standard library. A project can make a library's panic visible in all its files with the [Imports setting](modules.md#project-imports), which imports names into every file: A panic can also be named through its module, as in fault.panic \"stopped\", to use one that isn't imported. Two wildcard imports that both bring in a panic make it ambiguous. The compiler reports it at each of the imports, since the file's checks would have no single panic to use: When no panic is visible at all, as in a project without the standard library that declares none, failed checks still stop the program, but with no message: the program crashes at the failed check. A bare panic crashes the same way, and so does one when the only visible panic takes a message. With no panic visible, there is nowhere for a message to go, so panic with a message is an error there too: \"'panic' doesn't accept a message\"."}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Atomics", "url": "atomics.html", "text": "An atomic operation reads or changes a value in memory as one indivisible step. When several threads use the same value, an atomic operation never sees half of another thread's write and never loses another thread's update. Cella has no atomic types. Instead, the atomic keyword goes in front of a pointer dereference, and turns that one access into an atomic load, store, update or compare-and-swap. Atomic types such as a counter or a flag are ordinary records built on top of it. The examples in this chapter run on one thread, so their output is the same every time. The operations behave the same way when other threads use the memory at the same time."}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Loads and stores", "url": "atomics.html#loads-and-stores", "text": "atomic *p reads the value p points to atomically, and atomic *p = v writes it: The pointer can be any ptr[T] value, such as @total for a global or a local, or a pointer held in a variable or a field. The keyword covers only the dereference after it, so atomic *p * 2 loads *p atomically and then multiplies the result as usual. An atomic store is a statement and has no value. Something other than a dereference after atomic is an error: The pointer has to be a typed ptr[T]. A borrow, a library pointer type with its own * operator and an untyped ptr can't be dereferenced atomically:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Updates", "url": "atomics.html#updates", "text": "A compound assignment after atomic reads the value, combines it and writes the result back in one step. Its value is the new value: The operators that can be atomic are +=, -=, &=, |=, ^= and the wrapping +%= and -%=. Others, such as *= and <<=, are errors: Which operators work depends on the type: | integers | +=, -=, &=, |=, ^=, +%=, -%= | | f32, f64 | +=, -= | | bool | &=, |=, ^= | | char | += and -= with an i32 | Atomic += and -= on integers and char are checked for overflow like the plain operators, and +%= and -%= wrap. Checks and panics covers when overflow checks run:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Compare and swap", "url": "atomics.html#compare-and-swap", "text": "atomic *p == expected = desired stores desired only if the value is expected at that moment. Its value is a bool that says whether it stored: The first swap finds 0 and stores 7. The second finds 7, so it leaves the value alone and gives false. The usual pattern for an update that has no atomic operator is a loop: load the value, compute the new one, and try to swap it in. If another thread changed the value in between, the swap fails and the loop tries again: Compare-and-swap compares bit patterns, not values, so for floats a NaN matches the same NaN, and -0.0 doesn't match 0.0: Without = desired, == is an ordinary comparison: atomic *p == 7 loads *p atomically and compares it with 7. Parentheses end the atomic expression, so (atomic *p) = 5 isn't a store but an assignment to the loaded value, which is an error:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Memory orderings", "url": "atomics.html#memory-orderings", "text": "On its own, an atomic operation only guarantees that the access itself is indivisible. A memory ordering also says how the operation orders the thread's other reads and writes, which is what makes a value written by one thread safe to read in another. The ordering goes between atomic and the dereference: | none | Sequentially consistent: every thread sees all such operations in one order. | | relaxed | Only the access itself is atomic; nothing else is ordered. | | acquire | Reads and writes after the operation can't move before it. | | release | Reads and writes before the operation can't move after it. | | acquire release | Both. | The typical pair is a release store that publishes data and an acquire load that sees it. A thread that reads ready as true with an acquire load also sees every write the other thread made before its release store: Loads take relaxed or acquire, stores take relaxed or release, and updates and compare-and-swap take any of them. With no ordering, every operation is sequentially consistent, the strongest and safest choice. Other combinations are errors: acquire release is always written in that order; release acquire is a syntax error."}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Fences", "url": "atomics.html#fences", "text": "atomic with an ordering and nothing after it is a fence: it orders the thread's memory accesses around it without accessing anything itself. atomic alone is a sequentially consistent fence: A fence is a statement. relaxed orders nothing, so it can't be a fence:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Which types can be atomic", "url": "atomics.html#which-types-can-be-atomic", "text": "These types can be accessed atomically: integers up to 64 bits, bool and char f32 and f64 typed and untyped pointers, other than pointers to trait objects cstr and ext fun values enums without payloads, unless their tag is i128 or u128 tagless enums (enum match) over one of the types above Records, str, i128, u128, enums with payloads, pointers to trait objects and fun values can't be: Loads, stores and compare-and-swap work on every type in the list. Pointers swap like any other value:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "Generic code and T: atomic", "url": "atomics.html#generic-code-and-t-atomic", "text": "In a generic function or type, atomic works on a T only when T has the atomic constraint. Without it, the body is rejected: With T: atomic, every type argument must be a type that can be accessed atomically: Each error has a note that says why, such as \"'Point' can't be accessed atomically\". T: atomic allows loads, stores and compare-and-swap. Updates depend on the type, so they're checked where the function is used with a concrete type. A call that would apply an update to a type that doesn't support it is an error at the call: A value that can be accessed atomically can always be copied, so T: atomic can't be combined with drop:"}, {"page": "Atomics", "title": "An atomic counter type", "url": "atomics.html#an-atomic-counter-type", "text": "Atomic types are records that hold the value and do every access to it through atomic. Methods take imm self, because many threads share one counter and none of them owns it exclusively; the write goes through the pointer @self.value, which the borrow doesn't restrict: hits is a val, and add still changes it. That is the point of an atomic type: the value inside changes, but the record itself is shared, never reassigned or moved. Atomic[bool] is fine as long as nothing calls its add, since updates are checked where they're used. Adding busy.add(true) to the program would be \"Cannot use '+=' atomically on 'bool'\"."}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Keywords", "url": "keywords.html", "text": "Cella has two kinds of keywords. Reserved keywords mean the same thing everywhere and can never be used as names. Contextual keywords have a meaning only in certain places, such as at the start of a declaration, and are ordinary names everywhere else."}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Reserved keywords", "url": "keywords.html#reserved-keywords", "text": "A reserved keyword can't name a variable, parameter, function, type, field or case:"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Values", "url": "keywords.html#values", "text": "| true, false | The two bool values. | Bool, char and strings | | null | The null pointer, for ptr, ptr[T] and cstr; T: null constrains a type parameter. | Pointers, Generics | | undef | A value left uninitialized on purpose: var buffer: array[u8, 64] = undef. | Variables | | sizeOf | The size of a type or value in bytes, as a usize. | Pointers | | alignOf | The alignment of a type or value in bytes, as a usize. | Pointers | | nameOf | The name of a declaration as a string: nameOf(Point). | Bool, char and strings | | self | The value a method was called on. | Methods |"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Declarations and bindings", "url": "keywords.html#declarations-and-bindings", "text": "| fun | Declares a function (area: fun(...)), starts a lambda (fun(x) => x * 2) and names function types (fun[i32 -> i32]). | Functions, Closures | | val | Declares a binding that can't be reassigned; also a read-only static field and a pattern binding. | Variables | | var | Declares a binding that can be reassigned; also a writable static field and a pattern binding. | Variables | | const | Declares a compile-time constant; also marks constant functions (const fun, const op), value parameters (const length: usize), constant implementations (impl const Hash) and constant bounds (T: const Hash). | Variables, Constant functions, Generics | | new | Names constructors (new: op(...)) and explicit conversions (new: op(self) -> T); T: new(...) constrains a type parameter. | Records, Conversions | | drop | Names destructors (drop: op), destroys a value early (drop x, drop *p); T: drop constrains a type parameter. | Ownership | | default | Restores zeroed construction (new: op() = default); T: default constrains a type parameter. | Records, Generics | | ref | Declares a record that holds borrows (ref rec) and names function types that may borrow (ref fun[...]). | Borrows, Closures |"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Control flow", "url": "keywords.html#control-flow", "text": "| if, else | Conditional statements; else if chains them. | Control flow | | while | Loops while a condition holds. | Control flow | | do | Runs a body once before checking its while condition. | Control flow | | loop | Loops until a break. | Control flow | | for | Loops over a range, an array or a type with loop operators; for: op declares a loop operator. | Control flow, Operators | | in | Separates a for loop's variable from what it walks; in: op declares a loop operator. | Control flow, Operators | | break | Leaves a loop, optionally a labeled one. | Control flow | | cont | Starts a loop's next iteration, optionally a labeled one. | Control flow | | ret | Returns from a function, with or without a value. | Functions | | match | Picks an arm by matching a value against patterns. | Pattern matching | | is | Tests a value against a pattern: shape is circle(_). | Pattern matching | | when | Compiles code only when a flag condition holds. | Conditional compilation | | panic | Stops the program with a message: panic \"bad index\"; panic: op declares a module's panic operator. | Checks and panics |"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Modes and memory", "url": "keywords.html#modes-and-memory", "text": "| imm | Borrows without writing: imm parameters and arguments, imm[T] borrow types, for imm x in xs. | Parameter modes, Borrows | | mut | Borrows for writing: mut parameters and arguments, mut[T] borrow types, for mut x in xs, match mut. | Parameter modes, Borrows | | own | Moves a value where a plain use wouldn't (own *p, own slot out of a mut parameter, own self.field), and declares types whose values only move (own rec, own enum) and closures that own their captures (own fun). | Ownership, Pointers | | dyn | Names trait object types: dyn[Shape]. | Trait objects | | atomic | Starts an atomic load, store, update or fence; T: atomic constrains a type parameter. | Atomics |"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Contextual keywords", "url": "keywords.html#contextual-keywords", "text": "These words are keywords only where the grammar expects them. Anywhere else they are ordinary names: | mod | Starts a file's mod line, makes a declaration visible to its module only, and starts a path from the root module (mod.geometry.area). | Modules | | use | Imports names from other modules; use when compiles a whole file only when a flag condition holds. | Modules, Conditional compilation | | pub | Exports a declaration from its project. | Modules | | pvt | Makes a declaration visible to its file, or a member visible to its type. | Modules, Methods | | ext | Declares C functions and globals (ext { }), C function types (ext fun[...]), C-compatible enums (ext enum), C names (ext(\"name\")); T: ext constrains a type parameter. | Calling C | | rec | Declares a record type. | Records | | enum | Declares an enum type. | Enums | | union | Declares a union type. | Unions | | trait | Declares a trait; K: trait makes a parameter that takes a trait. | Traits, Trait objects | | impl | Implements traits for a type (rec impl Shape, Circle: impl Shape) and adds members in impl blocks. | Traits | | op | Declares operators, constructors, destructors and conversions: +: op(...), new: op(...), drop: op. | Operators | | as | Declares an implicit conversion: as: op(self) -> T. | Conversions | | req | Marks fields that every construction must set, and lists the traits and constraints a trait requires (Shape: trait req Named). | Records, Traits | | prop | Declares a property with get and set accessors. | Methods | | get, set | Declare property accessors; pvt set and mod set limit who can write a field. | Methods | | copy | Constrains a type parameter to types that can be copied: T: copy. | Generics | | noref | Constrains a type parameter to types that hold no borrows: T: noref. | Generics |"}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Memory orderings", "url": "keywords.html#memory-orderings", "text": "After atomic, the words relaxed, acquire and release, and the pair acquire release, choose the memory ordering. Without one, the operation is sequentially consistent. Anywhere else these words are ordinary names: Atomics explains what each ordering guarantees."}, {"page": "Keywords", "title": "Built-in names", "url": "keywords.html#built-in-names", "text": "The names of the built-in types (i32, str, ptr, array and the rest, listed in Built-in types) and Self aren't keywords, but nothing can be declared with them:"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Operators and precedence", "url": "operator-reference.html", "text": "This page lists every operator and symbol in Cella, how tightly each one binds, which ones records and enums can declare for their own values, and where each is explained. Operators covers declaring them."}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Precedence", "url": "operator-reference.html#precedence", "text": "From tightest to loosest. Operators in one item have the same precedence. Postfix ., (), [] and ?, left to right. Prefix -, +, !, ~, *, @, own, mut, imm and atomic, right to left. *, /, % and *%, left to right. +, -, +% and -%, left to right. <<, >>, <<< and >>>, left to right. &, left to right. ^, left to right. |, left to right. <, <=, > and >=, chained. is, which doesn't chain. == and !=, chained. &&, left to right. ||, left to right. = and the compound assignments, right to left. Unlike C, the bitwise operators &, ^ and | bind tighter than comparisons, so a mask test needs no parentheses. Shifts bind looser than + and -, as in C: flags & mask == mask is (flags & mask) == mask, and 1 | 2 == 3 is (1 | 2) == 3."}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Chained comparisons", "url": "operator-reference.html#chained-comparisons", "text": "A run of <, <=, > and >= is one chained comparison: a < b <= c means a < b && b <= c, with b evaluated once. == and != chain the same way among themselves. A chain stops at the first comparison that's false: Numbers covers comparisons and their typing rules."}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Pattern tests", "url": "operator-reference.html#pattern-tests", "text": "is sits between the comparisons and the equality operators. Its left side can be a comparison or arithmetic, and the result can be compared with ==:"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Assignment", "url": "operator-reference.html#assignment", "text": "Assignment binds loosest and groups right to left, so a = b = 5 stores 5 in b and then in a:"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Operators and line breaks", "url": "operator-reference.html#operators-and-line-breaks", "text": "A new line ends an expression, so a line can't start with a binary operator, an assignment, is, &&, || or =>. Break the line after the operator instead: -, +, *, !, ~ and @ can start a line, but there they are prefix operators that begin a new statement: Lexical structure has the full rules for line breaks."}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Arithmetic", "url": "operator-reference.html#arithmetic", "text": "Numbers covers these and their typing rules. Records and enums can declare all of them. a + b: addition a - b: subtraction a * b: multiplication a / b: division a % b: remainder a +% b, a -% b, a *% b: wrapping addition, subtraction and multiplication -a: negation +a: the value unchanged"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Bitwise and shifts", "url": "operator-reference.html#bitwise-and-shifts", "text": "Numbers covers these. Records and enums can declare all of them. a & b: bitwise and; on bool, and without short-circuiting a | b: bitwise or; on bool, or without short-circuiting a ^ b: bitwise exclusive or; on bool, true when the two differ ~a: bitwise not a << b: shift left a >> b: shift right; signed types keep the sign bit a <<< b: rotate left a >>> b: rotate right"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Comparison and logic", "url": "operator-reference.html#comparison-and-logic", "text": "Numbers covers comparisons, Bool, char and strings the logical operators and Pattern matching is. Records and enums can declare the six comparisons, but not &&, ||, ! or is. a == b, a != b: equal, not equal a < b, a <= b, a > b, a >= b: less than, less than or equal, greater than, greater than or equal a && b: logical and; b runs only when a is true a || b: logical or; b runs only when a is false !a: logical not a is p: tests a against the pattern p"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Assignment", "url": "operator-reference.html#assignment-2", "text": "Variables covers assignment and Numbers the compound forms. Records and enums can declare the compound assignments, but not =. a = b: assignment a += b, a -= b, a *= b, a /= b, a %= b: arithmetic and assign a +%= b, a -%= b, a *%= b: wrapping arithmetic and assign a &= b, a |= b, a ^= b: bitwise operation and assign a <<= b, a >>= b, a <<<= b, a >>>= b: shift or rotate and assign"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Memory and access", "url": "operator-reference.html#memory-and-access", "text": "Records and enums can declare * as *: op(self) and indexing as []: op, as Operators shows, and ? as ?: op(self). The rest can't be declared. @a: the address of a (Pointers) *a: the value a pointer points to (Pointers) a.b: member access, also through pointers (Records) a[i]: indexing arrays, pointers and strings (Arrays) f(x): a call (Functions) a?: calls the type's ?: op member, which gives a value or returns early from the function that uses a? (Operators) own a: moves a where a plain use wouldn't, such as out of a mut parameter or a pointer (Ownership) mut a, imm a: borrows a with or without writing (Parameter modes) atomic ...: an atomic load, store, update or fence (Atomics)"}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Other symbols", "url": "operator-reference.html#other-symbols", "text": "| a..b | The integers from a up to but not including b, in for loops and patterns | Control flow, Pattern matching | | a..=b | The integers from a up to and including b, in for loops and patterns | Control flow, Pattern matching | | ... | Extra C arguments in an ext function, a typed variadic parameter, and spreading arguments | Variadic functions | | -> | The result type of a function or function type | Functions | | => | An expression body, a lambda body or a match arm | Functions, Pattern matching | | : | Separates a name from what it declares, and a binding from its type | Lexical structure | | , | Separates arguments, elements and members on one line | Lexical structure | | { } | A block or body, and a hole in an interpolated string | Control flow, String templates | | [ ] | Array literals, type arguments and indexing | Arrays, Generics | Ranges aren't values: .. and ..= appear only in for loops and patterns."}, {"page": "Operators and precedence", "title": "Conditions in when", "url": "operator-reference.html#conditions-in-when", "text": "The conditions of when, use when and [When.\"...\"] project tables combine flags with their own small set of operators: !, &&, || and parentheses, with ! binding tightest and || loosest. Conditional compilation covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Built-in types", "url": "builtin-types.html", "text": "These are the types the language provides. Everything else, such as records, enums, unions and traits, is declared in code. Sizes and alignments are in bytes, as sizeOf and alignOf report them on the 64-bit targets Cella builds for. The default is the value a variable of the type starts with when it's declared without one."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Summary", "url": "builtin-types.html#summary", "text": "| i8, u8 | 1 | 1 | 0 | | i16, u16 | 2 | 2 | 0 | | i32, u32 | 4 | 4 | 0 | | i64, u64 | 8 | 8 | 0 | | i128, u128 | 16 | 16 | 0 | | isize, usize | 8 | 8 | 0 | | f32 | 4 | 4 | 0.0 | | f64 | 8 | 8 | 0.0 | | bool | 1 | 1 | false | | char | 4 | 4 | the character with code 0 | | str | 16 | 8 | the empty string | | cstr | 8 | 8 | null | | ptr, ptr[T] | 8 | 8 | null | | ptr[dyn[K]] | 16 | 8 | null | | array[T, N] | N times the size of T | the alignment of T | every element at its default | | imm[T], mut[T] | 8 | 8 | none | | imm[dyn[K]], mut[dyn[K]] | 16 | 8 | none | | fun[...], ref fun[...] | 16 | 8 | none | | ext fun[...] | 8 | 8 | none | | fstr[K] | 24 | 8 | none | | dyn[K] | no fixed size | | none | | never | no size | | none | sizeOf and alignOf take a type or a value and give a usize:"}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Integers", "url": "builtin-types.html#integers", "text": "| i8 | -128 | 127 | | i16 | -32768 | 32767 | | i32 | -2147483648 | 2147483647 | | i64 | -2\u2076\u00b3 | 2\u2076\u00b3 - 1 | | i128 | -2\u00b9\u00b2\u2077 | 2\u00b9\u00b2\u2077 - 1 | | isize | -2\u2076\u00b3 | 2\u2076\u00b3 - 1 | | u8 | 0 | 255 | | u16 | 0 | 65535 | | u32 | 0 | 4294967295 | | u64 | 0 | 2\u2076\u2074 - 1 | | u128 | 0 | 2\u00b9\u00b2\u2078 - 1 | | usize | 0 | 2\u2076\u2074 - 1 | Signed integers use two's complement. isize and usize are as wide as a pointer; usize is the type of sizes and lengths. Numbers covers literals, arithmetic and overflow."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Floating-point numbers", "url": "builtin-types.html#floating-point-numbers", "text": "f32 and f64 are IEEE 754 single and double precision numbers. Numbers covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "bool and char", "url": "builtin-types.html#bool-and-char", "text": "bool is true or false, stored in one byte. char is a Unicode scalar value: a code point from 0 to 0x10FFFF, other than the surrogates 0xD800 to 0xDFFF. Bool, char and strings covers both."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "str and cstr", "url": "builtin-types.html#str-and-cstr", "text": "A str is a run of UTF-8 bytes it doesn't own, with two read-only properties: data, a ptr[u8] to the first byte, and byteLength, a usize. A cstr is a single pointer to zero-terminated bytes, the form C functions take. A string literal becomes either, depending on the type it's given. Bool, char and strings covers both."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Pointers", "url": "builtin-types.html#pointers", "text": "ptr[T] points to a T, and ptr points to memory of no particular type. Nothing is checked through a pointer. A pointer to a trait object, ptr[dyn[K]], also carries what it needs to call the trait's members, so it's twice as large. Pointers covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Arrays", "url": "builtin-types.html#arrays", "text": "array[T, N] holds N values of type T in a row, with no padding between them, so its size is N times the size of T. N is a usize known at compile time and can be 0. Arrays covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Borrow types", "url": "builtin-types.html#borrow-types", "text": "imm[T] and mut[T] borrow a T that lives somewhere else, without and with the right to write it. A borrow is the size of a pointer, or twice that when it borrows a trait object. Borrows covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Function types", "url": "builtin-types.html#function-types", "text": "fun[A, B -> R] holds a function that takes A and B and returns R. ref fun[...] can also hold closures, which borrow the variables they capture. ext fun[...] holds a C function pointer, the only kind C can call. Function values and closures covers them."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Trait objects", "url": "builtin-types.html#trait-objects", "text": "dyn[K] is a value of some type that implements the trait K, chosen at run time. Its size depends on that type, so it's used only behind a borrow or a pointer, never by value. Trait objects covers it."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Templates", "url": "builtin-types.html#templates", "text": "fstr[K] holds the pieces of an interpolated string, with every value it fills in implementing the trait K. String templates covers it."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "never", "url": "builtin-types.html#never", "text": "never is the result type of a function that doesn't return, such as one that always exits or loops forever. It's only allowed as a result type, and it has no size. Functions covers it."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Defaults", "url": "builtin-types.html#defaults", "text": "Writing a built-in type's name with empty parentheses gives its default, and a variable declared with var and no value starts at its default. For every type that has one, the default is all zero bytes: Borrows, function values, templates and trait objects have no default, since all zero bytes isn't a valid value of them. A variable of one of these types must be given a value before it's used: The types with a default meet the T: default constraint, and an array has one when its element type does. Generics covers constraints."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Copies and borrows", "url": "builtin-types.html#copies-and-borrows", "text": "Every built-in type other than dyn[K] and never can be copied, so they meet T: copy. Borrow types, str, ref fun[...], fstr[K], borrowed trait objects and arrays of any of them hold borrows, so they don't meet T: noref: cstr and pointers hold no borrows: nothing is checked through them. Borrows explains what holding a borrow means."}, {"page": "Built-in types", "title": "Built-in names", "url": "builtin-types.html#built-in-names", "text": "None of these type names can be used to declare anything else, as Keywords shows."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Project settings", "url": "project-settings.html", "text": "A project file, the .celp file in a project's folder, is written in TOML. This page lists every setting it can hold, the values each takes and what it does when it's left out. Projects and building introduces project files and the commands that read them."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Summary", "url": "project-settings.html#summary", "text": "| OutputType | \"Executable\", \"WindowedExecutable\", \"SharedLibrary\", \"StaticLibrary\" | required | no | | CellaVersion | a version such as \"0.1.0\" | none | no | | StandardLibrary | \"Full\", \"Core\", \"None\" | \"Full\" | no | | SystemLinkPreference | \"Dynamic\", \"Static\" | \"Dynamic\" | yes | | AssemblyName | a file name | the project file's name | yes | | BoundsChecks | true, false | true | yes | | OverflowChecks | true, false | true in debug builds, false in release builds | yes | | DebugInfo | true, false | true | yes | | Referenceable | true, false | false | yes | | Imports | a list of import paths | none | no | | Prelude | a list of import paths | none | no | | [Flags] | flag names set to true or false | none | no | | [Lints] | lint names set to \"Off\", \"Hint\", \"Warning\" or \"Error\" | each lint's own level | yes | | [Naming] | kinds of names set to patterns | the usual style | no | | [[ProjectReferences]] | Path and Alias | none | yes | | [When.\"...\"] | a condition and the settings it changes | none | | Setting names are case-sensitive, and any other name is an error such as \"Unknown setting 'Optimize'\". A setting given twice is an error too."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "OutputType", "url": "project-settings.html#outputtype", "text": "What the project builds. It's the one setting every project file needs; without it the build stops with \"Missing 'OutputType'\". | \"Executable\" | a program | name.exe | name | | \"WindowedExecutable\" | a program that opens no console window on Windows | name.exe | name | | \"SharedLibrary\" | a library loaded when a program starts | name.dll | libname.so | | \"StaticLibrary\" | a library linked into each program that uses it | name.lib | libname.a | name is the AssemblyName. Executables need a main function, as Functions explains."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "CellaVersion", "url": "project-settings.html#cellaversion", "text": "The version of Cella the project is written for. cella new writes the version that created the project. When you run cella, the launcher reads this setting and runs that version of the compiler, which must be installed; without it, the launcher runs the newest version installed. Projects and building covers the launcher. The compiler compares the setting with its own version. A project pinned to a newer version is an error (\"Pinned to Cella 99.0.0, newer than this compiler\"), and one pinned to an older version gets a warning. The value must be a full version with three numbers, such as \"0.1.0\" or \"0.2.0-dev\"."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "StandardLibrary", "url": "project-settings.html#standardlibrary", "text": "How much of the standard library the project references. | \"Full\" | all of the standard library | | \"Core\" | only the parts that don't depend on the operating system | | \"None\" | nothing | With \"None\", a project has no panic unless it declares one, as Checks and panics shows."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "SystemLinkPreference", "url": "project-settings.html#systemlinkpreference", "text": "How a program links the C runtime when it's built for Windows with Visual Studio's C++ build tools. \"Dynamic\" uses the runtime's DLLs, and \"Static\" copies the runtime into the program. Other builds link the C runtime the same way with either value. All projects in one program must agree, in every build. A program that links the C runtime statically can't use a shared library project, and a library used by two programs that link differently is an error. Calling C covers linking."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "AssemblyName", "url": "project-settings.html#assemblyname", "text": "The name of the output file, without its extension or lib prefix. It defaults to the project file's name, so game.celp builds game.exe. It must be a valid file name, so it can't be empty or contain a path, and the name of a shared library must be ASCII."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "BoundsChecks", "url": "project-settings.html#boundschecks", "text": "Whether indexing an array or a string checks that the index is in range, stopping the program with a panic when it isn't. On by default in every build. The built-in flag boundsChecks says whether the checks are on. Checks and panics covers the checks."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "OverflowChecks", "url": "project-settings.html#overflowchecks", "text": "Whether arithmetic checks for overflow, stopping the program with a panic when a result doesn't fit its type. On by default in debug builds and off in release builds. The built-in flag overflowChecks says whether the checks are on. Numbers and Checks and panics cover overflow."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "DebugInfo", "url": "project-settings.html#debuginfo", "text": "Whether the build includes debug information for debuggers. On by default."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Referenceable", "url": "project-settings.html#referenceable", "text": "Whether other projects can reference this executable and use its pub declarations, as they would a library's. A referenceable executable is built as a shared library that holds its code, and a small program that runs it. The setting applies only to executables, and a referenceable executable can't link the C runtime statically. Referencing an executable without it is an error that names the executable's project file: \"... is an executable without 'Referenceable = true'\"."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Imports and Prelude", "url": "project-settings.html#imports-and-prelude", "text": "Imports lists imports that apply to every source file in the project, written as they would be after use. Prelude lists imports that apply to every project that references this one. Modules and visibility covers both."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Flags", "url": "project-settings.html#flags", "text": "[Flags] declares the project's own flags, each set to true or false. Code tests them with when: A flag's name must be an identifier, and it can't be the name of a built-in flag. cella build --flag verbose=false changes a flag's value for one build, as Projects and building shows. Conditional compilation covers when. The built-in flags are always defined: | windows, linux, macos | building for that operating system | | x64, x86, arm64, wasm64 | building for that processor | | debug | building without --release | | release | building with --release | | boundsChecks | bounds checks are on | | overflowChecks | overflow checks are on |"}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Lints", "url": "project-settings.html#lints", "text": "Some of the compiler's hints and warnings come from lints, checks for code that works but is likely a mistake or could be simpler. [Lints] sets how each is reported: \"Off\" drops it, and \"Hint\", \"Warning\" and \"Error\" report it at that level. An error from a lint stops the build like any other: count is a var that never changes, which would get a hint, but NeverChanged is off. The lints and their usual levels: | Redundant | marks that change nothing, such as an imm where a value is borrowed anyway | hint | | RedundantImport | imports of names the file can already use | hint | | NeverChanged | var variables that are never changed | hint | | UnusedLocal | local variables that are never used | hint | | UnusedDeclaration | functions and globals that nothing uses | hint | | UnusedImport | imports that the file never uses | hint | | Naming | names that don't follow the [[Naming]](#naming) rules | hint | | UnreachableCode | code that can never run | warning | | FloatPattern | patterns that compare floats | warning | | SelfConversion | conversions that call themselves | warning | | EndlessRecursion | functions that call themselves on every path | warning | UnusedDeclaration leaves out pub declarations, main, operators and ext functions. Hints are shown only in builds given --hints (see Projects and building). [When.\"condition\".Lints] tables change single lints in some builds, so [When.release.Lints] with UnusedLocal = \"Warning\" would keep release builds going."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Naming", "url": "project-settings.html#naming", "text": "The Naming lint checks every name a project declares against a pattern for its kind. [Naming] replaces the pattern for some kinds: A pattern is a style in braces, {PascalCase}, {camelCase}, {snake_case}, {SCREAMING_CASE} or {lowercase}, with any letters, digits or _ before and after it, as in C{PascalCase}. | separates patterns a name may match instead. PascalCase and camelCase names don't allow two capital letters in a row, so HttpServer matches and HTTPServer doesn't. | Types | records, enums and unions | {PascalCase} | | Traits | traits | {PascalCase} | | TypeParameters | type parameters | {PascalCase} | | Functions | functions and methods | {camelCase} | | Fields | fields, including payload fields | {camelCase} | | Properties | properties | {camelCase} | | Locals | local variables | {camelCase} | | Parameters | parameters, including value parameters | {camelCase} | | Constants | constants | {camelCase} | | Globals | global variables | {camelCase} | | EnumCases | enum cases | {camelCase} | | Modules | each part of a module path | {camelCase} | Names declared in ext blocks and the members of trait implementations aren't checked, since their names come from C or from the trait. A name that doesn't match gets a hint such as \"'max_players' doesn't match {camelCase}\"."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "ProjectReferences", "url": "project-settings.html#projectreferences", "text": "Each [[ProjectReferences]] table makes the project use another one, usually a library. It has two settings: | Path | The other project's .celp file, relative to this project's folder. Required. | | Alias | A name to reach the other project's modules through. Optional. | Without an alias, the other project's modules are reached by their own names. With one, they are reached only through the alias, which keeps modules of the same name in different projects apart: An alias must be an identifier, can't be the name of a built-in type, and can't name two references. Projects and building covers using library projects."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "When tables", "url": "project-settings.html#when-tables", "text": "A [When.\"condition\"] table changes settings in the builds where its condition holds. The condition uses flags, built-in or the project's own, with !, &&, || and parentheses, as in a when block. A condition that is a single flag can leave out the quotes: [When.debug]. This is a debug build, so the first table applies and the second doesn't. A release build, or one with --flag fast, would also turn bounds checks off. A setting in a When table replaces the one at the top level. Only SystemLinkPreference, AssemblyName, BoundsChecks, OverflowChecks, DebugInfo, Referenceable, [Lints] and project references can go in one; a Lints table replaces only the lints it names. [[When.\"condition\".ProjectReferences]] tables add references in the builds where the condition holds, after the ones at the top level: Two tables whose conditions both hold can't set the same setting: that's an error such as \"'BoundsChecks' is set more than once, by [When.\"debug\"] and [When.\"windows || linux\"]\"."}, {"page": "Project settings", "title": "Problems in project files", "url": "project-settings.html#problems-in-project-files", "text": "The compiler checks the whole project file before building and reports every problem it finds. Besides unknown and repeated settings, it reports values of the wrong kind, each naming the setting and the table it's in: \"'Program' is not a valid 'OutputType'\" \"'BoundsChecks' must be true or false\" \"'logging' in [Flags] must be true or false\" \"Unknown lint 'Unused' in [Lints]\" \"'Loud' is not a valid 'Naming' in [Lints]\" \"'Pascal' is not a valid 'Types' in [Naming]\" \"'Imports' must be a list of strings\" \"Missing 'Path' in [[ProjectReferences]]\" \"Project 'lib/lib.celp' not found\" \"Cannot set 'OutputType' in [When.\"debug\"]\" \"Flag 'unknown' not found in [When.\"debug && unknown\"]\" A folder can hold only one project file; a second one is an error, \"Cannot share a directory with 'other.celp'\"."}];
